      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>Tramp's Regret.</head>
        <p>A Tramp's Regret.</p>
        <p>Belore Mr. F. A. Frost at the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday, Edward Kelly, a tramp, was charged on remand with wilfully setting fire to a barn contain- ing corn and hay stacks,the property of James J. Brook, Mersey View Farm' 9 Rixton on December 9th. Mr. Brook stated that at 2.30 p.m. on the date named he heard some shouting and in consequence went behind the barns. He found the corn stack in the brick barn was alright. His attention was called to the prisoner who was on the road about twenty yards away and subsequently went to the Police Station and brought P.C. Clarke upon the scene. As he was being driven to Newton Petty Sessional Court on the following day prisoner told witness "he was very sorry it was his place. He wished it had bOonged to a guardian of the poor. He had nothing against the prosecutor." Prisoner complained that he had been very badly treated at the Warrington Workhouse. The Chairman of the Board of Guardians, Mr. Brooks, lived in the vicinity of witness's farm. The damage done by the fire amounted to £6OO. _</p>
        <p>John Naylor, confectioner, 1, Stamford-street, War- Warrington, said ho was driving a horse and van along Manchester-road, Rixton, on thc date in question. On passing the farm at 2.55 he saw the prisoner coming from behind the barn. The fire had just been dis- covered. .</p>
        <p>P.C. Clarke stated that at 3 o'clock prisoner came to Rixton Police Station and said " There is a farm build- ing on lire down the road and a man with a van says I have done it. He accompanied prisoner to the farm where the witness Naylor stated that he saw prisoner come from behind the barn where the fire started. He then told prisoner he would be locked up, and he replied "I was behind the barn lighting my pipe but I didn't start the fire." On being taken to the police station prisoner admitted that he set fire to the barn by throwing a match on some straw. He added that he was starving and wanted to be locked up. He had a pipe and a box of matches in his possession. Prisoner complained bitterly of his treatment at the Warrington Workhouse, which, he said, led him to com- mit the offence. He only wished the fine had been at the farm of a guardian of the poor instead of at the prosecutor's. ' He was committed for trial at the' next Liverpool Assizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WHAT CHRISTMAS DISHES CAUSE.</head>
        <p>WHAT CHRISTMAS DISHES CAUSE.</p>
        <p>At Christmas time more people suffer from indiscre- tions of diet than at any other period. The rich dishes then taken throw more work upon the digestive system than it can do, and the result is indigestion. flatulence, bad taste in the mouth, headache, and sometimes con- stipation and piles. If, after a meal of enjoyable but heavy Christmas dishes, one of Chas. Forde's bile beans be taken, followed by one or two beansupon retiring for the night, all these unpleasant consequences will be avoided. Just as the Post Office staff needs to be increased to deal with Christmas correspondence so the digestive apparatus needs help in dealing with its extra Christmas work. Bile Beans give just the necessary help. They arenot sugar-coated, and may be carried loose in the pocket, thus being always accessible immediately after a meal. Sold by all chemists at one and three half- pence or two and nine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>IS SMOKING INJURIOUS?</head>
        <p>IS SMOKING INJURIOUS?</p>
        <p>A Philanthropic Offer by a World Famed Tobacco Planter.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuda the famous tobacco planter, cigar and cigarette maker, has made an unparalleled offer to the Editor of the "Daily Telegraph" and other leading journals throughout the country. During the next month he has offered to set aside one-fifth of the entire returns emanating from his ex- tensive advertising throughout the country for the benefit of Hospitals and Charities. Every smoker has thus an opportunity of obtaining high class cigars, smoking mixture or cigarettes at an exceedingly low price—at the same time assisting the most deserving of charities. The Hon. Sidney Holland, Chairman of the London Hospital, and well-known in charitable circles, has undertaken to act as treasurer to the fund. _ _ _ _ - -</p>
        <p>The great a‘p!vantage that .I\lr. Samuda claims for his "Avoca" specialities is the entire absence of arsenic, as all the tobaccos of his products are grown on his own vast plantations on virgin soil only. Mr. Samuda's Cigar Manufactory is a proof of the quality of the goods he supplies, for whereas in 1870 he employed 4 cigar makers only, he now employs from 1,000 to 1,500 experts. and his guaranteed output last year exceeded 25,000,000 cigars. This huge business has been built up without the assistance of a single advertisement or traveller. 'the " Avoca "cigars are made by hand. Mr. Samuda never had a machine, nor does he employ female labour, only employing men who have passed their six years apprenticeship. "Avoca" Cigars are guaranteed free from anyArace of arsenic. It has always been the desire of Mr. Samuda to produce a Cigar free from that poisenous and volatile chemical, arsenic. After careful experiments extend- ing over a period of thirty years Mr. Samuda has solved the problem. Mr, Samuda will be pleased to send any reader of this paper full particulars of the scheme, together with a very interesting booklet, "Is Smoking Injurious ?" a work no smoker who has any regard for his health cau. afford to miss reading. Our readers should write clearly and address their letter to—Mr. J. Samuda, 84, Leadenhall-street London, E.C.</p>
        <p>DRUNK IN CHARGE OF CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>On Mon-cin'i-Ei-t-itunc orn, Anniaßav, a married woman of Weston, was charged with being drunk in charge of two children, under the age of seven years. Police- .Fonstable Ellis stated that at 8 p.m. on Saturday he was in Greenway road, when he heard the screams of chil- dren and found prisoner very drunk, with an upturned bassinette beside her. The bassinette contained two children aged three years and one year.—lnspector Egerton corroborated, and Superintendent Ennion stated that the prisoner's conduct in the cells was scandalous.—The prisoner denied that she was drunk but said her head went !dizzy.—The b.nch said they would impose the lenient penalty of 20s and costs or fourteen days.—The prisoner : I will go for fourteen days.</p>
        <p>FAILURES IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to "Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of failures recorded in England and Wales during the week POISONED BY FALSE TEETH. 1 p ending' Friday, Dec. 18th, was 170 viz. :—New Bank- The evidence given at inquest on Monday upon ' ruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, - an the wife of a carpenter named Passingham, of Town- s 93; deeds of arrangement registered, 77. The res- pective numbers in the corresponding week of last year mead-road, Fulham, showed that the cause of death was septic poisoning, duo to her having swallowed her false were : Bankruptcies, 77 ; deeds of arrangement, 82— teeth. These lodced in the lower part of the neck, and total 159; being a net increase in bankruptcies of 30, were removed at St. George's Hospital, but deceased and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in 1903, to • date., of 360. did not recover. ,</p>
