      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>:No season of the year has more old world notions associated with it than Chrittmas, and among the quaint old traditions which still survive in Devon and Somer- set, is the burning of the ashen faggot on Christmas Eve. This consists of green ashwood, fastened into a bundle with withy bands. It is placed on the fire with much ceremony, the famiiy and guests seated round, and as each withy band gives way, glasses are raised and emptied to "A Merry Christmas." The origin of this custom is supposed to have some connection with the kelief that a fire of ashwood warmed the stable at Bethlehem.</p>
        <p>The date of Christmas.—The actual day and Month of the Nativity are unknown, but it is generally agreed that it did not take place in December. One reason for this belief is,that so far as is knowe this has alwaysbeen a rainy season in Palistine, so that the shepherds would not be watching their flocks by night as is recorded by St. Luke. The early Christians celebrated the birth of Christ in March, at the time of the Jewish Passover,but the festival has also been held in September, and the Abyssinians, who claim to have heenamongst the earliest Christians, still make June 21st Christmas day. The December date was fixed by Pope Julius in the fourth century, but by adhering to the old style calender, the Greek church still celebrates Christmas on January 7th. The Cumberland Family.—Both the Duke and Duchess of Cumberland, who have just celebrated their silver wedding,arenearly related to our King and Queen. The Duke is second cousin to King Edward,. whilst the Duchess as is well-known, is a sister of Queen Alexandra. The deposing of the r«yal family of Hanover wasmuch resented by Queen Victoria, but it is understood that the Duke himself no longer takes up an irreconciliable attitude with regard to that event. He has two sons and three daughters, and the second daughter—named after the Queen—Princess Alexandra, has just become engaged to the Grand Duke of Mecklenburg, Schwerin. Another Ducal Wedding.—The marriage arranged between the Duke of Norfolk mid Miss Gwendolin Maxwell will take place before Lent. This interesting matrimonial announcement has caused some sensation, especially in Roman Catholic circles, as the Duke has been so long a widower that it was thought very doubt- ful if he would marry again. His first wife, Lady Flora, the daughter of Lord Donington, died in 1887 and his only son—a hopeless invalid—died last year. The Duke is fifty-six, just thirty years older than Miss Maxwell, who is the eldestelaughter of Lord Herries,and is second cousin to his Grace. In consequence of this relation- ship, a special clispensatien will be requisite before the marriage can take place. Miss Maxwell is heir to the ancient Scotch barony of Herries, which may possibly add one more to the many titles that have been swallowed up-by the Dukedom of Norfolk. The present Duke is three times an Earl hnd four times a baron. besides being hereditary Earl Marshal, but he is withal one of the most kindly, retiring, and simple-minded of men. Women's altered Status.—lt suggests the dark aces when we are told that in former times 4 husband had absolute control over his wife's belongings, and men had a recognised .right to beat her "in moderation." Yet, as Mr.Lush,K.C., reminded his audience in a recent lecture upon the law relating to women, and their pro- perty,this was the state of things up to two generations ago. To be wedded to a spendthrift, or a brute, meant irrevocable ruin, but at the same time there is little doubt that these risks made girls more careful as to the men they married: We would not return to such an unenlightened era, but it is quite possible there are as many, if not more, unhappy marriages now-a-days than in former times, owing to the fact that the bonds of matrimony are less seriously regarded under thealtered conditions. Women and the Weather.—The weather that is both cold and wet,is a trying time to wdmen,especially those with a fair complexion. The dark woman does not as a rule look so pinched and miserableas the blonde, but none of us look our best in the winter time. If only we had a regular season of clear bright weather, we should know how to dress, but we get such variations of cold, wet, or muddy weather that unless we have the most sumptuous wardrobe,and a maid to lookafter it,we are bound to go through the winteralternatelv shivering or prespiring. It is the exception to be really com- fortable,as it is only the clear bright, cold,and exhilara- ting days that are enjoyable, and these are fewand far between during our long winter. Shapes in Headgear.—The large picture hat is just now conspicuous by its absence, and wisely so, for no woman can present a dignified appearance, clinging on to this particular form of headgear. The discomfort to the wearer also, is great, for pin it as one will, no method has yet been found to prevent a large hat from waving sympathetically with every wind that blows. The marquise is the shape most in favour for promenade wear on the Riviera. and, aceording to authorities on the subject this will be the leading shape here this spring. Home Millinery.—What the home worker needs to study carefully when building a hat or toque. is the side view. The front and back are always well looked after, but the side view is often neglected, the effect in con- sequence leaving much-to be desired. Sometimes there occurs a curious hiatus,a blank that' needs to be filled up with a twist of velvet or taffetes.- or n cluster of flowers. A good milliner first studies her customer, then builds the hat or toque upon her head, studying her creation" from all points. Fashionable Ornaments.—Quite a fa veurite form of ornament for up-to-date young mothers is a minnture of "Baby" surrounded with pearls, diamonds, or other precious stones, and worn as a brooch or pendent. The mode strikes one as somewhat wanting in taste. Photographs, or miniatures, when worn, should be en- closed. or tucked in out of sight, otherwise like name brooches, they appear to give too 'Mich publicity to one's privateaffairs. However pretty and fascinating a picture of one near and dear may he, the well-bred Woman does not care about presenting it to the gaze of any Tom, Dick, or Harry, who may choose to stare at it, AdulteratedSilks.—The astonishing degree to which silk may be adulterated was illustrated in a lecture Upon this subject. which Mr. Frank Warner recently gave before the Society of Arts. He produced a skein of spun silk such as is used for fancy embroidery work. which he said was weighted un to 160 ounces to the le ounces of the genuine material. That is tome., only one tenth of its weight was silk, the rest being made tin of salts of tin, iron, and tannin. The dancer of wetting such silk with the tongue, in order t o more easily thread the ravelled end through the needle, is therefore sufficiently obviorts,—cmite apart from any risk of noisoning from the dyes used in its manufacture. Woven silk admits of less adulteration but it , can he enrripd farenough, and some lengths of brocade which Mr. Warner produced, had more than half their weight made up of metal. A simple test of metallic adultera- tion is however afforded by fire. A pure silk material will burn up almost as meekly as paper, but that -heavily weighted with metal will only char slowlv. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Christmastide is one of the busiest seasons of the year and one's time and hands are both fully occupied with the selection and despatch of pretty trifles, useful presents, or seasonable cards expressive of "peace and goodwill." It is really a delightful task to spend an after- noon or two shopping for the thousand and oneicharming items of dress or ithe toilette are this year even more fascinating than before, and in many instances wonderfully inexpen- sive. There are dainty lace or 'embroidered lawn collars and pretty silk stocks with fringed or lace appliqued ends, while the dainty handkerchiefs are gifts which would be welcomed by any one. More costly are the silk or chiffon blouses made for the most part with the yoke of transparent lace from which the material is accordion pleated. A blouse is a most sensible present, for one gets through</p>
        <p>so many in a winter that whether it is destinea for morning, afternoon, or evening wear it will always be gladly welcomed, provided the donor has exercised their commonsense and has not sent a dressy confection of chiffon and silk to the girl who goes out little and dresses plainly. People so often rashly make a gift without considering the circumstances and requirements of the friend, but send the article merely because it is pretty and possibly expensive.</p>
        <p>Vurs can always be safely sent by those who can afford to select something nice, and .53, long fur stole or scarf such as is shown in our sketch would be hailed with joy by anyone. A nice fur is somewhat costly, and the cheaper imitations must be selected with care as some of them are altogether impossible, while others are both pretty and useful. Black astrachan is fashionable and always looks well and does not cost so loch when cut fiat and</p>
        <p>lined with satin. I should not advise the girl who has to practise care in the expenditure of her limited dress allowance to be so unwise as to purchase any of the fur wraps which are of a very pronounced shape as some of the scarfs which are worn draped round the shoulders and one or two other ultra fashionable styles having now extended from the expensive to the cheap furs will soon be out of date, as is the fate of aU universally adopted modes. I should rather ,counsel the selection of a straight cut tie of either single or double fur finished off with a fringe of furry tails. A tie of this style will be in fashion for several seasons and will not date itself. .</p>
        <p>The charming evening toilette depicted is simply made yet very chic. The lace flounce Which hangs from the decolletage is deeper in front than at the sides or back in the manner of some of the smartest b0%,,:0s of last summer. The lace on the skirt is 4raife.d fully round the waist over the silk founclr,tion skirt, while a long flounce of lace forms the lower part of the sleeve. Soft puffings of chiffon soften the lines of the bodice, while the waist is finished by a deep swathed belt vhich must either match exactly the colour of the lace or strike a very strong note of contrast. One requires so many pretty frocks at this festive season that this sketch may be found useful by some of my readers who wish to freshen up a slightly</p>
        <p>, ... . ~....,. ,c e e; ' I r si , it \ „ 4., , 4! A FURS TOLE.</p>
