      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>RE HAT</head>
        <p>RE HAT</p>
        <p>soileu silk or satin gown with the aid of a lace overdress and yet cannot hit on a happy idea as to how to arrange it to 'the best • advan- tage. Lace is very popular this winter and overdresses with he skirt shaped and ready for mounting over a foundation together with material for the bodice and sleeves are being sold in all colours from white to black at a wide range of prices and in a great Variety of makes. The prettiest lace gowns are, of course, mounted on chiffon, but this adds greatly to the expense as a great quantity of this rather fragile fabric is required. The picture hat given in my last sketch is of soft hairy-surfaced felt trimmed with long curling ostrich feathers, while a twist of good lace is arranged round the crown and falls slightly on to the hair at the back. The smartest dressed women are wearing hats of this style in white or very pale cream, but these are costly and at the same time ex- travagant purchases. for those who live in a city where smuts and fogs soon work havoc among all sorts of pale coloured millinery. In black,, however, a hat like this is very becom- ing and looks nice with any dress. Some picture hats are of rich tones of crimson or wine colour trimmed with feathers to match, but one is apt to weary soon of anything that is very pronounced in colour, especially if it has to be worn rather constantly. Tall crowns are, it is true, a feature of some of the hats, but at present most people seem to con- tent ; themselves with one of medium height as the eye has not yet had time to become recon- ciled to any pronounced change from the plateau styles of -last summer.</p>
