      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>GERMAN LOCOMOTIVES FOR CANADA.</head>
        <p>GERMAN LOCOMOTIVES FOR CANADA.</p>
        <p>During the last two or three years the capacities of the several engine works within the German Empire have been more than equal to the German demand, and the con- sequence has been a 'reaching out" for foreign trade. This has resulted in the Canadian Pacific Railway Company dividing a contract for a number of powerful com- pound locomotives between British and German builders, the respective firms being the North British Locomotive Company, of Glasgow, and the Saxon Engine Works, of Chemnitz. This is the first instance in which locomotives have ever been built in Germany for a North American railway. Tne German locomotives, a number of which have just recently been delivered at Montreal, have six coupled wheels, each having a diameter of sft. 3in. The cylinders are 22in. by 26in., and the steam pressure 210 pounds to the square inch. The tender is of the turtle- back type, with a capacity of 5,000 gallons of water and no less than ten tons of coal. An electric-lighting dynamo, placed just in front of the cab and driven by a steam turbine engine, -supplies current for an in- stallation of incandescent lamps, placed along the running boards, over the motion, and in the cab, in addition to a powerful Edwards headlight. These engines together with the British-built engines, are now being submitted to exhaustive trials, the results of which are awaited with great interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DUKE OF NORFOLK'S ENGAGEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE DUKE OF NORFOLK'S ENGAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>The announcement last week that the Duke of Norfolk is engaged to be married to Miss Gwendolen Maxwelr; eldest daughter of Lord Herries, has created very general surprise, for though his grace has no heir upon whose shoulders his titles and vast</p>
        <p>DUKE OF NORFOLK.</p>
        <p>MISS GWENDOT-EN MAXWELL.</p>
        <p>estates can fall at his death, it was nevei anticipated that he would marry again. The Duke of Norfolk is Hereditary Earl Marshal and Chief Butler of England and piremier Duke and Earl. He is 56 year of age. His wife, Flora, daughter of Lord Donington, died in 1887. His Grace's only son died last year, after a lifelong incarceration in a private asylum. The Duke was Postmaster-General from 1895 to 1900. He then resigned his post to serve in South Africa. Miss Maxwell is a first cousin once removed of her future husband. She is heir to the ScottiSh barony of Herries, which will now become merged in the Duke- dom of Norfolk. As the Duke and his future bride are related, even distantly, a special dispensation for the 4-triage, had to be ob. tamed from the Pope.</p>
