      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>BE GREAT FINGALL VERDICT.</head>
        <p>BE GREAT FINGALL VERDICT.</p>
        <p>Very general satisfaction is expressed in the ,:ity at the exemplary punishment of ten years' penal servitude imposed upon Anthony Stanley Rowe, the late secretary to the Great Fingall Consolidated, a wealthy gold mine belonging to Messrs. Bewick, ` Moreing, and Company. Rowe did a deal of unprofitable speculating on the Stock Ex- change, and early last year was in very shallow Water indeed. He ex- trieated himself for the time being by bor- rowing f 5,000 on shares in the Great 'Fingall Companyvalued at 1,30,000. The money - lender, however, soon discovered that the security was bogus, the whole papers in connection witti the transaction being forgeries. Rowe iliturned the 5,000 in full, but in order to do ,to made out another forged transfer, with which. he duped some one else into lending him the required amount. Shortly afterwards he absconded to Canada, whence he was extradited. . Rowe, said the prosecuting covsel, had incurred defalcations to the enormous amount of £loo,oOo, which Messrs. Bewick and Moreing were called upon in honour, hut not in law, to make good. The total loss thus sustained amounted to 00,000. They had just learned that, 20 years ago, Rowe was convicted of embezztement, and sentenced to nine months' imprisonment. With regard to the prisoner's antecedents, counsel was instructed that he was formerly in the employ of Messrs. Streeter, jewellers, whom he robbed. and absconded. The judge said it was not by reason of anything the prisoner had done that no one had suffered from the frauds which he had committed.</p>
        <p>3 A , ! ,C 1 Trur PRISONER ROWE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>REVOLUTION IN BOOT REPAIRING.</head>
        <p>A REVOLUTION IN BOOT REPAIRING.</p>
        <p>A new invention which promises to be of great value is the method by which everyone will be able to re-sole and heel his own oots. What it might have meant in the last war or in the Crimea, is incalculable</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>;OLE IS FITTED</head>
        <p>;OLE IS FITTED</p>
        <p>for one of the biggest breakdowns during both were the boots for the Army. Under this new system " Tommy " can always be equipped with new soles and heels, which can be fixed in a minute, without much extra room being required, as they do not bulk at all. In civilian, life, too it will be very pleasant, when ;,you can find a sole worn through or a boot down at the heel, to adjust a new one before you • put it on. On the edge of the permanent sole there runs a ridge, • which permits an overlapping slide on the new sole to, be pushed up, till the hole in the heel piece is over the permanent base of the heel. Then the new heel is put on to fix the sole firmly in its place, awl the .whole is fastened immovably by a plug in the heel from the inside of the boot. The whole process only takes a few seconds; and an additional advantage is the fact that the same pair of shoes or boots may have a light or a thick mit- of soles. according to the weather.</p>
        <p>sHE LAST OF THE MOAT FARM CASE.</p>
        <p>, The final 'sad scene' in connection with the murder of Miss Camille Holland by the man Dougat who recently expiated his crime on the Chelmsford scaffold, is the erection of a valuable monument over the lady's grave in Saffron Walden cemetry. The memorial takes the • form of a cross with a solid triple base. The whole is carried out •in the finest White marble, and is a very massive structure, over six feet high.. It has been erected at considerable expense by Mi,is Holland's neph- ews. On the cross is the gure, o f an an- gel receivinp. woman upon'her knees, copied from one of</p>
        <p>Miss Holland's</p>
        <p>THE MONUMENT OVER MISS own drawings HOLLAND'S GRAVE. • The inscription</p>
        <p>on the base is as follows: "In sympathetic memory of. Camille Cecile Holland, of Maida Vale, Lon- don, who died at Clavering under distressing. circumstances on May 19th, 1899, aged fifty- six- years. Nunc demum requiescat in pace." The white marble kerb, placed round the grave, bears the text.: "And _death shall be no more; neither shall there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain any more; the former things are passed away (Rev. xxi., 4)." Afready numbers of curious tourists in the district have made Saffron Walden in their way to look upon this speaking memento of one of the most atrocious and widely commented on crimes perpetrated in recent years, and it is anticipated that when the holiday season sets in the Saffron Walden cemetry will be so be- seiged with sightseers that consideration is being given to the advisability of having the grave efficiently 'protected, probably with railings; certainly, some restriction 'will have to be placed upon that souvenir-seeking Sec- tion of the public who consider a mild form of vandalism quite justifiable.</p>
        <p>. • ote 01- k 74' HOW THE NEW S,</p>
