      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>reason</head>
        <p>reason</p>
        <p>grateful t 9 him</p>
        <p>Within the last "WALKER, OF THE BANK." ten years he has over</p>
        <p>collected</p>
        <p>six hundred letters from subscribers to enable cripples to obtain surgical appliances free oi charge; and with many City men t is a practise to place their letters at his disposal. "However poor a man may be he can always help others," has been Walker's motto in life: and one of 'his protégés was an old womar of 83, who sold matches in the street. Fox four yeaas walker shared his custom with her: and then when she got too old and feeble to bear the exposure, he started a subscription list amongst his customers to help her to end her days in comparative comfort. Training himself at the Borough Polytechnic and at the School Boards evening classes, Walker holds two certificates from the Chamber of Commerce and two from the Society of Arts for proficiency,in book-keeping, and another from the former body for the "Methods and Machinery of _ Business." His daughter he is training to be a school-mistress. Walker is a worthy example of how disabilities can be fought and conquered. His ambition is, as soon as he is proficient, to get a position in a business office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FRENCH MOTOR MANUFACTURE PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>FRENCH MOTOR MANUFACTURE PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>The Paris Motor Car " Show is surely con vine* the world that to France belongs the honour of the most up-to-date motor con- struction. The new Boll&amp; automobile is said to be as perfect as a petroleum-driven car can be. One correspondent declares it is simply marvellous. "It does not," he says, "make the slightest sound. There is nothing, in fact, to indicate that it is petroleum-driven. One could imagine it to be a highly-perfected elec- tric motor car." In appearance it resembles the 40 horse-power Mercedes machine. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>SIX,</head>
        <p>THE SIX,</p>
        <p>.Wll EE:</p>
        <p>cooling-fan is placed just behind the honey- comb radiator, and is driven by a wire belt. The pulley of the fan is, however, held in position by a spring, which ensures it being always tight, thus overcoming the tendency of the belt to slip. The circulation of the water is maintained by a slow-working pump, which forces a good volume round the cylinders. The vehicle is fitted with two distinct igni- tions, magneto and coil, and the spark of either of these ignitions is controlled from the steering wheel as in the Mercedes vehicle. The.carburator also contains some novel feat- ures which render it wonderfully quiet at all speeds. The motor itself is fiXed to the chassis in knovel way. One end of the motor is bolted to the side frames in the usual man- ner, while the other end comes to a point in the centre of the frame, and ends in a rod which brings a bearing on the cross member of the frame. Should a front wheel strike an obstacle and distort the frame, the motor, which is supported at three points only, does not follow the distortion of the frame, but remains steady. The 13ollee firm is probably the oldest automobile firm in France. Leon. Bollee was the inventor of the first light carriage, the Voiturette. It had its moment of popularity, and then disappeared. That was over ten years ago. After that nothing was heard of M. Bollee until six months ago, when it was announced that he had invented this automobile, which may be looked upon as the model French machine. Another novelty is the Flexbi automobile, with six wheels, constructed by M. Janvier. The front forms a sort of bogie, and it is claimed that by reason of the solidity obtained by this four- wheeled forward section complete stability is stainoci. •</p>
        <p>ji., i 1,- ''',..L -.',',,,,,,-- ...4.' 041,!...L1D • _ 15Ve -.• s I irwir `.4..• ....14...., ~1 (.... .....,,icA. ,---1,..- ---,..., =,-. • ,„, t ~, , -,,,,,,i, , . ,-_ 'LED FLEXBT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LU \/iN</head>
        <p>LU \/iN</p>
        <p>Jgg were fou. usie-stool, what iould it remind you "The Lay of the Minstrel." WHEN is a bonnet not a bonnet? When it becomes a</p>
        <p>ad on poem of? Last</p>
        <p>woman. WHY. an an author more free than a monarch? Because he can choose his own subjects. WHY is an author the queerest animal in the world? Because his tale comes out of his head. WHY is a dressmaker a deceptive woman? Because she is not what she seams. • WHAT do liars do after death? Lie still. WHY are ladies like arrows? Because they can't go off without a beau, and are always in a quiver till they get one. NAME the greatest stand ever made for liberty. The inkstand. WHAT'iii the way to make thin babies fat? Throw them out of the window—they will be sure to come down plump. WHICH is the only way that a leopard can change his spots? By going from one spot to another. WHAT makes the oyster the most curious thing in nature? It grows a beard Without a chin, and you can take him out of bed to tuck him in. WHY is poultry-farming always profitable? Because for every grain the poultry take they • each give a peck. CAN you give a soldier's definition of a kiss? " A report at headquarters. How can a gardener become thrifty? By making the most of his thyme, and by always putting some celery in the bank.</p>
        <p>WHY is it probable that beer was made in the Ark?</p>
        <p>Because the kangaroo went in with hops and the bear was always bruin. WHY is an umbrella like a hot cross-bun ? Because it is never seen after lent. WHY is life the greatest riddle of all? Because you must give it up. WHEN does a man have to keep his word? When no one will take it. - WHEN are fleas like idiots? When they die cracked. . WHICH is the liveliest city in the world? Berlin: because it is always on the Spree. WHAT are the best letters of recommendation? s. d. How does a railway-guide resemble human life? It contains many " Ups " and "Downs." WHAT ships hardly ever sail out of sight? Hardships. WHAT pudding resembles W. G. Grace? A good batter. WHAT motive led to the invention of railroads? The loco-motive. WHAT is that which has only a nose left when one eye is put out ? Noise. WHY are deaf people like Dutch cheese? Because you can't make them here. WHICH is the most valuable: a ten pound noio or ten sovereigns? A ten pound note : because when you put it into your pocket you double it, and when you take it out again you see it increases. WHY need a traveller never starve in the desert? Because of the sand which is (sandwiches) there. WHY is sympathy like blind-man's-buff? Because it is a fellow feeling for a fellow creature WHY is a mouse like a hay-rick? Because the cat'll eat it. WHY is a little man like a good book? Because he is often loOlzed over. WHY. is a pig in a parlour I.ke a house on fire? Because the sooner he is put out the better. WHAT is the difference between a soldier and a bomb-shell? One goes to wars and the other goes to pieces. How can you double your capital? By making one pound two every day. WHAT is the difference between a Roman Catholic priest and a Baptist? One uses wax candles, and, the other dips. Wilv are birds sad in the morning Because their little bills are all over dew. WHAT words may be pronounced quicker and shorter by adding syllables to them? Quick and short. WHY is love always represented as a child? Because he never reached the age of discretion. NAME the only King ever crowned in England since the Conquest. James 1., who was King of Scotland before he was crowned King of England. WHY did Eve never fear the measles? Because she'd Adam How can you shoot hundreds of hares with one shot? Fire at a wig. •</p>
        <p>Wno had the greatest appetite ever heard of ? The man who bolted a door, threw up a window, and then sat down and swallowed a whole story.</p>
        <p>IN which part of Great Britain are the lightest men found—in Ireland, Scotland, or England?</p>
        <p>In England; because although in Ireland there are men of Cork, and in Scotland there are men of Ayr, in London, on the Thames, we have lighter. men. WHY is a good husband like dough? Because a woman needs him. WHY is a whisper like a forged bank-note? Because it.is uttered but not allowed. IF all the seas were dried up, what would every body say? We haven't a notion. • WHEN is a judge like high-water at Liverpool? When he is Mersey-full. WHY is the inside of everything mysterious? Because we can't make it out. WHY is Buckingham Palace the cheapest of Royal Residences? Because it was built for one sovereign, and fur- nished for another.</p>
        <p>WHERE can we be always sure of finding happi. ness ? In the dictionary. How can you get a set of teeth inserted gratis? Kick a dog, WHAT is lengthened by being cut at both ends? A ditch. WHAT is worse than raining cats and dogs? Hailing omnibuses. To what question has it always been impossible to anything but yes? The question, " What does 'y -e-s' spell PV IF Santos Dumont fell from his airsliip what would he fall against? His inclinations. WHY is a little dog's tail like the heart of a tree? Because its farthest from the bark. WHAT is smaller than a gnat's mouth? It's tongue. WHY is a map of Turkey in Europe like a drip- ping pan? Because there is Greece at the bottom. WHEN is a man thinner than a lath? When he is a-shaving. WHY is an egg like a horse? BECAUSE you can't use it till it is broken. IF a man who is carrying a dozen glass, lamps drops one, what does he become? A Lamp-lighter.</p>
        <p>THERE is one letter •in the marriage ceremony the substituting of which by another-would induce thousands to marry who are now single, and would give a licence for unfaithfulness to thousands who are married. Which is the letter? The letter "V." If you su6stitute the letter " K " you alter "So long as ye both shall live" into "So long as ye both shall like." WHAT is the weight of the moon in round numbers? Four quarters.</p>
        <p>THERE IS a well-known word id the English language, the two first letters of which signify a male, the three first a female, and the four first a greet man, and the whole a great woman. He, her, hero, heroine,</p>
        <p>t?,ATRONISEO BY ROYALTY, NOBILITY, B,?' TO GAIN EVERYONE'S ADMIRATIOIs;, FURNISH AT PITTS'Se.</p>
        <p>Established 1845. Tel. 1124. MANCHESTER, Who for a comparative trifle will make your home a source of pride and pleasure to you. -</p>
