      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>LIBERAL POLITICS IN 1903.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL POLITICS IN 1903.</p>
        <p>_ET A. BIRRELL, K.C.</p>
        <p>• '-The year 1903 marks an epoch in our •:unodern political history. Since the Restora- tion of the Stuarts we have never been threatened seriously with reversals of any settled mode of government. Whigs and Tories, Liberals and Conservatives, Unionists And Radicals, have quarrelled with oneo another, have reviled one another, and have, with the easy assurance of the partisan, 'prophesied disaster to the country if the other side were listened to; but, the dispute ,once settled, it has never been the fashion to go back upon accomplished facts, or to retrace the steps of an abandoned policy. After a bout •of Reform, the Conservative party would, as likely as not, be returned to office; but it -never occurred to them to repeal the Acts of Parliament of their predecessors, however bitterly they had opposed them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CONTINUITY.</head>
        <p>CONTINUITY.</p>
        <p>This method had its conveniences, since it -secured in our domestic concerns that con- -tinuity in policy the absence of which in our 'foreign affairs is the great blot upon our system of Party and Cabinet Government.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>MR. CHAMBERLAIN HAS CHANGED ALL THAT.</head>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN HAS CHANGED ALL THAT.</p>
        <p>Recognising, as he easily did, that the rank :and file of the Tory party, both agriculturai and industrial, were Protectionists, and that it was only their leaders who professed to be Free Traders, Mr. Chamberlain has, with an indifference to his past which wins him`loud applause in some quarters and secures for him contempt in others, placed himself at the head ,of the great crowd of interested persons whom no argument has ever convinced that Free imports are good for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>EMPIEE.</head>
        <p>THE EMPIEE.</p>
        <p>As if to make confusion worse confounded, 'Mr. Chamberlain has baptised his tariff schemes in the name of Empire, and has thus -succeeded in handing over a difficult economic .question to the tender mercies of the music- halls. Hatred of Germany, envy of the United 'States, are now "party principles," to be :appealed to at every election on behalf of the Empire candidate. An uglier spectacle can- .not be witnessed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>FISCAL UNITY NOW IMPOSSIBLE.</head>
        <p>FISCAL UNITY NOW IMPOSSIBLE.</p>
        <p>The mischief of it is that this strife and ,contention must destroy for years to come— unless, indeed, the Free Traders win an -immense victory at the polls—all Fiscal unity. So long as the Liberal party exists it must be a Free Trade party. It cannot be other- wise. A Liberal Chancellor of the Exchequer ,could no more frame or maintain a Protec- tionist Budget than he could propose to .exempt landlords from taxation. So long as ,one-half of the electorate of this country have -the habit of voting Liberal, so long must Untaxed Bread, Cheap Food, and Free Imports be the Fiscal policy of the country when Liberals are in power. It is not a pleasing prospect, and I can imagine even a Protectionist man of business thinking that it 'were better to have a permanent policy of Free Imports than a half-hearted and frequently discontinued war of tariffs. THE WORKING CLASSES.</p>
        <p>But we are told Fiscal unity will be secured by the overwhelming because unanimous vote of the working classes. These good people are supposed to have been "got at" by "our Joe's personal pledges, that if he is only allowed to have all his own way, and to hit the foreigner in the eye and keep out foreign- made goods, a good time will come, when wages will be always high and occupation invariably constant. "For my part,' cries Mr. Chamberlain, "I shall never be satisfied until in this country there is full employment at fair wages for every decent, honest, and industrious man." Why, 'tis the noblest Socialist of them all! Where is your Keir Hardie now?</p>
        <p>The working classes are not much given to aunanimoua voting about anything; but they would indeed be doubly-distilled fools if they did not vote for Mr. Chamberlain—provided Always they believed a word he says.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>_ THAT IS THE WHOLE QUESTION.</head>
        <p>_ THAT IS THE WHOLE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Will Protection—i.e., a tax on foreign- grown corn and meat and a host of other imports—make us better or worse-off than we .sre to-day? If it would, a cemetery of Cobdens, Milky Way of John Brights, would not prevent me from joining the ranks of the Chaplins and Lowthers, Vincents and Eckroyds' and their latest recruit, the ex- Colonial Secretary.</p>
        <p>ADAM SMITH.</p>
        <p>But how can it? Behind Cobden, behind Bright, stands a greater than either—a Scots- man called Adam Smith. The fourth book of " The Wealth of Nations" cannot be unwritten, nor the truths it unfolds falsified—not though .all the music-halls in London and Melbourne were to unite in singing "Great is Chamberlain of Birmingham."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>FOOD TAXERS AND THE POLL.</head>
        <p>FOOD TAXERS AND THE POLL.</p>
        <p>What credence is to be• given to Mr. 'Chamberlain's bluffing assertions that the working men are taking him seriously I do not know. When for the first time a great -constituency has been asked to vote for a bread-taxer we shall at least have some guidance. Till then we must wait and wonder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>STATUTE-BOOK</head>
        <p>THE STATUTE-BOOK</p>
        <p>of 1903 need not long detain us from the real politics of 1903—the Education Act and the Fiscal question. But a glance at it is necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>IRISH LAND ACT.</head>
        <p>THE IRISH LAND ACT.</p>
        <p>This is the one big measure. Let it be duly recorded, for more will be heard of A— S Edward 7, Chapter 37. It is indeed an amazing measure; every clause of it is full of history, and tells a tale of past stupidity, ,greed, and passion. It might properly be intituled "An Act to dissolve the partnership 'created by Mr., Gladstone between landlord :and tenant in Ireland, and to induce the • former by English money to sell his estates to his tenants and to cut the country." The loyal garrison is to march out amidst the chink of English sovereigns and the rustle ,of banknotes, and the disloyal, conspiring -tenants it has taken more than seventy 'Coercion Acts and a great army of mounted constabulary to keep down are to march in under the patriotic direction and stage- management of Mr. William O'Brien. And .all this was arranged behind the Chair by a Tory Secretary for Ireland and the leader of the Nationalist party, whilst the House of Commons, which for more than thirty years has discussed Irish Land Bills with fire and fury, had practically nothing to say to this one which writes the epitaph of English supremacy in Ireland. How marvellous a thing, how sublime, yet how supremely ironical -.21 thing, is the course of inevitable events! Lord Salisbury was what is called a powerful Minister; he was an able man, and for a poli- tician, "indifferent honest," yet he had hardly .more influence upon the course of events in Ireland than had the Lama of Thibet.</p>
        <p>How many thousands of acres in Ireland will become the property of their tenants under the Act? and how soon ? are questions not to be answered; but it is fervently to be hoped that the Act will prove a working measure. The landlords get £12,000,000 sterling more than the tenants are willing to pay them, but it is only fair to remember that the pranks Parliament has played with Irish land have reduced its market value; and, more potent argument than this, it is only fair that when a strong, rich country like England has prevented a weak and poor country like Ireland from settling its own land question by revolution, i.e., the cutting of</p>
        <p>throats, it should lend a helping hand to produce a settlement by credit. When all is over and done England will still be in Ireland's debt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>TEE FISANCE ACT.</head>
        <p>TEE FISANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>This measure contains one quite hilarious clause—the first, which runs (and almost giggles as it runs) as follows: "On the Ist of July, 1903, the duties on grain and other articles imposed by the Finance Act, 1902, shall cease and determine."</p>
        <p>Here we see the beginning of the policy of reversal. The fun of the clause, of course, consists in the circumstances, now public property, of its history. We all know how it came there. Cabinets cannot long survive such frank revelations. Even unemployed idiots will hardly loiter any longer in the neighbourhood of Downing-street in the hope of seeing dunderheads go to their bewildered. councils. An absurder beginning of a Protec- tionist career can hardly be imagined; and, to make it more upside-down, both the Minister who insisted upon the clause and the Minister who bitterly opposed it have left the Cabinet.</p>
        <p>THE MOTOR-CAR ACT. deserves a line, not from any merits of its, own, but because it is the first measure which tells the Statute-book of the appearance of steam-engines without rails on the King's highway. The wayfaring man need expect no mercy in the future. However, the rate-run public automobile of the proletariat may soon be expected to rush the machine of the millionaire owner of "Slimy Slips" off the road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>EMPLOYMENT OF CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE EMPLOYMENT OF CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The most useful Act of the session is one which authorises the local authority to make by-laws with respect to children trading (as it is called) in the streets, and prohibits any child under eleven from being employed in street trading; or, if a half-timer under the Factory Acts, from being employed in any other occupation. The Act contains many other useful provisions, and is one the study of which may safely be recommended, not only to all good men and women at home, but also to Germany and the United States of America. As there is nothing else in the Statute- book to detain us, I now return to the two great questions, the discussions about which have constituted the politics of 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FISCAL QUESTION</head>
        <p>THE FISCAL QUESTION</p>
        <p>is one which sometimes seems vast almost beyond the power of imagination in its possi- bilities of mischief, and at other times quite small. In the Prime Minister's hands it is really no great shakes. He could hardly do worse than he has already done with the Sugar Con- vention' and might well be asked to stay his retaliatory hand until that experiment has had time to work. We were told two things about it: First, it would benefit trade in our West Indian Colonies • and, second, it would not raise the price of sugar at home. In twelve months we ought to know whether these tellings are true or false.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RETALIATION</head>
        <p>RETALIATION</p>
        <p>is so purely a question of how we are going to do it, against whom, and with what, that until Mr. Balfour answers these questions, put to him again and again, it is hardly possible to do more than express a doubt whether it is a game worth playing. The Chancellor of the Exchequer, who would have us believe that he was a swashbuckler whilst at the University, as terrible a fellow as once was Shallow of Clement's Inn, ridicules Lord Goschen for referring to bruised knuckles. The Chancellor of the Exchequer, if only he can break a German head or a Yankee nose, is willing to run the risk of bruising his own, or, rather, his country's knuckles; but Lord Goschen's point was that we shall not break anybody's head, but only bruise our own knuckles—which seems_ superfluous.</p>
        <p>As Mr: Crombie. M.P., has pointed out in an admirable pamphlet, if Retaliation is any good in negotiation we can begin at once, for we already for revenue purposes impose heavy duties upon foreign goods—on American tobacco, for example. What effect would be produced upon the McKinley Tariff by our threatening to double that duty if the States do not let in our Bradford goods? Let Mr. Balfour try. If the area of Free Trade is likely to be increased by actual Retaliation or the threat of Retaliation, the experiment is not a dangerous one if made by men who are real lree Traders. But how many real Free Traders remain, or are likely long to remain, active and trusted members of the Tory party? Very few indeed. Retaliation in the hands of a Protectionist is a very different thing from Retaliation in the hands of Sir Michael Hicks- Beach. The latter would only use it as a means for an end, and that end Free Trade. The former would use it as in itself an end, viz., the protection of a home industry.</p>
        <p>WHAT Is ME. BA.LFOUR ?</p>
        <p>Is he a Retaliator bent on Free Trade, or is he a Protectionist like most of his party? I do not know, and unhappily it does not much matter, for unless he is willing to become the greatly regarded leader of a small body of men, most of whom will lose their seats in the House of Commons who refuse, on the one hand, to repeat the formula that import duties should be for the purposes of revenue alone, and, on the other hand, to levy taxes upon the food of Great Britain for the supposed benefit of Canada and New Zealand, he must come to Joseph's heel. He has no choice. His whole party and more than half his Cabinet have preeeded him thither. He must either go with his party or leave it to be led by Mr. Chamberlain. Which will he do? It is an interesting but purely personal question. THE EDUCATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>How so astute a politician as Mr. Chamber- lain came to allow the Education Act of 1902 to pass in the form it did is amazing. It was not done behind his back. He knew all about it, and he knew, or at least once knew, the strength of the Nonconformist connection throughout the country. He is himself a dissenter among dissenters. He lived strenuous days with Dr. Dale in the early seventies. Just as he coached Lord Hart- ington in 1885 against Fair Trade, so he coached Mr. Morley in 1873 to write his fiery tractato on "National Education," and infused into it a dissenting zeal not quite natural to the distinguished author. He can hardly have supposed such an Act of Parlia- ment would pass by unnoticed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>FEELING IN THE COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>THE FEELING IN THE COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>About the strength of this feeling there can be no question. You must go back to the reign of Queen Anne to find anything equalling it. People in this country have got out of the way of being bamboozled. No doubt the language employed to express Non- conformist indignation has often been exaggerated. There is a difference in being required to pay a school-rate and being tied to the stake; but unfair treatment in 1902 seems as shocking a thing as' downright persecution in 1550.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>FIGHT INEVITABLE.</head>
        <p>A FIGHT INEVITABLE.</p>
        <p>The question must-now be submitted to the electorate, and 'so submitted it will be. Mr. 'Chamberlain has probably already discovered his error in supposing that his Fiscal hubbub will drown the Education row. Until the two parties have ascertained their respective strengths compromise of any kind la out of the question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WHEN WILL THE ELECTION COME?</head>
        <p>WHEN WILL THE ELECTION COME?</p>
        <p>One piece of advice may safely be given: Never believe a Government Whip. The only time when a Whip really knows the date of a General Election is the only. time when he will tell you nothing about it. Mr. Chamber- lain can force an election at any time by sneering at Mr. Balfour, anct Mr. Balfour can have an election whenever he likes—so the odds are it will not be long delayed. A. RIRRELL</p>
        <p>A right good fellow was James P. Monaghan, small, keen, and wiry as a terrier, a gentleman at heart, but a trifle brusque and overbearing in his manner; as all men are who have fought their way to the top. He had made an enormous fortune in oil, and preferring to end his days in the compara- tive peace of England, had purchased Lypiaq Great House from the executors of the last Lord of Lypie.tt, and had settled down to the life of an English country gentleman. His success had been complete and instantaneous. His riding was a revelation to those who had never seen a Texan cowboy break in a buck jumper, and from the day that young Lord Partington saw him picking oft</p>
        <p>"Not at all," I ans-wer'cd. •• Lots of places that have been shut up for a long time are like, that." " I am afraid, Stewart,' he said," You didn't observe very carefully. beck again," kad striking another mat,:h he again thrust his band into the darkness. The same thing happened. The light went out. But this time I noticed, with a start, that a truly extraordinary -thing had happened. Not only bad gone out. but the mateh, the had that held it, the shirt sleeve, • the coat almost up to the elbow had all disappeared. They had all gone out like the flame. Monaghan' a rin was cut of at the edge of the panelling; as though it had been thrust in`.,o a pool of ink.</p>
        <p>MONAGHAN'S ABM WAS CUT OFF BEEN</p>
        <p>AT THE THRUST</p>
        <p>EDGE 01</p>
        <p>swallows with ft revolver, he was aimed co every shooting party in the county. But he pandered to none of the prejudices of a somewhat old fashioned aristocracy. He made friends with many outside the pale of society. He installed electric light into the Great House, He hunted in a costume only fit for the praries of Texas, and, worst of all, he once shot a fox that had killed nine and twenty of his finest fowls.</p>
        <p>Such was the man who will, if you care to ask him, bear witness to so strarige and inexplicable an occurrence that I scarcely dare to put it down as a fact on my own authority. James P. Monaghan will corroborate every word I write, and I would have you note that he was a hard-hearted American, with an absolute contempt for all forms of super- stition, and without a grain of imagination in his whole character.</p>
        <p>On New Year's Eve, a week after my arrival, there was a ball at the Great House. It was more than an ordinary occasion, for Cynthia Monaghan, the only daughter of our host, was to come out from the semi-seclusion of her school room, and bring— as little Cynthia hoped—the whole of Stormshire to her feet.</p>
        <p>•It is impossible to describe Cynthia to any ordinary mortal. From the. age of ten, and even earlier, she had Always been indescribable. It is sufficient to say that she was very beautiful, very American, very piquant and charming, from which description and from the fact that I am unable to particularise her charms, an intelligent reader will guess that I was in love with her. There was no understanding between us. We were old friends, but no more, and I think she was too much of a child to read the secret of my eyes. But on this particular night I had resolved to make some efforts to advance my hopes. I as not ineligible, even for the daughter of a millionaire. I am a distant selation of the aged Earl of Port- mannock. and will•everrtually succeed to the title. I have a fair share of this world's goods, and hope one day to inherit a third of a county. Our fathers had been intimate friends. Cynthia herself was the only difficulty, and I wanted to discover if this was an insuperable one. I had no intention of frighten- ing her with a declaration of love, but I was deter- mined to make her understand what was in my heart. •</p>
        <p>Cynthia looked her lovliest on that night, and was sufficiently bewitching and provoking to drive a lover mad. We had two dances together in the earlier part of the programme, and I had thrown so much tenderness into my speech and manner that no reasonable girl could have helped noticing the change, and being deeply affected by it. But the effect on Cynthia was discouraging. She asked what was the matter with me, and when I looked as passionately as I could into her eyes, eke did not blush or look on the ground as she ought to have done, but merely laughed in my face and said she knew the lobster omelette we had for dinner was enough to daze any man. I laughed back at her and asked her to look after me at supper, as it was evident I was not to be trusted with so much food on the table. I was battled for the moment, but only spurred to fresh efforts. There would be plenty of time and opportunity at supper, and half a glass of champagne would work wonders. But before supper came, an event happened which put a sudden end to the festivities, sent all the guests away with horror-stricken faces, and filled me with a greater •fear than I have ever ex- perienced in my whole life. We were dancing the Lancers, and Cynthia had as a partner Bobbie (sometimes called Sir :Robert) Cordillion, a man who weighed over eighteen stone, and could not find a hunter in the county to carry him. I was in the same set, and spent half my time in looking at Bobbie's efforts to combine swiftness with accuracy, and at Cynthia's energetic struggles to steer him into the right place. " arms are breaking," she whispered as she passect me, and I could well belicxe it. Few girls couldi have borne the strain so loi;g. The crisis came at last. The set was swinging round like atop.. The centrifugal force was terrific. I looked anxiously at Bobbie who was moving like a runaway traction cigitie. At least three people had their hands on his wrist, or coat sleeve. But nothing on earth could resist that terrible strain. There was a sudden rip of cloth and linen, Cynthia and another girl were flying on their faces, the rest were tumbled together in unutterable confusion, and out of the melee, like a bolt from some ancient mangonel, shot Bobbie Cordillion.</p>
        <p>THE PANELLING AS THOrGH IT HAD</p>
        <p>_ He was only six feet from the wall when the eittastrophe occurred, and he came against the panels like a cruiser ramming an oak three decker, There was a loud And and the crack of wood. Half the panel flew inwards and 'fell clattering on to a stone floor beyond. Bobbie bounded back with a groan and began to rub his arm. The set stopped and everyone roared with .laughter. Then half-a-dozen of us crowded round the hole and peered into the darkness. Monaghan came hurry- ing across the hall and asked sharply what was the matter.</p>
        <p>"A secret hiding place," said Cynthia. "Dis- covered by Sir Robert Cord i Ilion—qui te accidently," and she turned to her partner with a mocking little smile, "Let me see," said 'Monaghan, and we made way for him. He looked into the hole, which was as dark as pitch, and sniffed dubiously. We all 'chattered round the crpenincr for a few minutes, and 'made various conjectures about the secret chamber, but no one entered it. I think they had too much regard for their clothes. Then the music Of the next dance struck up, and people drifted off to find their partners. I. Monaghan, Cynthia and Bobbie Cordillion were the only ones who retained any curiosity for the broken panel, and I think the latter only lingered for decency's sake. "Smells musty," said Monaghan. "Have you a match?" .1 handed him my box. He struch- one of the matches and thrust his hand into the darkness. The flame disappeared as though it had been plunged into a bowl of water. Ho took his hand out again and looked at me enquiringly. "Carbonic aci4l gas," I said. , " M'yes," he said. "Cynthia, where's your partner for this dance ? " "Oh my, it's the Duke," she cried, looking at her programme. "I would'nt miss him for fifty gecret *ambers," and she went off to find his</p>
        <p>_ __ ___. ~... - The Examiner, Thursday Dedernber 24 1903 , 5 • _ THE ROOM OF ETERNAL NIGHT. hand. MefiSehge took them from him and gaV4 one to me. The footman stood as though expect-- Iz* further Orders. By HARRIS BURLAND, Author of " Dacobra," etc. "You can go i James," said my host, and when 1 spent the Christmas of 1899 at Lypiatt Great grace, wno was prunatny enjoying v., cigarette in the man had gone he crossed the room and, lock- House with James P. Monaghan, ex-cowboy and some remote part of the bou:.,e, and entirely forget- ing the door, returned to me. miMonaire.• He had been an intimate friend of my ful of all obligations. "Now, Stewart," he said, I father's, and I had known all the family from my "Very sorry to .I:are - opened such a beastly down:, boyhOod. The house party was a select one, too hole," said Bobbie •apolog.etically. "Anything I We both set to work with a will, and in less than select indeed for my own tastes, which verge on can do? Will mend it to-morrow with pleasure." a quarter of an hour we had stripped the whole the Bohemian. It certainly included one dluke, "Don't worry," re,-..ii&amp;l Alont=gban, and Bobbim side of the hidden room of its fine old panelling and a very renowned politician, while commoners made off. When he 'hsd gone my hatt turned tc from floor to ceiling, and laid it bare to the blaze like myself were so scarce as to be almost sought me with a thougntrul expression on his face. iof the electric light, after. "Odd about that light," he said, _ . I The extraordinary'phenomenon of the darkness _. . .</p>
        <p>'we must have it all</p>
        <p>was now made still more apparent. Under ordinary circumstances the whole of the chamber would have been almost as light as the ball room itself. But it was still a dark and impenetrable wall, absolutely indefinable as to substance, yet as defi- nite in colour as black velvet. Monaghan looked at it in silence, and then broke into a harsh laugh. " I believe we are both going mad," he said drily. "Did you ever see anything like that before ? " "No," I answered, "but I am going to see a good deal of it before I have finished," and I stepped right through the wall of gloom, half expecting, as I did so, to encounter something solid, and come</p>
        <p>crashing back to the ball room fl.cior. Directly I found myself inside the room I turned round to speak to Monaghan. But he had vanished. Lights, ball room, and every single thing had dis- appeared. There was nothing but darkness—dark- ness so intense and absolute that I might have been buried four hundred fathoms under the earth. Yet I was less than a foot from a flood of dazzling light. _ _ .</p>
        <p>-The sensation Of absolute darkness was at first curious, especially when I struck a match and could see no flame. But I made my way to the wall, and, passing my hand along it, soon got used to .the gloom. I walked all round, and across the floor several times, but encountered nothing. It struck me as curious that I had not come across any of the panelling, for in our haste we had driven at least half of it into the room itself. Then I came out to the light, and Monaghan and I both returned to the search. We went in, one on each side, and agreed to walk along the wall in opposite directions. We did this, and talked to each other all the time. I heard his voice come nearer and nearer, until it sounded right in my ear. Then it grew a little fainter and he had evidently passed me. "Did you keep your hand on the wall, Monag- han?" I said. " Yes ! "he answered, "why didn't you? " I did," I replied, for I had not taken my fingers from the stone. "One of us is—is mistaken," Monaghan said.</p>
        <p>"We must have met if we were both toiiching the wall. Where are you now?" I did not answer him. A sudden idea had struck me and was overwhelming me with its horror. I hurriedly made my way back to the light, and called to my companion. He emerged in a few seconds like a fly from an ink pot. "Did you really keep your hand on the wall all the time," I asked. " Yes " he replied, with a slight frown. "Well, I swear that I kept my hand on the wall." "Anyhow you passed me," said Monaghan. "No," I answered slowly and with emphasis, "I did not pass you, Monaghan, I passed through you." "Through me ?" he repeated. " l'hrough me? What's the matter with you, Stewart? Have you been drinking?" "Look here," I said, and taking his hand in</p>
        <p>mine I thrust both of them into t ':e -darkness. The result was precisely as I expected. Though I never let go of his hand my fingers suddenly grasped nothing and closed on my cnapty palm. He evidently had the same experience, for he withdrew his hand sharply, and I did the same, and as both our hands came out together I felt the curious sen- sation of my fingers being suddenly filled with something, and forced open, and looking down I saw that our hands were once more clasped together. "What the devil does it all mean ? " he said. "I don't profess to understand it," I answered, "but it explains why we found no fragments of the panelling inside ,the room. We simply walked through them." I stopped and watched the effect of my words on his face. His lips twitched, and he grasped me by the arm. "Then Cynthia! "he cried. "Yes," I said. "Cynthia's disappearance is ex- plained. She is still in there, and our feet have probably passed through her body a dozen times." "But why has she not spoken, and why has she not come out. We could hear each other speak. and we could move about at our will." "She has probably fainted," I said, and I told him of the scream and of the thud we had heard. "When she comes to her senses she will be all right. It must have been a terrible shock to her when she first turned round and saw that there was no light streaming through the opening in" the panel, and that she was buried in that unutterable darkness. I was pretty startled myself. One can imagine how a girl would move hastily to and fro, how she would strike the wall, how she would grasp at the, empty air, far too terrified to reason out a mode of escape, and too weak to stand 2tie nervous strain for more than a minute or two. You and I both knew what to expect, but she looked upon it as nothing but an ordinary room." - "She must be saved from a second shock," Monaghan said hurriedly. "That will be easy," I replied. Now that we know the facts we can find out her whereabouts. Sound appears to be one of the senses left to us in this place. 'We can neither see nor feel, but we can hear. If we listen carefully we shall doubtless be able to hear her breathing. We can both be close to her when she revives, and at once get her out of the lizlat."</p>
        <p>, Secause you couldn't take it if I-- aid 1! will eltogll all when we get out of this .room Will you promise to follow my directions carefully?' "Yes," she answered, "but I don't know wig you won't give me your hand. It would have been different if you heel asked for mine." "If I had," I whispered, " what would the-answer have been ? " . "The same as yours, of course," and then mimicking my tone, "I can't give you my hand, because you couldn't take it if I did.' " Cynthia! " I said passionately, " Cynthia f I love you, my darling little Cynthia. Just tell me that I have not offended you by saying so. Ido not ask for anything more at present." "You have asked too much already, sir," she said haughtily, but I thought I could hear her laugh. "How dare you say such things to me in the dark—and just after refusing my hand." I moved towards her to grasp her hand and pour forth more demonstrations of love. Then T remem- bered, and cursed the place we were in. "We wiil get back to the ball' room now at once, Cynthia," I said angrily. "For reasons which I cannot explain lam unable to lead you there. But I will keep on talking and you must follow the sound of my voice. Keep it as close to you as possible." We both advanced, and came out into the blaze of light and corporeal existence. By a happy coincidence my arm was in reality round Cynthia's waist, and her face so close to mine that our cheeks touched. She gave a:little scream and sprang away from me. "How dare you," she cried. Womanlike she thought nothing of the wonderful vilienomenon, but a great deal of the result. "I am very sorry," I said humbly, "but it was not my fault," and then I started to explain all the things I did not understand myself, and I showed how two hands might touch in that darkness and neither feel the other, and how the wholq thing was a mere concidence. "I see," she said thoughtfully, "and I suppose the words you spoke to me :just now were al§o part of the phenomenon." "Cynthia," I replied earnestly, "a man could hardly jest when for more than an hour he believed that the woman he loved was dead." Then for a moment a deep flush dyed her cheeks, and there came a look into her eyes that I have seen neither before nor since. It was not the full glow of love, which, thank God, has shone on me many times since then, but it was the dawn, the faint light and rose colour of the daybreak. Then her father entered, and behind him a crowd of guests bearing enough food and wine for an army. Cynthia insisted on the ball being resumed, and we danced till four o'clock in the morning, and as a background to all the dances and laughter and light stood that twenty feet wall of night, out of which the sun had risen for me. . The next morning Moneghan located the opposite wall of the hidden room. It was made of solid stone, but be..ore evening there was a hole in it ten feet wide. We ought to have been able to look clean through the chamber from side to side, but there was only the same impenetrable curtain of, blackness. I had thought much about the strange phenom- enon of this room, and had resolved to try an experiment. I drew a small scented cachou from my vest pocket, and going on my hands and knees placed it on the stone floor just within the dark- ness, so that I could tell exactl—, where it was. Monaghan looked at me impatiently and thought I was going out of my senses. "What the devil are you up to?" he said. For answer I thrust my head into the gloom, and putting my face down to the floor where I had placed the cachou, sniffed vigorously. The little sweet had a powerful odour, which under ordinary circumstances could have been perceived two feet feet away. I could smell nothing. Then I drew back, and putting another cachou in my mouth, again thrust my bead into the darkness. I could not detect the slightest taste. I laughed, and rose to my feet. "Well," said Monaghan, "have you finished ? " "I have learned all I wanted to know," I answer- ed with a smile. "In that room, Monaghan, a man can neither see nor feel, nor taste, nor smell. He can only hear." "I felt the walls and floor all round," he said. "Yes," I replied, bet they are the material boundary of the chamber. In the atmosphere of the chamber itself a IMF 11 has only one sense. It is almost increditable, but it is a fact." I turned away and began to examine the piece of masonry which had been hewn out of the wall for some clue to the strange phenomenon. With the kelp of the workmen I turned each piece over and scrutinised it carefully. At last I found a letter faintly carved on the stone, and continuing my search found other letters. With infinite labour, lasting a whole day, I managed to arrange these letters so as to make an intelligent sentence, and finally made out the following inscrip- tion in dog Latin; "Hie jacet corpus Edvardi Lypiatt, qui °lnnis cognovit." A week later I chanced to be in the library. It was a wet day, and I was looking through a recent publication on witchcraft in the middle ages. I was idly turning over the pages and glancing at the curious illustrations reproduced from old pictures, when a name caught my eye, and I read a few lines with interest. Then I went back to the beginning of the paragraph. It ran as follows— "The most interesting and curious event of 1456 was the burning of Edward Lypiatt, youngest son of Sir John Lypiatt of Lypiatt Great House. It was rare, indeed, in those days that one of the eristocracy was accused of a crime which has nearly always been attributed to the lower orders. This man appears to have been endowed with ex, trHordinory knoNs triNe and in.klleet, and had nlN'st:c :I lie claimed</p>
        <p>AT LAST, I FOUND A ERTTRR FAINTLY CARVED IN TUE STONE. 'Can't we get her out before she comes round r• among other things, to have discovered a means ot "Impossible," I answered. "The very nature 3epriving matter of some of its attributes His DE the case makes it impossible. Remember that • theory was as follows— while she is in there she is intangible. Let us find " Everything is known to us from our five senses. her, that's all we can do." In certain cases a thing is perceived by only four We both returned into the darkness, fell on our of these, in others by only one, and in the case of. hands and knees, and moved across the stone floor the atmosphere by none at all until the air is put in all directions. After five minutes search I heard in motion. It is also possible to deprive matter of the very faint sound of breathing, and by carefully some of its attributes, such as taste and smell. listening I located as nearly as possible the position The Indians even claim to make things invisible, of her lips. lam ashamed to say that I kissed the and it is possible for one who has the true secret of place more than once. There was little harm in earthly existence to deprive matter of every attribute kissing the empty air, and it was even ridiculous, :and practically wipe it outi of all human knowledge but it gave me as much satisfaction as the kisses , and perception. Edward Sypiatt claimed to have which lovers send to one another in letters, and I discovered this secret, and if he was at all success- felt 'guilty in proportion to the pleasure of it. ' ful it is small wonder that the people of his times Then I heard a deep sigh, and rose to my knees, burnt him at the stake. and a moment later a faint voice cried out "Where ) "It is, at any rate, related by John Malvert of am I? Where am I? Oh I" and she gave a terrified Winchester that when this ' perjured heretic' was shriek, bound to the stake, and the fire lit, both flame, "Cynthia," I said quickly, "It is all right, lam smoke, stake, body and everything entirely disap. here, close to you. The electric light has gone peared from the public gaze for a few moments. wrong. That is all. How do you feel?" Then he suddenly came into view again, and in "Is that you, Dick," she said, then she laughed. half-an-bour nothing was left of the magician but " Oh, lam real glad. How silly of me to faint. I the few charred remains of his bones. It appears remember now. ,The darkness I Am I still in the Lhat the influence of his father, though not sufficient hidden room ? " to save his life, was great enough to obtain these "We are both here." I answered, "and your few relics from the public executioner. They were father is close by." interred in Lypiatt Great House, but their restlng Monaghan spoke a few words to her and she place has never' been made known to the general answered him. public, audit is commonly believed that this is a "I am going to get some food and champagne," secret handed down from father to son." he said. "You take her out, Stewart. You seem I showed this passage to Monaghan. He said to understand this business better than I do." nothing, but the next day the masons were at work We were left alone, and once more I kissed the rebuilding the broken wall,and putting up a new place where I thought her lips might be. She and solid structure of stone between the hidden heard the faint sound. chamber and the ball room. "What are you doing?" she asked. . I also showed it to Cynthia, who laughed, and "Nothing," I answered with some truth, for it said that she had often heard of people losing their was ver3 little more. senses. " Whhre are you?" she cried "Give me your p I explained to her how I had lost mine, and bow hand. I don't believe you are anything more than &amp;they might possibly be restored to me. Alter BOT6II 0 ghost, and that I am dead." I lemur my suggestion was adopted, and it Us to ,f I cannot give you my band," I answered (sr been embirely euacessful.</p