      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>INTO A POOL OF INK.</head>
        <p>INTO A POOL OF INK.</p>
        <p>";Great Scott," I exclaimed. darkness." "Stand aside from the aperture," said Monagii; I did so, and we let the full radiance of the bit room lit with eight hundred incandescent lights stream into the hole. I thrust my arm round the corner, and looked at it. I could see nothing. Then Monaghan grasped the panelling on either side of the opening and leaned as far into the darkness as he could. I heard him draw a deep breath. Then he withdrew his head. "The air is all right," he said, "it wigidn t hurt a baby with delicate lungs. Well, any y, I guess this can wait till the morning," and stooping down he groped for the pieces of panelling, which had fallen on the floor inside. To our surprise, he could not find it, though he went on his knees, and reached cut at least a yard into the darkness, He. looked like a man whose body has been cut in half. "Its no use," he said, " Sir Robert's weight must have sent it right away from the wall," and he crawled back out of the hole. His hands were black with dust. Then I had a try, and my attempts were equally unsuccessful. I could feel nothing but a stone floor. "Come along, Stewart," he said. "We can find</p>
        <p>"This is h</p>
        <p>it in the morning. INe. have something better to do now." I came beck to the light, and we returned to our duties in the ballroom. The dance was just over, and we crossed the room to make apologies to our neglected partners. As I reached the Other side, I looked back and saw Cynthia and the Duke standing by the broken Panel. She was laughing merrily and apparently enticing the Duke to crawl through the hole. Two or three others came up to them, and the whole group A'ere chattering and peering into the darkness. Then I saw Cynthia gathering up her skirt, and suddenly changed my mind about 'finding my partner. I hurriedly retraced my steps across the room, bnt I was too 'late. •Full of mischief, and caring as little for her costly ball dress as a child for its Sunday frock, she stepped into the opening, and disappeared from view. "Cynthia," I cried angrily, and half the room turned round tolook at me. For a moment the golden head thrust itself out from the darkness, and there was a merry laugh from her lips. Then she vanished as though she had been blotted out. I made my way 9irough the group of chatterers, and looked into that well of night with a white face and anxious eyes. I might as well have looked into a blackboard. 1 could hear her footsteps and the rustle of her skirts, but the sound was a long</p>
        <p>way off "Cynthia," I cried. once. It is not safe." There was no Answer, and the rnstle and foot- steps suddenly stopped. Then we heard a scream, and a second or two later a faint thud, such as a man might make by r:tr;king a cushion lightly with his hand. Then there was complete silence. All the spectators were now thoroughly frightened, Monaghan came up, and caring nothing for Dukes, nor Countesses, pushed his way through the crowd that had now collected. " Cynthia," I said hurriedly. " She is in there." 'Little fool," he exclaimed sharply. Then he stepped through the opening and disappeared. "Cynthia," he cried. "You come out of this place at once or there'll be trouble." There was no reply, and we heard him move across the stone floor. "Why doesn't he strike a light ? " said the Duke. I did not answer him. It was no time for explana- tions of the incredible. We listened to the footsteps, circling round and round, now sounding loud and now faint. Howas evidently keeping his hand on the wall. Then the rhythm, of the movements changed, and he was a9parently crossipg and recrossing the floor. From the sounds it was evidently not a passage but a room: Every minute we heard the muffled cry of " Cynthia, Cynthia." But no voice replied. After a few minutes Monaghon emerged into the light. His face was very pale and he was covered with dust from head'to foot. The whole assembly was now gathered round the broken panel. He faced them with a wild look in his-eyes. "This is a serious matter," he said hurriedly: "I am sure you will excuse me if I end the dance. I trust you will all have some supper before you go." Then he went among them and wished them good night, after which they broke up into little groups, the women chattering to one another in frightened Whispers, the men silent and uncomfort- able. In a few minutes the room was empty, for Monaghan had asked even his house party. to leave him, assuring thin that they could offer him no</p>
        <p>"Come out of this place at</p>
        <p>assistance. .When the last person had gone he rang the bell, and a footman entered. ‘,‘ Go to the gardener's at once, James," he said, " and get me an axe, or two, if you,can' find them."' The man left the room. "I guess we'll have all that panelling down in ten minutes," he continued, turning to me. "I would rather we two did it by t-,nrselves. God knows what we shall find." And a terrible expression crossed his keen, strong face. "Did you find anything ? " I asked. "I found nothing," he replied, "not even the pieces of panelling. The room is, as far as I can judge, about twenty feat square. I walked round it several times, and crossed and re-crossed it in every direction. I could scarcely have missed a small object on the floor, and certainly not—not a body. The walls and floor are of solid stone. • I 'felt the entire surface with my hands on the chance of finding some other panel through which she might have passed, or trap door through which she had fallen. There was nothing but stone. I stamped with my feet, and beat with my hands all over the surface. It was like 'solid rock. There was not a hollow sound in any part of it. lam completely baffled. Have you anything to suggest?" "It is possible she may have dinned out somp. ' how " I answered thoughtfully. Then I remind' the faint scream and the thud. Monaghan had not heard these sounds, and no one had dared to tell him of them. In my own mind I had no doubt that the girl was still there, and that her father had missed her in the darkness. It is easy for a person who thinks he has crossed .every inch of a room in the dark to avoid almost half of it. For a minute or two we were silent, puzzling our brains for some possible solution of the mystery. Then the footman entered with an axe in either</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>CONSTIPATION.</head>
        <p>CONSTIPATION.</p>
        <p>When the bowels cannot throw off the body's refuse matter, that is Constipation. Think what it means. All matter of filth is harboured up. Once rid of this, the body can perform its proper work. Health is the result. When Constipation holds a person in thrall, disease and sickness are imminent. Some organ must give way. The kidneys, stomach or liver become deranged. Thus from one trouble, others spring. Mrs. A. Dickinson, North Kelsey, Lincoln, had for many years suffered from all the bad effects of Constipation. " nasty taste" she says, "was ever in my mouth. A dull, heavy feeling would creep over me. I always felt. sleepy. My liver was sluggish, and neither doctors or their remedies could rid me of my troubles. I tried ' Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills. They were a boon'; for . they brought back the vigour and health of my youth, and freed me from Constipation and its evil effects." Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills are a perfect blood puri- fier cure liver and kidney complaints, biliousness, indi- gestion, constipation, piles, dyspepsia, female ailments, eczema,pimpl es, and blotches. Sold by leading chemists and stores, price Is. 1-N. per bottle, or on receipt of ' price, from The W. H. Comstock Co., Ltd., 21, Farringdon Avenue, London, E.C. FREE SAMPLE. Mention this paper and enclose Id. stamp for postage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LONDON SKETCH.</head>
        <p>A LONDON SKETCH.</p>
        <p>[By Godfrey Dean.]</p>
        <p>It is Christmas eve; and the scene of this slight sketch, Loudon E.C. From early morn there has been a pervasive sugges- tion of change, something .'gnite.different from that. which has obtained during the 'three hundred and sixty-four days that have gone before. _</p>
        <p>That something is the sentiment—did you say " Dickens ? "—I agree—that attaches to this particular period of the year. RS influence is subtly diffused around, touching even the little things of daily life. There is no escaping it if one have eyes to see and perception to understand. The wit of the streets. emanating from cabby, the 'his driver, the conductor (past masters, all,of the art of repartee!) and the passing carman (who is not in the same class) is " Christmassy "—it always is topical—and if there is point that penetrates,at least it leaves no , sting. They also feel the subtle change from, say, yesterday, but the effect on them is peculiar to themselves. Being licensed commentators on the passing; show, they must sustain the part. But in the case of the city men and the multitudin- ous others crowding the thoroughfares the occasion is betrayed by more tolerance or sympathy. Does one buy violets, one's business instinct, does not assert itself, or matches ?—always with qualms of conscience—then the conscience money tribute is large. And so on, through all the minor acts of the day. But more than all is the time of the year apparent on the faces of one's fellows. See this man —one of many, but not quite a type. Some day. maybe; but one hopes not. He is on 'Change, Usually his face is set, the lips drawn tightly toget- her, the eyes unseeing (and with an abstracted expression), as •he moves along mechanically, in stepping quickly here, dodging there, or slipping sideways between two loiterers—altogether, one would think, by instinct. Perhaps his lips more and he gesticulates—thus does the great city mark some of her children. But to-day he is not quite the same, even externally. He is conscious of events outside his own personal interests. Occasionally he even smiles, and small changes in individuals are very noticeable,seen in the mass. . . .</p>
        <p>. As the night closes in, and the city puts on its garb!of artificial light, the moving stream of humanity thickens. The city clerk leaves the office with a lighter step. The feminine element increases. Sweethearts, wives, mothers and children leaven the hard humanity of the grey streets and with the brightly lighted and garnished shop windows, give colour and picturesqueness hitherto absent from the scene. °From the Fleet-street Office from which journalism speeds me every working day of my life to red hot eventsi walk slowly—because I have quite a minute to spare—to the place of my appointment. ' At the edge of Newspaperdom I stand and look towards St. Paul's opposite. On the other side of Ludgate Circus a fine view of the dome is to be had, particularly on a bright, sunny day. I cannot see it now. But instead, there is the fringe of the toy market to draw one's attention. Recently the vendors have been lined up against the kerb, each contributing to the distracting din while extracting the utmost possibilities out of his particular ware. There has, of late, been a babel of noise all up Ludgate Hill towards Cheapside. But the number of hawkers is lessened now. For the laSt few days the police have moved a large proportion of them on. An influx of these itinerants, in addition to the considerable Dumber of those whose regular stand it is, has made the pavement at times almost impassable. And their voices—shrill raucous, penetrating, often pitifully pleading—as they thrust out the toys, with a dexterous twist of the 'finger to give life to the inanimate, have conveyed more than a suggestion of a provincial fair to thewhole surround- ins. But now it is otherwise. The great god Circum- stance, Whose dictatorial voice often has so much to do with the action of the police in these apparently trivial matters, has decreed that it shall not be so any longer. The result is that the regular hawker in this local- ity enjoys comparative freedom, while the new comer, whose necessities, doubtless, are just as great has constantly to break ground on the approach of the constable. Every day with these men meads a desperate struggle to exist, to get enough food, in fact, to keep the vital spark alight in the feeble b )dy. Starvation, the Hag, will claim some of these as her' victims this winter. Christmas never comes alone, and suffering often obtrudes itself on sentiment. I turn away from the scene—to rn,?et a brother journalist, whose destination is mine, Warrington.</p>
        <p>A most useful booklet on the subject of Cornwall and the Cornish Riviera as a winter health resort is about to be published by the Great Western Railway- Co. The information contained in it should be care- fully studied by those who desire to dvoicl the fogs and cold of the British Winter, or the biting winds of the British Spring, at a minimum of inconfenienceandex- pense. A copy of this booklet can be obtained free or charge upon application from any of their Stations, or from The Great Western Railway Co. Paddington, London.</p>
        <p>,C I I : 7 I I 1 3 1 1 I I "I ; . I N _ _ _ c ''' .</p>
      </div>
