      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>We once again heartily wish all our readers " A. Merry Christmas and A Happy New Year."</p>
        <p>THERE are alwap croakers to OFIRISTAIAS. tell us, when the great</p>
        <p>festival of Christendona comes round, that Christmas is losing its hold upon the imagination of, the pole. You will hear of men who have gone to Algiers, not for their health,but to escape the English CaristarJs. Tnese unfor- tunate persons, it seems, are unable to look their relations in the face, and hold that Cnristrnas Day is insupportable unless they can spend it among strangers in a foreign hotel. We have not oh- served that they form any considerable proportion of the nation. No statistician has yet remarked the exodus of citiz3ns who fly tie country once a year to avoid the family party. There may be warrant for the believe that Christmas is not so' boisterous as it was in old times. We cannot eat and drink as rniih as our ancestors. It is possible that if a computation W3re inide of the geese and turkeys consumed at this season in the course of half a century, it wou:d he found that the average has not increa----A with the copulation. Pessimists might see in that another proof of the national decline. Life is less simple than it used to ba, and our nerves cannot stand the continuous entertainment which de- lighted our forefathers. If the old prints are to be trusted, nobody thought anything in the old days of paying a round of visits in stage-coaches, which were always buried in snowirifts. Snow- drifts in England have apparently gone out because there is so little use for then although the motor-car may bring them in again. It may- be admitted that poaple used to (I:tnn) a great deal more at Caristmastide than they dream of doing now. In this respect we have groca languid. The amazing exercise which was called the polka some sixty years ago is no longer popular with the stout and elderly. At that time, as we read in JOHN HoßsLEv's"Runiniscences,'it was a common thing for the sober citizen, without the least regard for his age or figure, to cut the maddest capers. We leave these to the children now and even among-the children the polka is apt ts, a very mild excitement.</p>
        <p>But if the gaiety of our generation is somewhat chastened, the real spirit of Christmas is surely un- subdued. The Post Office appears unconscious of any slackness in the circulation of seasonable gifts and greetings. Solemn articles are written now and then on the decline of the Christmas card ; but it comes up smiling in new designs. We have heard of no falling off in hampers, although they do not always reach the most deserving. Gloomy things are said about the trade of the country ; yet nobody has discovered Christmas crackers among the ruined industries. Some misguided person lately started a report that there was no demand for mistletoe. For decorative purposes,he said, it was declared to be useless, and he cited the opinion of some youthful gourmet that mistletoe, stuck into a plum pudding, was likely to spoil the flavour. These statements are no more worthy of serious notice than the profligate suggestion that misletoe is discarded because it is no longer needed as a special licence at Christmas parties when the spirits rush together at the meeting of the lips. Tlie benevoLnce of housewives, who pack their plum puddings to various parts of the country, and warrant them good for at least a year, shows no sign of exhaustion. Does anybody know how many puddings travel in j-irs all over the civilised world 9 Let the exiles speak who languish in countries where the art of making plum-pudding is unknown, and who are made glad by the arrival of the blessed c )mpound, which bursts into blue flame, to the stupefaction of the benighted foreigner. By a multitude of tokens Christmas asserts its unrivalled potency in the diffusion ot goodwill, whatever the croakers may say about the tedium of some of its manifestations. Its literature may have lost the first fine careless rapture of ghost stories. We may have grown a little sceptical about the conversion of Mr. EBENEZER SCROOGE. The glamour of DICKENS may:have:waned, although it is libellous to say that his ideal of Christmas was unlimited punch. But if some of the traditions show traces of wear and tear, the essential inspiration of the festival is unimpaired, the inspiration of spontaneous thankfulness and rejoicing.</p>
        <p>lAlderman Smethurst must hencefr)rtil be con- sidered a tactician. He hss admirably chosen his time for resurrecting the Warrington Bridge scheme from the obscurity into which it was consigned a considerable while ago. When there is p3ace on earth and goodwill all round the Alderman seizes his opportunity, and it says much for his sagacity and per- suasiveness that the proposed w:dening of the bridge by cantilevers was almost unanimously recommended by the General Purposes Committee on Monday. The matter has only to receive the confirmation of the Council and a great im- provement at small expend :tare wil t theu nq under way. It is remarkable that a towa oi' W trring- ton's siz ) ani importance has not tackled the 1 bridge difficulty long before now, hut in the past there has been a decided oostructionist element 10 the C itincil added to the impossibility of persuad- ing the County Council to bear a portiou of the expence of a new bridge. The cantilever schenle has aiways appeared to us a very sansible one. I will c msiderably increase th 3 wichh 0( toe road- way, provide ample and safe sec mim ,dation for the pedestrians,snd should meet the raqu.rements of the town and district fot a good tie noer ofyears. When traffic again becomes congested owing to the growth of the town, then will be the time to talk of spending, 1.,"5,000 or £O,OOO on a new bridge. We hope Alderman Smethurst will hay_ the hearty support of the Council at its nee' monthly meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>The charge of bribery in the recent Bewsey Ward election resulted yesterday in the committal of Councillor S. Gough to the Liverpool Assizes to be held in February next. Counsel were engaged on both sides, and a protracted police court hearing was necessary ere the Bench would decide that a prima facie case had been inide out. The magis- trates were engaged for nearly six hours, hearing evidence on both sides, 4nd statements of a most -conflicting nature were made on oath. It will be for the Assize jury to sift this evidence and ex- tract the truth from the untruth. The hearing yesterday was not without its amusing features and the reply of one witness quite convulsed the court. Counselsuggested that she had been in- dulging in a tete-a-tete and the reply given with some warmth was that "it was all she had." Under what class of light refreshment, we would ask, does" tete. a tete " come ?</p>
        <p>We notice from the speech of Mr. F. E. Smith, the counsel for the defence, that the poor Liberal party is in trouble once more. This time the prosecution of Councillor Gough is laid at its door, and the motives of "political malice and spite" are imputed. A week or two ago the party leaders were charged with supporting a Tory publican and opposing a Labour candidate. A challenge for proof resulted in the blame being removed to the shoulders of a few irresponsible indi- viduals who have certainly been honoured by the description, " Liberal leaders." We should like to ask now what "the Liberal party has got to do with the case against Coun. Gough? There is no party action in the matter at all that we are aware of. The Liberal party has no funds to fritter away on the prosecution of successful candidates . for alleged bribery, and a good many people would like to know the origin and the financing of the present action. Mr. Ssai..h is a little wide of the mark, and before attributing "political malice and spite " he might have made sure whether his castigation of the Liberal party was deserved.</p>
        <p>"The Devils Parade" ,is the striking title given to the evening seems of riotous and ribald be- haviour in the principal streets of the Pottery towns, along with the allegations of immorality which have been made with such insistence of late 'Towns nearer home are not without their "Devil's ' Parade" however, and it is not long since since i the Sunday night scenes n Sankey-street,War- rington fully merited such a description. Thanks to the energetic action of the Chief Constable however, matters have improved considerably and there is a minimum of the horse-play and open vulgarity which were formerly so prevalent. Even now, however, the condition of Sankey-street on Sunday evenings is far from satisfactory to respect- able inhabitants. Its crowded state makes it almost impassable and there is still room for some action which will convince the youthful Jack and .Jills that the pablic street is not the place for them to loiter about in. Perhaps Mr. Talbot will devise some means of mending matters. .Wa believe his -fertile imagination has previously turned to the 'use of the fire-hose, but this method of clearance might be attended with consequences of a too serious nature. While we may congratulate our- -selves that we are probably a bit better than our neighbours and considerably ahead of the Pottery towns, in the interests of public decency and good .government further action is called for. Sankey- street on Sundays falls far below the standard which should be maintained in a respectable community.</p>
        <p>The matters discussed at the meeting of the Education Committee on Monday evening were many and various. The agenda extended to 24 printed pages and was as imposing as a Town Counnil summons. A large amount of detail work is being transacted and ere long we may hope to see elementary and secondary education in Warrington established on a wise and com- prehensive basis. School attendance was -a subject upon which a long discussion took placa at Monday's meeting. The ‘complaints which have been made recently have induced the School Attendance Committee to -increase the staff of visitors by an additional officer. There is a well founded opinion that the staff is still below the required strength if the work is to be thoroughly well done. This department of work is not only important in the interests of the children but also from a finan- -cial point of view. The Government grant is based on the number of scholars at school, and as the returns become larger, the grants increase at the rate of 10s. 3d. per scholar. The difficulty experienced in getting some children to attend school is largely due to the indifference -of parents, and until the advantages of education are more generally appreciated, a large staff of -attendance officers will be required to remind neglectful parents of their duties to their offspring.</p>
        <p>The school -accommodation question is already being faced and steps are being taken to secure -sites for two new schools—one at Latchford. and 'the other at Cockheige. In view of the possi- bility of the Government making elementary -education an exclusively imperial charge, it would -seem rather impolitic on the part of the C3M- mittee to rush into any large expenditure on new -schools at the present time. It is hardly probable that the Government would refund any -expenditure of this kind, whilst there is just a possibility that the Education Department may -furnish the money at a later date. It all depends upon the fate of the Government in the ensuing .session of Parliament. In the meantime, the local committee might profitably rest on their ears.</p>
        <p>People possing along. Knutsford-road cannot fail to notice the extensive preparations for the municipal bowling green at Victoria Park, and noticing they must wonder whether in some respects the game is worth the candle. The site chosen is alongside the road, and there has been -a wholesale slaughter of the fine trees which ornamented this part of the land. Not a hun- dred yards away there is plenty of grass-land ',which might have been utilised for the bowling .green experiment with considerably less expendi- ture and without pulling down any trees. we -should have thought trees were a va'nable 'met in a pulilic nark, hut the Park C numittee must -either think otherwise or not at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Widening Scheme Approved by the General Purposes Committee.</p>
        <p>TilF) important question of the widening of Warring- -4°n Bridge was under discussion at a meeting of the General Purposes Committee held at the Town Hall, on Monday afternoon, under the presidency.of the Mayor. Alderman SMETHURST introduced the matter and -stated that the bridge was opened to the public in its .present form in 18:37, when.the population of the town was 20,000 and the ratable value was £32,000. The ~population was now close upon 65,000, and the ratable value amounted to £243,445. An' average of some 260 vehicles per hour passed over the structure, be- -side an average of about 150 cyclists and about 2.000 pedestrians. Warburton Bridge was six miles •distant, and was the nearest bridge to it available for wheel traffic between the counties of Lancashire and 'Cheshire. The present measurement of the .Warrington Bridge carriage-way was 20ft. between the kerbstones. 'The footpaths were only four feet wide, and he con- tended that there was a great source of danger to pedestrians, who had constantly to step into the road- way owing to the pressure of pedestrian traffic. As 'vehicles were constantly crossing the bridge close to 'sidewalks and mostly at a rapid pace, he was surprised that accidents (especially considering the tramway .service) were not more frequent. The Alderman alluded to the fatal accideneto the boy Bennett, which occurred on the bridge several months ago, and said the time had arrived for the bridge to be widened. A long discussion took place and, ultimately it was decide d, practically unanimously, to widen the bridge by means of cantilevers. It was proposed to throw out on each side of the bridge a footpath 10ft, in width, and to reserve the entire present width for vehicular tra ff‘c. Thel stone wall on each side will be removed. -The cost is estimated at from £2,500 to £3,000. At the last Town Council discussion on the subject, a 'suggestion was made that a new bridge should be erected but this did not 'meet with the support of the members. The cost of a new bridge would be about £15,000 or £16,000. In his report submitted to the Council Mr. Webster estimated the cost at £17,000 but since then there has been a reduction of £3 or £4 per ton in the price of materials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>ATTENDANCE QUESTMN.</head>
        <p>THE ATTENDANCE QUESTMN.</p>
        <p>Another Officer Appointed. THE PROPOSED NEW SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The Use of Schools as Vaccinating Stations.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Education Committee was held in the Town Hall, Warrington, on Monday evening. Mr. F. Monks,J.P., presided, and there were also present : The Mayor (Alderman Bolton), Alderman Bennett (vice-chairman), Alderman Pierpoint, Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Ellison, Burton, Dodds, Maginn, Rev. Father Wilson, Colonel Buckton, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, T. May, J. Fulton-Smith, C. H. Unsworth, E. Plinston. J. Upson, R. T. Fairclough, G. Walker, and J. Moore-Murray (director of education),J. and Fairhurst (borough treasurer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS..</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS..</p>
        <p>•The TOWN CLERK wrote stating that the Town Council. had appointed Councillors Burton and Lord representative members of the committee in place of the late Councillor Brophy and Mr. J. Charlton Parr and that Mr. Charles H. Unsworth had been elected a co-optative niember in place of Mr.W.Maddenlresigned. ORDERS FROM THE EDUCATION DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>The DIRECTOR reported the receipt of an interim order in respect of the infants' and Mixed Department of Trinity National school, draft final order for Wycliffe British, and final Orders for St. Barnabas', Latchford, Fairfield, Sankey Bridges, and Silver-street schools. FINANCE COMMITTEE'S BUSINESS. MR. MAY AGAIN PROTESTS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance Committee the Director and the Borough Treasurer were requested to endeavour to arrange, in future, for a comparative statethent to accompany the accounts each month. Letters were submitted from the managers of the St. James' school, Latchford, notifying the death of Mr. Frederick Burrows, the keeper of the school, on the 17th Novem- ber, and recommending Mr. Thomas -Slater for appoint- ment inhis place, as from the 7th December. 1903, and it was resolved that Mr. Thomas Slater be appointed keeper, at a salary at the mtelpf £7l 195., the engage- ment dating from the 7th December, 1903, and further, that, Mrs. Burrows be paid at the rate of £7l 19s. per annum for the services rendered by her as keeper of the school from the 17th November to the 6th December,l9o3. A letter from the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, the correspon- dent of the managers of the Trinity National school, in further reference to the proportion of school fees to be refunded to the managers was submitted, but the committee could not see their way to consider the matter, whichiwould come up for reconsideration in the ordinary course in April next. In accordance with the decision of the Education Committee at their last meeting) the committee recon- sidered their resolution with reference to the use of schools after school hours by the teachers, and after discussion, it was resolved that the committee will remit the charge to managers in respect of the use of school rooms for purposes of an educational character, when such a use is continuous with an ordinary school meeting, and is limited to scholars on the school registers, provided that the managers satisfy the com- mittee as to the reasonableness of the fees charged. The committee also reconsidered the resolution which was referred back to the committee by the Education committee at their last meeting, with reference to the request of the managers of the Wycliffe British school that the school keeper may be ailowed to retain the office of keeper of the church, and had an interview with the correspondent of the managers of the school (Hr. P. T. Winskill) on behalf of the managers. After careful consideration, it was resolved, that the com- mittee re-affirms its previous decision that they cannot accede to the request. It was resolved that Henry Atkinson be appointed to assist in the office of the stationery and stores clerk, at a weekly wage of 12s 6d, rising on the Ist April 1903, and thereafter annually, by 2s 6d a Sweek to a maximum of 25s a week; the engagement dating from the 7th December, 1903. —A letter was submitted from the Town Clerk, intim- ating that the Finance and Estate Committee would be prepared to rent the old Vicarage in Sankey-street to this Committee for use as an office, for the sum of 50 per annum, and it was resolved that the premises in question be rented on the terms set out in the letter.— A letter was submitted from the Association of Foundation Managers of the Cherch of England schools, requesting the committee to arrange for the insurance of the school furniture against fire, and it was re- solved, that a scheme fer the insurance of the furniture in the schools, including provision for an equitable arrangement for the payment of a proportion of the cost thereof by the managers, be drawn up for the con- sideration of the committee.</p>
        <p>The Board of Education forwarded a notification of the payment of the grant due under section 10 of the Education Act for the year 1903-4. The number of scholars in average attendance in the elementary schools maintained by the authority is 11,627 and the grant at the rate of 103. 3d. per scholar amounts to £5,958 16s. 9d. Deducting the instalment already paid- -22,181 iGs. 6d.—the balance of grant payable is £3.777 Os. 9d.</p>
        <p>In moving the adoption of the minutes Mr. JOLLEY pointed out that the resolution in regard to a com- parative statement to accompany the accounts was passed in order that the members might have an oppor- tunity of comparing the expenditure month by month. During the committee's first year of work it was almost impracticable for the officials to submit statements which would be really comparative and useful. During the second year it would be very much easier. The Borough Treasurer and the Director had, however, promised to do all they could, if it was in any way practicable, to submit to the Finance Committee's state- ments month by month which should be, as nearly as possible, comparative. The minutes with reference to the use of schools after school hours was the outcome of careful deliberation and the committee hoped it would meet the wishes of the majority as expressed at the last monthly meeting and at the same time would not hamper any purely educational effort of an un- denominational character. The resolution in regard to the school keeper at Wycliffe expressed as tersely and mildly as possible the very strong feeling of the Com- mittee that they ought not to depart from the principle they had hitherto acted upon of declining to recognize as suitable for the office of schoolkeeper any person who was a paid official of a church or chapel. Alluding to the state- ment as to the grant, he said thatarnount was to replace the aid-grant given to the voluntary schools prior to the new Act coming into force. The amount of 105.3 d. per head was the sum, they estimated when the estimates for the current year were prepared, but the total amount was slightly less than was expected. They estimated that it would be paid on 12,Q00 scholars whereas it was only 0n11,627. As a matter of fact, during the current year they were really receiving in the special grant more money than they expected. The reason was that they thought it would be paid in instalments and that one of the instalments would not be paid until after March 31st. It had, however, already been paid,so that for the current year they were actually receiving £B5B 10s. 9d. more than they expected. Next year they would be about£2oopoorer owing to having received this yearan instalment which they did not expect until next year. Alderman PIERPOINT seconded.</p>
        <p>•Mr. MAY said he rose to make his monthly protest against the item in the accounts—general, £1,031 os.Bd. It seemed to him that that was .a very inadequate state- ment for the expenditure of over £l,OOO, and he did not feel justified in voting for it in entire ignorance, being unaware—like a number of other members—of the reason why it had been incurred. In regard to the preparation of a comparative statement each month, he had been arguing for a statement of that description on several occasions. He, however, wanted the statement to include the, cost previoug to the handing over of education to that committee. That would supply use- ful information, as it would show the increased cost incurred by. the committee to the ratepayers of War- rington. That would be a useful statement and enable the ratepayers to judo how they were going on. The CHAIRMAN: It would be if we conld get it. Mr. MAY again referred to the item of £1,031 and said it seemed to him that the committee would be betra,yiug its trust and would be unworthy of public confidence if it continued to allow such an item to be passed on a mere vote on the minutes. At least two- thirds of the members did not know why theexpenditure had been incurred. He had been told that if he wanted to attend the meetings of the Finance Committee he would not be admitted. He thought the Director's ruling in this li:tatter was indorrect as the right of every member of the committee to attend the meetings of levery sub-committee had already been vindicated. They hadibeen told on high authority that the poor of War- rington were not paying the rate.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE FEES OF CHILDREN UNDER SCHOOL AGE.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT AND VACCINATION STATIONS.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee reported the re- ceipt of a letter from the head mistress of.the infants' department of the Warrington Parochial school, en- quiring as to the disposal of moneys obtained by the collection of fees from children under three years of age. It was decided that the money should be expend- ed by the head mistress as in the past, but that the attention of the managers of the school be drawn to the undesirability of children under three years of age being admitted to the school.</p>
        <p>The CHNIRMAS stated that he had received a letter from the Rev. Morley Stevenson regretting his inability to attend. Tu Mr. Stevenson's absence he moved the confirmation of the minutes of the School Management Committee. The mimutes comprised twelve pages of the agenda and represented a great deal of work. (Hear, hear.) Referring to the inquiry from the head mistress of the infants' department of the Parochial school as to the disposal of fees from children under three yeal.s of age, the Chairman said the department did not recog- nize children under three years of age so that the</p>
