      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events.</p>
        <p>At Cl7olborne-street Church on Sunday evening the choir will render selections from " The Messiah." The principals will be Miss E. Miller and Miss Wood- land (soprano), Mrs. W. H. Cotterill (contralto), and Mr. Harry Berrey (tenor). Mr. T. Brough will con- duct. There will be a watchnight service on New Year's Eve and on New Year's Day the annual tea meeting will beheld.</p>
        <p>The -km J. Yonge will preach at Wycliffe Church on Sunday next morning and evening. On New Year's Eve there will be a watch night service. There will be a Christmas choral service at Bold- street Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening. . The Banks in Warrington will close at noon on New Year's Day. The Stockton_ Heath Tramway.</p>
        <p>The plans of the extension ol this tramway to Victoria7square have this week received the approval of the Cheshire County Council and the Board of Trade, and the work will be proceeded with early in the New Year. Fire in Mersey-street._ - -</p>
        <p>At eight o'clock yesterday morning P.C. Turner conveyed information to the Fire Engine Station of an outbreak of fire at the shop of Mr. Isaac Houghton, Mersey-street. The brigade, in charge of Superin.- tendent Turner, was promptly on the scene with the engine and all appliances. Their Services were not, however, required as a number of neighbours had extinguished the flames, but not before con- siderable damage was done to the stock. The fire originated on the ground fl or where a new gas stove had been fixed.</p>
        <p>Licensing. At the Borough Police Court on Monday, Arthur Hughes was granted temporary authority to sell a an off-licensed ' house, 63, Priestley-street in lieu of Nathaniel Adams. FOR CLASS AND CHINA. of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Harvesting in Christmas Week. The housing of grain is just being completed in the Caldbeck and Ireby districts of Cumberland. Harvesting within a week of Christmas is an opera- tion as novel as it is undesirable and the poor farmer will trust that this year's record will never be broken</p>
        <p>GIVE THEM A TRIAL.—ALF. TAYLOR'S wonder-</p>
        <p>ful boots for everybody. The Action Against the Ship Canal Company._</p>
        <p>In the Court of Appeal on Monday the case of Crosfield v. The Manchester Ship Canal was men- tioned by Mr. Fletcher Moulton, K.C., who asked that the appeal should not come on on the first day of next sittings. The application was granted, and leave was given to counsel to make another application with regard to the hearing of the appeal.</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER theCLocK.—Advt.</p>
        <p>London and North-Western Big. Extension.</p>
        <p>The London and North-Western Ifailway Company have served notice upon the Mid-CheShire local autho- rities and landowners that where necessary they intend to compulsorily acquire land, for a big scheme of rail- way widening. It is proposed to widen and have a double set of rails over a considerable stretch of the Liverpool to London main line. So far as Cheshire is concerned all points between Acton Bridge, Winsford, Hartford, and Crewe will be included in the widening project. FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt. Cake Toy, and Apron Sale. • . . . _</p>
        <p>_ With the object of raising funds to pay the interest on the debt still owing on the Wycliffe Memorial Building, Edgeworth-street, a cake, toy, and apron sale was held on Saturday evening and was attended with success. Three stalls were attractively arranged and the wares fnund a ready sale. The ladies who assisted at the stalls were :--lirs. Yonge, and the Misses Porritt, Bateman, Bindloss, and Corney. The amount realized was between £7 and £B.</p>
        <p>A GOOD RESOLUTION.—Wear ALP T,tYLOn'S Wonderful Boots.—Advt. Charge of Rope Stealing. Manchester Ship canal company have recently missed a considerable quantity of rope, which is sup- posed to have been stolen. On Tuesday, William Williams, a storekeeper in the company's employ, was charffed at the Manchester County Police Court with the offence, and another man named James Barlow, a barge owner, of Rixton, near Warrington, with re- ceiving the rope knowing it to have been stolen. Accused were committed to the Sessions, bail being allowed.</p>
        <p>Local Will. Mr. Alfred Bolton, formerly of IlThitecross, War- rington, and of Kragersdorp, near Johannesburg, but last of the Cottages, Middleton Hall-road, King's Norton, Worcester, who died on August 22nd last, at 29, Upper Gloucester-place, Dorset-square, London appointed his mother, Mrs. Elizabeth Bolton, as sole executrix of his will, and by whom his estate in the United Kingdom has been valued at £1,975.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE Tonc is the KINO OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warn i ngton.—Advt. Sankey Harriers. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>On gaturday Sankey harriers held their annual Chrismas handicap from the Black Horse. Trail was laid by Messrs. Potts Hardman and Tomkinson, over seven miles of good country, through Bold Park. Over 30 runners turned ou't, and, thanks to the generosity of several tradesmen in the town, each received a prize. The first six were :-1, T. Minshull; 2, L. Edwardson; 3, L. Green: 4, John Riley; 5, A. Wilcock; 6, R. Gibson.</p>
        <p>ASK tor' CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS JIIINERA.</p>
        <p>WATERS.—Advt. UNDER the CLOCK or GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. Unprotected Fires.</p>
        <p>"-There have been fourteen children burnt to death in my district since the 14th of October owing to fires being unprotected," said Coroner Brighouse, with much indignation, at an inquest at St. Helens Town Hall, on Saturday, on the body of Eveline Boylan, the five-year-old 'child of Mrs. Derbyshire, of 19, Athol- street, Earlestown. The child was left in die house on Saturday week by her mother. The hot cinders had been raked on to the hearth, and the fender and everything taken away. The child afterwards said she was putting coals into the grate, when one of the hot 'cinders on the hearth set her pinafore on fire. The Coroner spoke strongly on the matter, and the jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRIT) GE-STREET.—Ad?t- .BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS hi latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53. and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. High School Exhibition. There was an interesting exhibition of students' work on Monday afternoon at the High School for Girls, Wilson Patten-street, conducted by the Misses Partington. The school has been under its pr;esent direction for two terms only, but the progress already made by the students reflects infinite credit upon the school and its efficient staff, The work exhibited was that done by students in addition to the usual school cuAiculum, and included painting, chip-carving, poker-work, needlework and embroidery of all des- criptions. The work bore evidence, of the arti:tic taste of the students and their apitude for these accomplishments. There were specimens of lands- scope, flower and textile paintings, some excellently- carved panels for chairs, tables, cupboards and fancy-boxes, mid one or two fabrics showing skill in poker-wcrrk, in which, we under stand, heated needles play an important part. The lieedle*ork included Honiton point lace, Irish lace, ribbon-work, drawn-thread work and crochetrv. Other exhibits were of embroidery of various kinds, plain work, including blouses &amp;c., and kindergarten work. In an adjoining room there was a cafe chantant, at which the music was provided by the pupils and staff, and included violin and pianoforte playing. There were also several recitations. About GO parents and friends of the students visited the exhibition and were delighted with the exhibits.—The breaking-up dance in connection with the school took place on Tuesday evening, when about GO guests attended at the Masonic Hall. Mrs. Hayward, of the Winmarleigh Caf4 catered in her usually acceptable- manner. •</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK for BES BOOTS in LANCASHIRE.—AdVt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Examiner, Thursday December 24, 1903</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Thursday December 24, 1903</p>
        <p>IVlusical Successes. tile local examination of the Incorporated Society EDUCATION IN WARRINGTON of Musicians held at Liverpool on the Ist of December the. following candidates, who were all prepared by Miss Agatha Richardson of 114 Buttermarket street, (late 29, Irlam Street) were successful. Miss Cecelia Mary Caldwell;of Widnes, grade I ii,B6 marlthshonours, Miss Ann Jane Caldwell, of Widnes, preparatory grade, 80, marks honours, Miss Jessie Hibbert, of Warrington, preparatory grade, 80 marks, honours. Amongst the recent successes at the examination held in Liverpool by the London College of Music on December 15th, Miss Vera O'Brien 44, Bewsey Street, gained a first class for pianoforte in the elementary grade.—The following is a list of the pupils of Mr. Clarles H. DaWSOM, L.V.C.M., 28, Arthur-street, Warrington, organist and choirmaster of the Parish Church of Newton-in-Makerfield, who successfully passed the recent examination held at Warrington on December Bth :—Practical: Miss S. Rigby, 95 marks. honours, intermediate grade, singing;' Miss Violet Brown, 86 marks, honours, intermediate grade, piano- forte playing. Theoretical: Mr. Edward Jones, intermediate honours section; Miss Elsie Gandy, 95 marks, honours section, junior grade; Miss Elsie Dawson, 99 marks, honours section, junior grade; Miss Maggie Hankey, -95 marks, junior pass section Press and Pulpit. •</p>
        <p>The members Of St. John's Literary Society held their usual fortnightly meeting in the church school- room, John-street, on Tuesday evening, Mr. P. H. Brown, vice-president, occupying the chair. The evening was devoted to a debate on the question "Does the Press exercise greater influence than the Pulpit?" Mr. J. Bennett led for the affirmative. He drew attention to the great lack of interest on the part I of the masses in religious subjects, pointing out that I the best proof of the pulpit's influence would be the I people's attendance at places of worship Sunday after Sunday. That they did not so attend was shown by a recent church census taken in Liverpool where it was found that out of a population of 640,000 only 73,000 attended a place of worship in the morning and I 110,000,in the evening. On the other:hand there was scarcely a house in .the country into which the ! press did not enter in some form or other; it practically reached every man and woman in the land. The press ever lifted up its voice for all that ! was good, and comparing the condition. of their ownl country, with its press, with those lands where news- i papers were rare and priests reigned supreme, it' would be found we were infinitely better both as . re- gards religion and morals.—Mr. J. Robertson led for , the negative, and held that the church exerted a (Treater influence than any other agency. The reputed I influence of the press was greatly exaggerated. When the \vivid was in its infancy the priesthood was estab- lished and soon became a mighty force. Compared with it the press was a modern and mushroom growth, newspapers being comparatively rare in our I grandfathers' time. The press exerted little in- fluence in forming character, the contents being forgotten almost as soon as laid aside. Mr. Robert- son referred to the great influence exerted by early! apostles and modern 'preachers and evangelists, and I the results of preaching as seen in the mission field.— After considerable discussion a vote was taken and resulted in a majority of eight for the negative. You CANNOT DO BETTER! Buy your boots and shoes at ALP, TAYLOR'S. Cheapest and best town. . The Fatality at Orford Tannery. The circumstances attending the death of James Lee, 35 years of age, a general labourer residing at 29, Victoria-street, who sustained fatal injuries at Orford Tannery last Thursday were inquired into by Coroner Brighouse and a jury at the Police Station on Monday evening. Mr. E. A. Connor, solicitor' repre- sented the relatives of the deceased, and Mr. A. Lewis, Inspector of Factories for the district, and Mr. Harry I Fairelough, builder and contractor, were also in attendance.—Deceased, it appeared from the evidence, was Working at a drying shed: in course of erection at the tannery. The building had reached the fourth storey, and a crane was being used. On Thursday morning Lee, together with workmen named James Nairn and John Barrett, was working at the crane, when it was noticed that the sleepers Olt which the crane was planted had become loose. Lee went below to tighten up the wire rope ,k which were attached to the sleepers for the purpose of holding them down and was followed shortly afterwards by Nairn and Barrett. The rope, which was tightened by-means of a pole had been made taut, when the rope slipped off the end of the pole. The result was that the pole whirled round with considerable force, striking Lee and Nairn about the head. The men were conveyed to the Infirmary, but afiter being attended to, Nairn was able to go home. Deceased died early on Friday morning from a fractured skull.— Questioned by the Inspector, Nairn said the crane was one thatwas commonly used for that kind of work. —William Jerams, the foreman, said the crane was capable of lifting three tons. It was erected in the customary way.—The Inspector said he considered the method adopted for erecting the crane a very primi- tive and risky one,- and he was astonished to hear a practical man like Jerams say that he would use the crane to lift three tons. He certainly considered that , it would have been far better to have used iron clamps in the erection of the crane.—The jury re- turned a verdict of " Accidental death," and added that they attributed the accident to the faulty erec: tion of the crane. H. M. Astrros's LIFE TONIC is the Itnret off TONICS.---63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Oddfellowship.—Presentation. On Saturday a convivial evening was spent at the Loyal Olive Branch Lodge, a special feature of the proceedings being the presentini-, of two framed Past, Officer's Certificates by the Grand Master of the dis- trict (Bro.. Ellis R. Clare) to P.G. Sam Hassall and P.G. J. W. Livingston on behalf of the officers and: brethren of the lodge for services rendered. The meeting was presided over by Deputy Grand Master Holford the following officers and visiting brethren being present: M.G. William Hammond, V. G-. Butter- worth, L.O. M. Barnard, P.G. John Mather (treasurer), P.P.G. Thomas Steel, P.G. Frederick Taylor, P.P.G.M. Allen (Chester distriet), P.P.G.M. Norbury, G.M. Latham (Ashton-in-Makerfield),D.G.M. Seddon (Earles- town), P.P.G.M. Thane (Secretary, Viaduct Lodge, Earlestown), P.P.G.M. Gilbey and several other officers and brethren from the Ashton-in-Makerfield district.—The District Grand Master, in making the presentation, said that it was a very pleasant duty for him to present P.G. Hassall and P.O. Livingston with a past officer's certificate for the Work they had done for their lodge. He had presented similar certificates in other lodges, but this presentation afforded him most pleasure, because he was making it to igiembers of his own lodge. It was no easy task to pass threugh the offices in the Olive Branch Lodge, and he felt certain that these two brethren had discharged their duties in a most capable manner. He hoped they would put the certificates in a conspicuous place in their homes and when they looked upon them it would re- mind them of the happy times they had spent in this lodge and stir them to greater achievements. He had great pleasure in making the presentation to them on behalf of the officers and brethreilof the lodge. P.O-. Hassall in acknowledging said that he fully appreciated the honour his lodge had bestowed on him that night. His time was very much occo- pied and he had not been able to give the attention to his duties he would like to have done, but he had done his best in the interests of the lodge and he hoped at some future time to be in a position to make up for what he had not been( able to do in the past. He thanked them very much for their kindness in making him that presentation. P.G. Livingston said it had been a great pleasure to him in going through the offices of the lodge and doing what he could in its behalf. He was pleased he had given satisfaction and thanked them very much. During the evening songs were contributed" by P.G. Hassan, Bro. Bmith, Bro. G. Sumner, 0. M. Latham, P.P.G.M. Gilbev, P.P.G.M. Alban Boyle and a recitation by Houghton. -P.O. Fred. Webster ably officiated at the piano. The lodge was closed in the usual manner all having spent a most enjoyable evening ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.--Advt.</p>
        <p>` moneys received from them in fees belonged to the schools and tho head mistress was left to deal with the fees as she had done before. It was proposed to make a little addition to the pupil teachers' agreement, which he would ask Mr. Murray to read. "Ir. J. MooFte MußrtAy said there was no reference in the surety to any undertaking on the part of the parent to feed, clothe and watch over tne pupil teacher. He would suggest that the clause be introeuced into the surety that the parent or guardian shall clothe, feed, lodge and watch over the pupil teacher during the continuance of the engagement in a proper manner. The Rev. Father WILSON seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said he wishod to again refer to a matter which he raised before thecommittee that was the use of certain schools for the purpose of vaccina- tion. They were all extremely sorry to hear of the epidemic of smallpox and in his opinion it was a mistake to allow schools to be used as vaccination 'stations. The risk of infection might seem a remote one, but he thought that by allowing these people to come into the schools it was just possible to communicate contagion which might exist in some of their homes and that in- volved a risk to the children. He thought the com- mittee would have been better advised and the Medical Officer of Health also—he criticised him as well,although he had a high opinion of him—(hear, hear)—if they had conducted the vaccination on premises which were not used for the assemblage of large numbers of people. He noticed that in the warning notices which had been issued by the Medical Officer of Health it was stated that all persons suffering from smallpox who exposed themselves in any public place or vehicle and who did not call in a medical mart or the Medical Officer of Health would be prosecuted with the utmost rigour of the law. It was just possible that such people might come to the schools to be vaccinated and extend the disease still further to those who used the schools next morning. He did not want to be considered an alarmist or one who took an exaggerated view of pos- sibilities, but he believed there wts an element of risk which ought to be avoided. _</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS referred to the memorandum of agreement for head and assistant teachers, and called attention to clause 6 which stated that " if the teacher ,be continuously absent from 'duty through illness or any other cause the full salary will be paid." He I thought they ought to define the cause of absence, They ought not to allow, for instance, impropriety of conduct to be a cause of absence. The CHAIRM AN : That would not be. ' Councillor DODDS pointed out that he did not see anything in the agreement that precluded it. It ought tolbe clearly set forth in the agreement that the cause of absence must be certified by a doctor, otherwise the teacher got the money whatever happened. The CHAIRMAN : Impropriety of conduct would be dealt with by the Committee. Councillor DODDS : That is outside the agreement. Something ought to be inserted to prevent payment. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH suggested that the word •• reasonable" be inserted thus making it read "if the teacher be continuously absent from duty through illness or any other reasonable cause the full salary will•be paid." _ _ _</p>
        <p>This as agreed to and the minutes were then passed. 'SCHOOL ATTENDANCE. AN ADDITIONAL OFFICER APPOINTED.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee recommended that an additional school attendance officer be appointed at a commencing salary of £65 rising to a maximum of £9l per annum, and further recommended that Mr. William Farrar and Mr. Thomas William Holbrook be appointed school attendance officers at a commencing salary, in each case of £65 per annum Co incillor ELLISON, in moving the conformation of the minutes, observed that something was said at the last, meeting about the insufficient way in which cLildren were visited. He would like to read a statement on the work done during the past month. During the last month Mr. Bamber had visited 2,014 cases; Mr. Scans- brick, 744; and Mr. Rutter' 1,588; and in three weeks the new officers had visited 2,532 cases. Of these Mr. Farrar had. visited 970, and Mr. Holbrook 1,562. The total visits were thus 6,908. Continuing, he said that one officer visited 743 cases in one week, and in the following week another officer visited 768 cases. It was impossible for any officer to visit so many cases and report upon them all in a proper manner. The number of children on the books in the borough was 13,728, which gave an average of 2,745 for each officer. In Manchester the average for each officer was 2,011. Prior to the appointment of the fifth visitor the num- ber of children to each officer was 3,432. The work of school visitation was also more difficult in a town like Warrington, where the children attended schools out of the distric:, in which they lived, thus necessitating a system of transference for visiting purposes and a re- transference for reporting purposes. The great draw- back of the existing conditions was that an officer could not be attached to one school or set of schools, but often-times the whole of the five officers would have cases from one school. That destroyed that healthy rivalry which should exist between the officers, because no one officer could be held entirely responsible for the attendance at a school. Even with the increased staff the number of children to each officer was too high. There was no allowance for a special officer who should devote his time to cases of truant children, committals to industrial, deaf and dumb and imbecile institutions,aud what was more important house-to-house visitation and the tracing of removals. Every month there would be some six cases of removal which the officers say they could not trace on account of the large amount of visitation they were called upon to do. Further, owing to sickness the office has been understaffed since August. He thought those figures would show the necessity of appointing an additional officer. He hoped as a result that they would see a very great improvement during the 'coming year in school attendance. (Hear, hear.) Mr. May seconded and remarked that in supporting the appointment of an additional inquiry officer he was actuated by the fact that the officer would more than pay his own expenses by the increased grant that the schools would earn in consequence of his labours. He was glad that the committee were going to do some- thing forlthe poor class of children, after all.the money they had spent on one class for higher education. The last named were rich, whereas the poor needed educa- tion most.</p>
        <p>no relation at all to the business before us. Just stick I to the minutes. Mr. MAY: lam sticking to thil item of Cl,Oll. Alderman PIERPOINT : Well, stick to that an) g) on talking about it. - Mr. Max : tarn doing so. The pow of Wtrrin'4,l;on are p tying the rates and they ought to know something of what they are being paid for. Councilor DooDs: Perhaps if Mr. May would allow Mr. Murray to answer his question it would end his difficulty. The CRAIRM AN: practice— Mr. : Of protesting.</p>
        <p>Mr. May a rogalar</p>
        <p>_ The CEIAIRM Vs; (continuing): Month by month talking about things that people don't understand; I think it is just at well:to let him have his fling. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>. Mr.„ iwant to suggest that the finances should be left to the Gmeral Committee, alit at the proper time I shall make a proposal that the Finance Com- mittee be abolished. (Loud laughter and hear, hear.) The CfLun,3lA.N: r would like to ask the committee if they would like every item to be read out. If so we can have thelbooks brought here. Mr. MAY: That IS as much as to say that you cannot attend properly to the finances, and you will not allow anybody else to do it properly. At the Chairman's request, Mr. MURRAY pointed out that the sum of £1,031 included a payment to the Cor- poration of interest on the Technical Institute Fund of £395 19s. 6d.; return of fees to managers, £152 4s. 6d.; caretakers' salaries, :LIN 2s. 4d. The balance of about £4OO was for general items.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY S'aid that if he were permitted to attend the Meetings of the sub-committees he Would make no further protest, but at the proper timehe would suggest that the finances should be left to the general com- mittee. The Town Council had entrusted the finances with the committee and they had no right to delegate the authority without the express sanction of the Council With reference to the contention that the poor did not pay the education rate the School Attendance Com- mittee recently heard an application by a Man who stated that his rent had been raised 3d., per week and he was also paying school fees. Mr. May was proceeding to allude to the action of a sub-committee of the Cheshire Education Authority when</p>
        <p>The CIIAIRMAN interposed with the remark: You must keep to the minutes. Mr. MAY: It seems to ma when such matters are carried on-</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: I must ask you to keep to the minutes.</p>
        <p>Mr.lllfAY (ignoring the interruption) : by one-sixth of the members of the whole committee and when they control the expenditure and lam asked to vote for a sum-</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : Now Mr. May you must sit down. Mr. MAY (continuing) : on which T have had no- The CHAIRMAN: If you are are going to treat the chair like that, I'm off. Alderman PIERPOINF : Off the chair? (Laughter.) The CHAIR3IAN : Yes. Mr. MAY resumed his seat.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS said that when the committee had selected ten gentlemen to form the Finance Committee, he thought that so long as they gave the necessary attentionand were .prepared to explain any item which needed explanation, that ought to be ample. Councillor Pierpoint, the Chairman, Mr. Jolley and himself were at the last meeting half an hour before the time and they went carefully through every item of every account. They were quite prepared to defend the accounts if Mr. May would tell them which items he took exception to. Mr. Ursos : Are not these accounts open to Mr May's inspection. Alderman Pierpoint : Certainly. The CHAIRMAN He would not be allowed to attend the meetings. Mr. MAY again rose to speak but was ruled out of order. Alderman PIERPOINT remarked that members of the Town Council were entitled to attend the meetings of sub-committees of that authority of which they were not members, so long as they took no part in the pro- ceedings. He had no objection to Mr. May attending the meetings of the Finance Committee if he would not disturb them by making orations like they had heard that night. (Laughter). The minutes were then confirmed, Mr. May being the only dissentient. Alderman PLERPOINT thought Mr. Plinston had hit the right nail on the head. The discussion must afford a great deal of wisdom, especially to the Chairman of the School Attendance Committee. The Chairman would no doubt see that the attendances were made better. If he could not get theta better with his exist- ing staff, he must make application for farther assist- ance and he was sure the committee would grant it to him. (Hear, hear:) Mr. A. J. Jor.niv said he had no desire to interpose but he could not refrain from giving expression to a feeling of unholy joy that Mr. May who, after slating his (Mr. Jolley's) committee had at last found the committee of which he (Mr. May) was a distinguished memberomable to escape adverse criticism. (Laughter). ' Whatever shortcomings his (Mr. Jolley's) committee might have been guilty of, it would appear that even if they had had the benefit of Mr. May's assistance, they ; would not have escaped criticism. (Laughter.) Councillor ELLlsox,in reply, said they had 33 attend- ance cases before them on the previous Thursday. Mr. I Upson had said that more children ought to e sum- moned, but he (Councillor Ellison) thought the school- masters were at fault if they did not report them, (Laughter.) Mr. UPSON : They are reported twice a week. Councillor EursoN : Perhaps they don't report as many as they ought to do. Mr. UPSON : We make out duplicate registers each week.</p>
        <p>Councillor ELLISON : That nifty be so in your school but not in all. (Laughter.) Continuing, he said be- fore the recent outbreak of sickness he believed the attendance in Warrington was. quite equal to most towns in England, ani better than in a great many. With regard to certificates, the committee had always impressed upon the parents the importance of getting certificates. A nurnher of certificates were kept at the School Attendance Office, ready to be filled up, and if they would take them and get them signed, it would relieve the parents of any further trouble and expense. The minutes were then passed. SITES AND BUILDINGS CONIMITTEE. THE SITES FOR THE NEW SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. UPSON did not think he could sit quiet after the statement; of the Chairman. Last month he (Mr. Upson) called attention to the unsatisfactory condition of attendance. He did not see that they had improved it. Judging by the returns it. was rather the other way. The return stated that there were 8,000 children on the books. He supposed it meant that there was only that number in the schools that had been opened. in spite of epidemic sickness the attendance in Warrington was very unsatisfactory, and the town was losing very largely from the irregularity of the children's attendance at school. He was not 'prepared to say what was the cause, but speaking as a practical teacher he knew that the attendance was nothing like what it should be. Councillor Ellison had said that Warrington was a difficult town to work. He did not see why; it ought to be a very easy town to work. All boroughs had a difficulty with children living outside the Brie•ough boundary. In Warrington they had comparatively few. The committee had now six attendance officers and he maintained that the work ought to be done very much better than it had been done. He noticed there were 28 cases summoned before the School Attendance Committee in one month. He knew schools where there ought to be 28 such cases from each school. More parents ought to be brought before the committee and, if necessary sent before the magistrates. This question of attendance was a serious one. Every time the schools were opened there were 2,000 children absent. it was not a fact that children under five were keeping other children out. There were only 647 children under five attending school. It was not a sufficient excuse to say that the irregularity of attendance was caused by the lack of accommoda- tion. There was sufficient accommodation for the children on the books. Two children out of every ten or nearly so were not at school when it was open and where a place was provided. Rev. Father WILSON said he could not help agreeing with most of what had fallen from the last speaker. He would like to make one suggestion with regard to medical certificates for absence from school. Some- times it was a hardship on the parents to be obliged to send a doctor's certificate. Sometimes a child was away from school in consequence of a slight ailment and no doctor was called in. The committips, perhaps wisely, insisted upon a doctor's certificate to show the cause of absence. The charge for a certificate might vary from ls: 6d. to 2s. 6d. He would suggest it might be possible for the Attendance Committee to enter into an arrange- ment with medical men to supply at the expense of the Committee, certificates for a small , fee. Such an arrangement was ia existence in some towns, and it tended to make the .requirement of certificates for absence really like a regulation which acted and was not a dead letter, as he thought it was at present here. Such an arrangement would tend to ameliorate the attendance. There was a great deal of sickness about, and though the sickness did not account altogether for the lowness of the attendance, still it was made a pretext for keeping children at home longer than necessary if they had been sick; • and for keeping children away who were not sick on the chance of them being overlooked. Mr. PLINSTON said he could not enter into details of the work of the school attendance officers, but he was under the impression that the officers did their dutt, in a praiseworthy manner. He would like tounciller Upson to remember— Councillor DODDS: "Ex-councillor." ( Laughter). Mr. PLPNSTON : I beg pardon. Continuing, he said they had been for some time without the services of the chief officer and also of Mr. Scarisbrick. They had now get two new men and from the figures given, he thought it. would be admitted that they had done useful work. With reference to the number brought before the committee, he had sometimes sat 1. hours hearing cases. it had been stated in the committee that it was no use sending parents before the magistrates, because when there was a certain number on the bench, penalties of only one shilling and half-a-crown were inflicted. The magistrates did not help the committee. Mr. Upson ought to chastise the magistrates rather than the committee. (Laughter.) He thought an improve- ment in attendance would take place now that they had thee two new men. He would suggest that still another man was wanted.—(hear, hear)—so that the work would be thoroughly done. Alderman PIERPOINT (interrupting) : Although T am willing to give a good deal of my time, I am not ...prepared to come here and discuss a question that has</p>
        <p>The Sites and Buildings Committee considered the best means of dealing with the question of fair wear and tear in the various elementary schools in the Borough, and the proportion of the cost, in respect thereof to be borne by the Education Committee, and, after some discussion, it was resolved that, pending the receipt of the report of the Borough Surveyor on the condition of the school buildings, the matter be de- ferred for consideration by the committee at a special meeting to be held for the purpose. The Chairman reported the result of the sub-committee's efforts to secure sites for the new schools to he erected in Latch- ford and Cockhedc,re. and it was resolved that the Direc- tor do confer with ELM. Inspector as to the desirability of certain sites. It. was also resolved, that Messrs. C. W. Davenport's offer to sell the hoarding round the land at the back of the Technical Institute, and the clerk of the works' &amp;flee," for the sum of £2O be accepted. The Director and Borough Surveyor having asked for authority to obtain plans of all the schools it was resolved that a survey be made and plans of the schools peepared by the Borough Surveyor, and that he be authorised to obtain additional assistance for this purpose. The MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minutes, and referring to the preparation of plans for the exist- ing schools said this work tvas essential before they went into details. He thought the offer of Messrs. Davenport a reasonable one. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH seconded. Cotmcillor Dorms asked whether the additional assist- ance to the Borough Surveyor was temporary or permanent. The MAYOR: Temporary. The work will be sent out to various architects in the town. The Borough Surveyor could not do the work with his present staff. Mr. CPSON suggested that some of the school authori- ties had plans of their buildings. Would it not be a saving if a copy were taken? The Mayon: We want the plans certified as the buildings are at present. Many of the buildings have undergone alterations and extensions since the original plans were drawn. Major BLICKTON thought that b3fore this expense was incurred, it 'should be ascertained what plansw ere in existence. With regard to the school with which he was connected, the plan was framed and was hanging in the schoolroom. Any surveyor who wanted to make a copy of it could obtain it. The schools were built exactly according to .the plan, and he thought that would be the case in regard to all the newer schools. The CHAIRMAN thought the Surveyor's attention might be called to the fact. Alderman SMETIIIJRST said there were old plans in existence in connection with the schools he was connec- ted with, and it would save a great deal of trouble if the Surveyor would go over them and see if they were correct. The MAYOR said it was necessary that all the schools should be drawn to one scale. Mr. A. J. JOLLRY pointed out that the question of fair wear and tear was an important one to school managers, He would be glad of an assurance that the matter would receive speedy attention. It was desirable that the matter should be settled on an equitable basis as soon •possible. Councillor ELLISON asked whether the sub-committee was likely to secure the sites thought of for the Latch- ford and.Cockhedge schools. The MAYOR, in reply, said the fair wear and tear question had been before the committee more than any other subject. It was% question on which only the Borough Surveyor could really certify. Some of the schools had been so much worn that the cost of putting them in a good condition would be more than the com- mittee ought to pay. The committee was waiting foa the Borough Surveyor's report.He understood that, there 1 were a number of schools which would receive a few hundreds of pounds each. He did not know how they would use it, but they might spend some of it on the buildings. (Hear, hear and laughter). The sites of the new schools were those they had hadunder observation. One was opposite the Cheshire Cheese and they had made a temporary agreement with the °wilco% The</p>
        <p>other site at Cockhedge was one that rested with the Street Improvement Committee. It had been left in the hands of the Town Clerk and himself to make an arrangement. The minutes were carried.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, at the couclusion of the business, wished the members the compliments of the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>CRAWFORD'S</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>SCOTCH SHORTBREAD The " Crawford" Quality. lIIGRLAND. Thick Cakes. LOT RIAN. Thin Sections, sugared on top. AYRSHIRE. Thin oblong Cakes, sugared on top. Sold by Grocers and Bakers everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>OUR ALMANACK ILLUSTRATION. maDERN BRIDGE STREET.</p>
        <p>In the days of long ago Bridge-street changed its name from.N.ewgate-street to that by which we know it; to-day it is slowly but surely changing its appear- ance. An old Warringtonian who had been absent from the town for a brief space of time would fail to recognise the scene depicted in our photographic black as typical of the narrow and winding thorough- fare which he knew as Bridge-street. Where, for instance, he would ask is Sutton's cake shop, with its touching the footpath or the quaint look- ing establishments of Chandley's or Toft's or that old, dingy-looking hostelry—the Feathers? Then looking at Rylancls-street corner, so admirably portrayed by Mr. Parkinson in the photograph from which we have reproduced our illustration, an old Warringtonian could not fail to be agreeably surprised at the change which has been effected. :qr. Ralph Johnson's new and striking shops front heightens the effect, whilst at the opposite coraer there has disappeared forever, the little hair-dresser's shop, so long identi- fied with Mr. Podmore's name. The change which has been wroaglit in a few brief years in that section of Bridge-street extending from Friar's-green to Rylauds-street is remarkable and pleasing. In place of a narrow street, with mean looking biisinsss pre- mises, we have now stately business establishments abutting on a wide thoroughfare, lighted by elec- tricity, with trams moving smoothly and swiftly along its route.</p>
        <p>Street improvements of this kind in main thorough— fares are perhaps the most expensive which a Corporation can undertake. The Town Clerk at a recent Local Government Board inquiry referred to it as a "terrible legacy." The widening of main thorough- fares in Warrington has already incurred an expendi- ture of £270,000 and the Town Clerk estimates that a further expenditure' of £200,000 will be incurred before the work is completed. From inquiries made of ,the Borough Treasurer we learn that the widening of the section of Bridge-street from Friars' Green to Rylands street has approximately cost the ratepayArs £30,000. The first purchase of property in this section was nude in 1305 and the negotiations for the others occupied nearly five years. The money was borrowed under a provisional order and the work was steadily proceeded With until the section was completed. The C Tporation has still some back land to dispose of, and assessing this at its market value, it may be safely stated that £30,000 represents the cost of widening this section of Bridge-street. The improvement which has been effected is an important one, and without it the trams could not have run from Rylands-street.This is Dow a very busy centre and we are indebted to .llr. .Tanns Parkinson. photographer, of Egypt-street,for his faithful delinea- tion of the view from this point.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>: THE FISCAL PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>: THE FISCAL PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Letter Frani Mr. A. H. Grosfield. Sir,—" The .good old times, all times are good when old," is a satirical line of Lord Bsron's, which some people seem disposed to take in very serious earnest. Mr. Balfour 's half way house, unstable, built on a quicksand, is already swept away, and Mr. Chamberiain, with what Mr. Winston Churchill well describes as "Music Hall and Pantomime Finance" is inviting us to go hack to the old discredited system of Protection. What that means in the present day for even ago ahead country like Germany has been well described by the able General Secretary of the Iron and Steel Workers' Association. An excellent resume of his speeches—one of them delivered in Warrington—has been published in the journal of this Association. Mr. Cox has very kindly forwarded to Inc some copies and these speeches des6rve the attention of every iron and steel worker in our town. I take from them a few brief extracts illustrating the present state of affairs in Germany. Mr. Cox reminds us that in 1902 a memorial was presented to the German Government couched in the following terms : For two and a half years the whole economic life of Germany has bean inn condition which bears the character of a crisis, notwithstanding the consider- able export trade it is still conducting, under the protection of existing treaties of commerce. German industry in its most productive branch is suffering from want 01. employment in a high degree, a very widespread want of work for those whom ft. employs and from a reduction of wages. The spirit of enterprise is practically extinct. New plant is hardly anywhere being acquired, and manufacturing premises are hardly anywhere being extended. Moreover, the efforts of employers to keep the works going have resulted—especially in the case of syndicated industries—in the exportation of a large portion of their products at unprecedently low prices, a procedure which if it were to last • much longer must inflict the gravest damage upon the German economic body." Mr. Cox then cites some statements from the German "Government Tariff Enquiry Report"; statements made by German manufacturer:::— "One striking result of the dumping policy is that by supplying manufacturers &amp;abroad with materials at low prices, the German syndicates make it passible for these foreigners to compete on very favourable terms with their rivals in Germany in regard to the sale of finished products." The difference in price, e.g., between the prices charged to the Germap and foreign manufacturers, by German cartells, makes it possible for the Belgian and English rolling mills to lower their prices and quite ruin the foreign markets, with consequent evil result to the German manufacturers, who work up the half-finished products." "The cartells sold raw material and half-finished goods abroad at low prices, so that the home (i-c., German) industries which worked up this raw ' material were severely handicapped. Cases actually transpired in which German manufacturers had to decline orders owing to the exorbitant prices of raw materials, which orders Subsequently passed to Holland, Belgium, or the United Kingdom." Mr. Cox then asks:—" But how about German wages under this?" He cottild imagine someone thinking that probably this high-priced home cemmodity carried with it higher wages. Let the following extract from a speech delivered in the Prussian Landtag suffice:— "In Germany) manufacturers having to pay more for their materials than what foreign manufac- turers pay to the German cartells for the same article, and being unable to make any saving on the goods, are obliged to cover themselves by cutting wages." If the United States and Germany, supply, as they do supply, evidence which is an overwhelming indict- ment on many counts against the evils of Protection in the present day, our own country supplies us with proof in the past which is just as strong. Lord Macaulay has given us some weighty testimony and as he was an eve witness of it all may I venture to quote what he said when speaking in 1845 : "UnhaPpily we are able to bring to the test of facts the doctrine that the dearness of food benefits the labourer and injures only the capitalist. The price of food is rising. Are wages rising? On the contrary they are falling. In numerous districts the symptoms of distress are already perceptible. The manufacturers are already beginning to work short time. Warned by repeated experience they know well what is coming, and expect that 184 G will be a second 1841. If these things do not teach us wisdom we are past all teaching. Twice in ten years we have seen the price of corn go up; and,as it went up the wages of the labouring classes went down. Twice in the same period we hare seen the Price of corn go down; and, as it went down, the wages of the labouring classes went down. Surely such experiments as these would in any science be Considered decisive. The prospect is doubtless gloomy. Yet it has its bright past. I have already congratulated you that Lord John Russell and those who have hitherto acted on this subject in concert with him, have given up all thoughts of a fixed duty. I have to congratulate'you on another fact not less important. Tam assured that the working class of the manu- facturing districts have at last come to understand this question. The sharp discipline which they have undergone has produced this good effect; that they will never again listen to any orator who wilt have the effrontery to toll them that their wages rise and fall with the price of the A correspondent of yours hints at the time, let us hope a far more distant time, when in a better land he may hold a watching brief over the fartunes of this country, If Lord Macauley holds such a brief now, what would he think of the attempt that is being made by the High Priest of Protection, and made too with every artifice of the detnagogue, to cajole, and decoy .thoughtless people back to the old evil days of Protection? To ensure the defeat of a policy so reckless and so fatal, is not Mr. Churchill perfectly right in asking that. the mass of our population, whether Liberal or Con- servative, should join forces in order to offer over- whelming opposition to a scheme which is going to. make the poor man poorer all through the land,in order that a few rich people may be made richer, and which will have a baneful influence on the whole of our com- mercial and national life.—Yours Ste., A. H. CROSFIELD. London, December 22nd, 1903,</p>
      </div>
