      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>AN EXPLANATORY CATECHISM.</head>
        <p>AN EXPLANATORY CATECHISM.</p>
        <p>1. TVltat is Gwilym. Emits' Quinine Bitters</p>
        <p>An excellent vegetable Remedy, prepared in the most skilful and scientific manner, and containing the active principles of those plants generally acknowledged to be the best remedial agents, and to possess the most heal- ing and curative properties. It is now extensively used in many countries, and everywhere acknowledged as the Best Tonic Remedy known.</p>
        <p>2. TVhat does it contain, 7</p>
        <p>Quinine and the active principles of the following well-known herbs: Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Gentian, Lavender, Burdock, and Dandelion, scientifically pre- pared, and in such happyproportions as to form a Tonic Bitters positively unequalled.</p>
        <p>3. Wharts its action.</p>
        <p>assists an., promotes Digestion, strengthens t Nerves and Muscles, purifies the Blood, enlivens the Spirits, and removes all Obstructions and Impurities in the Human Body. It gives tone to the whole system. It strengthens and fortifies those parts which have been weakened by Disease, and are consequently more liable to Colds and unhealthful influences. It has gained the Highest Recommendations as the Best Family Medicine and a remedy for every kind of Weakness.</p>
        <p>4. What Diseases is it specially recominendeg for</p>
        <p>Influenza, and the after effec attack of Influenza, Indigestion, Chest Affect Diseases of the Liver in all their various forms, Neuralgia and all Nervous Disorders, Depression of Spirits, General Weakness, Loss of Appetite, Sleeplessness.</p>
        <p>Must patients confine themselves within doors when using it</p>
        <p>; the working man need not abstain from his labour; the mother from her household duties, or the child be kept trom school while using jt.</p>
        <p>6. Is there any difficulty in procuring it?</p>
        <p>There need be none, as it can be obtained of all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors, or will be forwarded direct by the Proprietors, carriage free, on receipt of stamps. It is sold in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each. Beware of Imitations. See the name " Gwilym Evans" on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. Sole Proprietors: Quinioe Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>THE INEFFECTUAL BLACK LIST. John Wilfred Delooze, Warrington's first and only "black-lister," appeared before the Mayor and other magistrates at the County Borough Police Court on Monday on the charge of having been drunk and dis- orderly in Market-street at 10.50 on Saturday night.— P.C. Clarke said the prisoner was lying across the foot- path. Witness got him os his feet and asked him to .go home, but he refused and was very violent on the way to the Police Station.— The Chief Constable said that in January prisoner was paced upon the ineffectual and futile black-list. This was his fourth appearance since then for being drunk. He said he sent out for drink from private houses and there was nothing in the world to prevent him from doing so. The bleck-list was not much use as a deter- rent if a man was determined to have drink.—Prisoner had been before the court 33 times, mostly for drunken- ness. He ought to change his name to "D3booze."— Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON POLICEMEN VIOLENTLY ASS A.ULTED.- " BRE A.K ING TEIEM IN."</p>
        <p>Violent assaults on police officers are no uncommon •offences at Warrington, and on Monday tvc o bad cases were dealt with by the magistrates. In the first, John Shields, of no fixed residence. was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street and assaulting P.C. Reddington on Saturday night.—The constable said prisoner, who was very drunk, was shout- ipg at the top of his voice, and stopping people who isa.ssed along the street. Witness advised him to go home, but as he persisted in his disgraceful conduct he had to he taken into custody. He then became very violent and threw himself to the ground. P.C. Clough came to witness's assistance and they were obliged to put the snaps on." Prisoner had to be " frog- marched " in Mersey-street, and he kicked .witness several times. At Bridge Foot Sergeant Smith came to the officers' aid, and the prisoner continued to be very rough.—P.C. Clough corroborated.—Prisoner said the case was "a made-up affair."—The Chief Constable said Shields had been before the court 46 times. He had served 5 years'. penal servitude and had 18 lashes with the" cat" for robbery with violence. There were 13 convictions against him for assaulting the police. Every time he came to Warrington he had to be locked up.—He was sentenced to two months' imprisonment.— A ship's fireman named John Ward was then charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Scotland- road on Saturday night and assaulting P.C. Burrows,— The officer said prisoner challenged another man to fight and as he refused to go away witness took him into custody. He was very violent and struck witness in the chest, and commenced to kick. Witness managed to get him on the ground and a man named .Tames Bufke assisted him until P.C.'s Hewitt and Sweeney came upon the scene and helped to carry the prisoner to the Police Station. A large hostile crowd surrounded witness and threatened Burke for helping 'him.—Snperintendent Monaghan gave evidence and said Burrows was a new officer. Assaults on constables who had just joined the force ivere fre- quent. The people called it "breaking them in."—The Chief Constable said the prisoner was an habitual -assaulter of the police and had been before the court thirteen times. He had served twelve months'imprison- ment foriponnding a police officer.—The magistrates sent prisoner to goal for two months and cordially -thanked Mr. Burke who was awarded ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>RDAY.—Before Mr. Birchall (in the chair), Dr.</p>
        <p>Fox, Messrs. R. T. Fairclough, and Hayward. AN INVETERATE THlEF.—Catherine Pritchard, of no axed residence, was charged with stealing from a clothes line in the passage at the rear of Winmarleigh-street, a pair of sheets, of the value of 55., the property of William S. Haddock; further with stealing from a wash-house in the yard at 9, Froghall-lane, three Venetian window blinds and other articles of the total value of 30s. the property of Betsy Melling; and also with stealing from the y,g,rd at 21, Erwood-street, a dolly-tub of the value of 2T, the property of James Hickey—The Chief Constable said that at least half • a dozen charges could have been brought against the prisoner. She had been in the habit of going &amp;Jou; begging or pretending to seek employment at the back- doors of houses, and when not observed she purloined</p>
        <p>$</p>
      </div>
