ispose of them at any of the banhs in Warriagton, and so he came to Manchester I and offered them to the Bank of England. The officials I there would not accept them, but told him they knew a !man who would. He was recommended to Mr. Hartley, who, after some negotiation, purchased the lot. The L counting occupied over two hours, and in tho end the : value of the good coins came out at something between COLLIERY ENGINEER FINED. : I£s and £O. The Warrington- man got his money and Samuel Smith, of Crow-lane, Earlestown, who has , Mr. Hartley took the farthings, to dispose of in small been engine winder at the Havannah Collieries, Parr, 1 quantities in ,various parts of Lancashireand Yorkshire. belonging to Messrs. R. Evans and Co., of Haydock, 1 Another time a great shoal of farthings was brought pleaded guilty at St. Helens on Monday, to stealing a to the "poor man's bank" by the trustees in the will of quantity of timber and some bolts and washers from ; a Rochdale grocer. His mania was the hoarding of the colliery.—Police-constableCampsey, of Earlestown, met him going home early on Saturday morning with : these mites. ! There is more romance in copper than most people timber under his arm, and on visiting Smith's residence ' would imagine. he found in the backyard a shed built of the same kind: of timber.—Smith was fined 40s. • DIE DISTRESS AT EARLE.STOWN. On Saturday afternoon a general committee meeting of the Earlestown Thursday Football Club was held for the purpose of receiving and considering applications , for relief, which it was decided to give out of the pro- OLD WOMAN BURNT TO DEATH. eeds of the' concert held on Thursday evening which Ellen Harrison, widow, aged seventy-nine, of 6, mounted to the splendid total of £32. About 90 de- Grace's-square, Sutton, St. Helens, was the subject of serving eases were submitted, and it was decided to an inquest at Peasley Cross on Saturday, when Mrs. give each applicant a parcel of grocery, meat, Stca_a 'Jane Lees said