        <p>A RICH BEGGAR.</p>
        <p>Susannah Lomas, of Manchester, was begging about the St, Helens streets on Sunday night, having a little girl of eight with her. The woman was beseeching people to give her a penny for her night's lodgings, and as shewas very bold Police-constable Evans locked her np,frid found she had fifty-six pennies and forty-one halfpennies in her p(msession. The magistrates ordered the police to see her off to Manchester by train. AN OLD THEORY EXPLODED._</p>
        <p>"Finding is—keeping "is an old theory which was ex- ploded on Tuesday in the case of William Slater, aged eleven, of 57, Raglan-street, St. Helens, who was charged with stealing from a stall in the market. Slater said he was walking past with another boy and saw a pair of gloves on the ground. He picked them up and put them in his pocket. having found them.— The magistrates said this kind of finding must be checked. Slater must endure six strokes of the birch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>SIX MONTHS FOR CHILD NEGLECT.</head>
        <p>SIX MONTHS FOR CHILD NEGLECT.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Monday, John Townsend, Fisher-street MS charged on a warrant with negl.ecting his,two chil- dren. Mr. J. W. Knowles prosecuted on behalf of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, and said the prisoner was a low scamp and a wastrel, and was one of the worst of vagabonds that it was possible to imagine.—Accused was committed to gaol for .91x months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>"HOW PAUL STANHOPE HOODWINKED AN EDITOR."</head>
        <p>"HOW PAUL STANHOPE HOODWINKED AN EDITOR."</p>
        <p>BY THOMAS SANDERS.</p>
        <p>AUTHOR of :—"Bob Harringtons's Choice," "Sam's Sacrifice," "Tit for Tat," "How I Became a Peer of the Realm," "How I Became a Fortune-teller," "Twixt Husband and Father," "The Finding of Dorothy Dare," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Yes! I had just been up to hear Chamberlain's Glasgow speech, and was pacing up and down the Ex- changestation platform, Manchester,waiting for a train home, when, in the distance I saw a man approaching. There was nothing startling in that, of course, but as he came nearer I noticed that the people turned to stare at him. Evidently he was some great celebrity., • I stared too, and to my amazement recognised my one-time school-mate, Paul Stanhope. It was a mutual recognition, and, coming hastily up to me, he shook my hand until I hardly knew where I had put it—under a weighing machine, or between the buffers of an engine. , After we had exchanged some jocular remarks about ' each other's appearance, we began, to talk on other things. I felt very nervous for Paul had become a great man. After a time I mustered up courage to ask him the question which had for a low, time bewildered me. IWith an assumed nonchalance fblurted out,— "But my dear fellow how came you so suddenly into prominence? "Ah ! That is a story I have often wished to tell," I said he; "but we have so little time. lam afraid our I trains would go without us if I attempted to tell you here. But, however, we'll risk it." And pulling me with him into the refreshment bar, after my favourite drink of bovril,we sat down, and he began his tale. * * * * "It was Friday morning. My wife and I sat at break- fast. We had often sat thus, and it all depended upon a certain circumstance whether I left the table in a good humour or in a bad one. If a bulky looking *package was thrown to me across the table, everything began to go wrong. The tea was either too cold or too sweet, I the toast was try, oroblie eggs boiled hard. If, however., this formidable package did not arrive, I left the table light-hearted and joyous, for I thought the editor was considering my story favourably: I had been sending stories to the editor of "The Penman's Friend" for years, and had them returned just as regularly. They were good stories and the only possible reason for their refusal was that the editor had taken a prejudice against me. People called him a prince among litterateurs; I called him a fiend, and I think my wife regarded him in much the same light. had never seen him, but, beinga well-known man, i his photograph had often appeared n the magazines,and his features were therefore familiar to me. One day, as I waS walking along Fleet-street,a gentle- man happened to be walking briskly in front of ine. He turned to glance at a shop window, and I at once recog- nised the editor of," The Penman's Friend." Instantly the blood flew to my head, and with diffi- culty I restrained myself from seizing his eoat collar and shaking him. Yes! he was placidly walking along as though he had never injured anyone; and there was following behind, knowing that if he would only accept one of my stories, my fortune would be made. As I gazed at him the devil entered my soul, and the thirst for revenge took possession of me. I.vowed that if ever the opportunity occurred I would humiliate this proud and haughty man. One morning as I sat at breakfast, my jaws dealing savagely with a rasher of bacon,and my brain unravel- ling the intricacies of a new plot, my wife entered the room with a face wreathed in smiles.</p>
        <p>She threw down a thin envelope with a London post- mark, and I could see she thought it was a cheque for my last story. I opened it hastily, and glancing through its contents, I jumped up with an exclamation and began to read aloud.</p>
        <p>"My dear Stanhope, You ask me for a plot, your excuse being that you are spent out. Nothing of the sort. However, I will furnish you with the skeleton of a story which I at one time intended to write up." Here followed the synopsis, which I would have scorned to conceive. I was about to throw it into the fire with the comment "trash "when my wife intervened. "Evidently there is some mistake," she said. Impatiently I turned to the address again and read it, "Paul Stanhope, Esq., 2, Beech Grove, Rysdale Park, London, S.W." No! That was my address, but what the dickens did it mean?</p>
        <p>At last a thought struck me, and I rushed from the room into the one adjoining. There I flung myself into a chair, thrust ray hands into my trousers' pockets, stretched my legs out to their fullest extent and sat thinking. Yes ! I remembered that I bore the same name as the well-known story-writer who had taken the literary world by storm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>ABOUT BRIGHT'S DISEASE.</head>
        <p>ABOUT BRIGHT'S DISEASE.</p>
        <p>From Phcenix. Green, Winchfield, Hants, comes thE; report of a case which is sure to startle ma,ny physicians and others who, up to this time, have believed and declared that Bright's Disease was incurable. From the positive statement of Mr. C. W. Sumpster, of that town. Bright's Disease is curable. • He had suffered with Bright's Disease for three years. His doctors had given him up, they told him that nothing could cure him, "You must go into the hospital," they said, "and perhaps, with the greatest care, you may live through the winter. This is the best advice we can give you." Mr. Sumpster says : "I was nearly giving up. I saw no hope before me and felt that no powedan earth could save me. But I was wrong, Dodd's Kidney Pills saved me and, did for me what all the doctors failed to d 6, they completely restored me to health. I took nine boxes of the Pills before I was com- pletely cured, and now lam quite well and strong. I work sixteen hours a day without feeling it and, instead of being in the hospital, I am as well as I ever was in my life, and this is entirely due to Dodds' Kidney Pills."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Druids Camp Lodge.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above lodge, Warrington Equalised District Order of Druids .was held at the Higher Angel, Butterrnarket-street, on Saturday under the presidency of Bro. Richard Charnley P.A. The yiceTchair was occupied by Bro. Arthur Charnley who was supported by Bros. Samuel Hankey Robert Perdue P.A., William Hankev; Peter White and several visiting, brethren. The Chairman called on Bro. Samuel Hankey to present Bro. Richard Charnley with a beautiful illuminated certificate of merit, which he did in a few well-chosen remarks. Bro Bichard Charnlev thanked the members for the Present. Bro. S. Packer P.D.G-.11., secretary, gave an outline of the position of the Lodge. There was an increase of 20 members, with .a substantial increase in the funds. Songs and recitations were contributed by Bros. White, Derbyshire and Picton, and the meet- ing closed with a vote 6f thanks to the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>CHRISTMAS SPIRIT.</head>
        <p>THE CHRISTMAS SPIRIT.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Petty Sessions, on Monday, there were anumber of cases of drunkenness and profane language. and the chairman (Mr. Ockleston) said the magistrates would let them off cheap at ss. each because of Christmas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WARRINGTON FARTHING HOARD.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON FARTHING HOARD.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. WARRINGTON "A "TEAMS.</p>
        <p>These teams met at the Irwell-lane enclosure on Saturday, before a fair attendance. At the outset the home team had the best of play, for following Lang- ley's commencement, clever work by the Runcorn backs harassed Knight's charge. Eventually the Warrington backs displayed superiority in the passing department, and capital exchanges between Duckworth, Edmonds and Mahoney changed the venne,and Kenyon, receiving from the last-named, scored a really fine try. Knight added the goal. Re-starting from centre,Rnncorn made progress through the medium of Carroll, and this player looked like to score, until Short tackled hlra a few - yards inskle. Knight !kicked well, and eased the pressure slightly, and twice in quick succession Wilkinson was lucky in holding Kenyon when near the line. Faulty play by Faulkner endangered the Runcorn goal, and it was left to Warder to save the situation, a smart run transferring play to neutral quarters.4lllWalton, Faulkner, and Warder tried hard to break through, but he defence exhibited by Knight Edmunds and Duckworth was safe, and by the aid of short kicks the game ruled even, and nothing furthe't of interest was not up to half-time, at which period Warrington led by five points to nil. Turning round, the visitors took up a strong position, and early on Edmunds took advantage of a weak kick. Knight, kicked a goal. The combination by the Warrington backs resulted in Kenyon scoring his second try, from which he added a goal, Warrington running out by three gools three tries (15 points) to nil.</p>
        <p>How I mourned my fate and wished that even the mortification of bearing the same name as a well-known and successful writer had been spared me. Here I was in my little den, surrounded by all my familiar possessions. In this place had been matured all the plots of my stories ; here, my ambition had been born only to be cruelly murdered by the action of the editor of" The Penman's Friend." And, worst thought of all, I held in my possession the plot of a story which he had intended to write, and which was literally trash. I had a thought. That thought became an inspiration. •I wheeled round my writine table and began to write. I had in anticipation tasted' the first fruits of revenge, and I determined to be revenged still further. I beganto write, and continued writing. Hour after hour went by. Then I grew nervously excited, and jumped up with an exclamation, sending the inkpot across the floor. I knew it not. The writing fever was upon me. At that moment I heard a tap at the door and I knew that my wife wished to tell me that tea was ready. I could not stop—l dared not stop. I yelled out for her to leave me; I was just about strangling the villain. I heard her return to the sitting room, and I thought I detected a sob. But what did that matter? She had gone back similarly many a time, and who was to blame b it the editor of "The Penman's Friend"? At last I had buried the villain as respectably as I could in a field, near the graveyard, and brought to a climax" The Tririmph of Patience and Virtue. Then I sauntered into the sitting room to take tea with my wife. I. sat up very late that night typing out my story for oT. HELENS CABLE, CO. V. LONDON AND NORTH- the paper, and the following morning saw me posting WESTERN RAILWAY. larger parcel than usual addressed to the editor of `The Played on the ground of the former. The visitors Penman's Friend." kicked off, no return being made. From the ensuing The days which followed went very slowly. My wife scrimmage Lloyd secured the oval and running finely watched with her usual anxiety for the letter which she I I gave to Jackson who completed the effort by scoring in thought would inform me that my story was accepted.' the first minute. The Cable undaunted by this early I did not: I knew that none would come; and, further, reverse played up gamely, and for a time held their that the MS would not be returned. Instead,l watched opponents at bay. At length however, the splendid for the second number from that date of "The Penman's back play of the railway men began to make things Friend." • I hum, and Timmins receiving from his captain dashed On the mornirig of its publication, I rushed to the aside the opposition and scored a capital try between newsagent's for 'a copy. I opened it hastily, There it ! the posts, the same player converting. The home was: "The Triumph of Patience and Virtue," by the ! team next had a turn, Gibson being well author of "Mysterious Moonlight," and " Roy Nether- I tackled when looking dangerous. Fine play by Roscoe stone's Will." I and Allen kept up the pressure, and twice the visitors "What, a lie." I said to myself. Bat what did it ! saved by kicking dead. From loose play Jackson got matter? I was revenged,—and that was all I wanted.' hold in his own half and putting on top speed raced I went home rhucklirig to myself, and congratulating lin grandly, Timmins doing the needful. From a cross all the powers that had helped me to achieve my object. I kick Jackson scored again, and the first half ended g4My wife could not understand me,and I did not intend L. and N.-W.R. 2 goals 4 tries, Cable nil. Upon the re- that she should. When I pointed to the story in the ! paper and told her it was • mine, she looked incredul- start it was seen the homesters were playing four three- : ! quarters, and for a time it served to check the visitors, ous. !hut eventually Lloyd and Timmins opening out the "But you did not write the other two which are given play, quick sharp transfers were the order, and Hewitt as the productions of the same author ?" I and Jackson combining well, the latter ran over thrice "No, my deaf; but lam having my revenge, on the in quick succession. The Cable though outclassed editor of "The Penman's Friend," and•that• is all I care about for the present." played a hard game, Roscoe at full back repeatedly I saving his side from disaster. Final result L. &amp; N.-W.R. A day or two went by and nothing further took places 2 goals 7 tries (25 points), St. Helens Cable Co. nil. Then the public were amazed to read in all the paper that the author of "Mysterious Moonlight " had not written " The Triumph -of Patience and Virtue." What did it all mean? Here was the editor of " The FLOATED THROUGH A TUNNEL. Penman's Friend" publishing a story annoenced as being A fisherman named itelland, nearly 70 years of age, written by a certain author. Then this was followed • was found floating, near Hallsands, in Start Bay, by a flat denial by that author to the effect that he had Devon, on Monday. He was missed front home on not written it; Surely there was something very Saturday night, and it is conjectured that he fell over- mysterious about it. It ought to be cleared up, and board in Sheepton Ley, a fresh water lake some distance was the only person who could clear it up. in from the bay, and that his body was carried through Was I going to clear it up? No. Certainly not until the lengthy tunnel that connects the Ley with the sea. I had punished the editor for his past discourteous There is no other way of accounting for i_ts being found treatment of me. And he was being punished. The at Hallsands,some three miles away from Torcross. papers circulated the rumour that the close friendship Charles Hammond, who had been employed as grave which had existed between the author of "Mysterious digger at Norwich Cemetery since 1857, had between Moonlight" and the editor of" The Penman's Friend" thatyear end 1900, when he , retired through infirmity, had been severed. ' dug over 15,000 graves. On Saturday he was laid to How I gloated over it. The leading journals were Test himself. Hammond was a Crimean veteran. full of the literary fracas. Some spoke of the action of Lbrd and Lady Dudley have been visiting some of the editor as an outrage on modern journalism. I the houses in the poorest- slums of Dublin, and leaving "Grand!" I thought. ' tea, plum-puddings, turkeys, roast beef, and in some At last T let my wife into the secret. I told her how cases money. The inmates did not know their visitors I had worked up the plot found, in the editor's letter. , were the Viceroy and his wife. Also how I had sent it in, signing my own name which ' • happened to be the same as that of the well-known story / writer. I had not put any address, but simply sent it in typewritten and without any note enclosed. When the office reader saw the typewritten signature at the bottom he thought the author had hurriedly composed the bottom, and so he passed it on to the editer who did not even trouble to acknowledge it, for, being such great friends, this business formality had been dropped between them. So it was printed and published, and, in due course, brought upon the head of the editor the wrath of the supposed author, and the vituperation of a sympathetic public. • . At litst, when I thoughtsufficient punishment had been meted out to him, I told my wife I intended to pay the , editor a visit, and. disclose my connection with the affair. She was frightened, and pointed out the risk I ran. I replied, however, that when I signed the name of the well-known writer T. had only been signing my own name. "But," she said, " there is the fact of your having used the plot of the editor which was not given to you."l</p>
        <p>.........</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>INTER-WORKS LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTER-WORKS LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A fine crowd of about 6,000 people assembled at Wilderspool on Saturday to witness the match with Runcorn. Excellent weather prevailed and both sides were strongly represented. The teams and officials were:—</p>
        <p>W-a-rriegton—Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dicken- son and Hockenhall, three-quarter backs; Davies and Bate, half-backs • Morrison, Boardman, Swift, Jolley, Cook, Thomas, Edmondson and Naylor, forwards. Runcorn—Hough, back; Moran, Price, Butterworth Flannagan, three-quarter backs; Richardson and Jolley half-backs; Walker, Farmer, Moulton, Higgins, Tomlin- son, A. Richardson' Padbory, and Dixon, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Priestley, Salford. Touch judges, Messrs. F. Knowles (Swinton) and F. Little (St. Helens). Play opened in favour of Runcorn, Baterworth and Price making bold efforts to cross. The attack was beaten off, and after Fish had had a futile shot at goal the Cestrians took advantage of some faulty fielding by the home backs, and the Warrington goal was hotly assailed. Hallam cleared only to see the Warrington ' backs at fault, and Price racing away gave Moran an easy chance to open the scoring. Farmer landed a goal from the touch line. Warrington immediately retali- ated, and from a pack on the line Bate crossed, and Fish landing a goal ends were changed with the scores 5 points each. Turning round, Runcorn pressed at the start, but the hoyae forwards displaying a marked superiority the issue was never in doubt. F. om. some loose play! Dickenson ran finely through the Runcorn backs, and Fish placed the goal. Then a concerted dribble between Bate and Isherwood completely beat Hough, Isherwood scoring the try and Fish landing the goal. Following,a particu- larly fine offort by Hockenhall which failed under the bar, Isherwood notched his second try in the last minute of the game. Result :—Warrington 3 goals 4 tries (18 points), Runcorn 1 goal 1 try (5 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WIDNES v. HALIF.AX.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. HALIF.AX.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, before 3,000 spectators. Halifax won the toss, and the first incident of note was a fine round of passing by Joe Riley, Williams, and Hadwen, but just as the latter received the ball he was collared by Tilley. A strong forward movement brought play into the visitors 25," and Douglas getting possession passed to Hue es, who kicked across. Wailing appeared to have a splendid chance of scoring a try, but he overran the ball, and Little cleared to half-way. The Halifax full back made two attempts to place a goal, but each time the ball went outside. Just before the interval he had a drop at goal, and again failed, and half-time arrived with the score sheet blank.</p>
        <p>„ Early in the second half Tilley mulled badly, and after a lot of scrambling play in the Widnes "25” Douglas cleared their lines. After this Whines had the best of matters, and Halifax were kept on the de- fensive. J. Tilley had an unsuccessful kick at ...pal, and one of the home forwards just failed to ground the ball before it crossed the dead ball line. Just before the whistle sounded Little had his kick charged down when over his own line, i andlfortunatelyfor Halifax the ball went dead, and the game ended n a pointless draw. Teams :</p>
        <p>Widnes—J. Tilley, back ; Peacock:l. Taylor and Hughes, threequarter backs; Bennett and Douglas half backs; G. Aspey, S. Aspey, Lynch, Leyshon, Mc Donald, McCabe, Cockram, and Webster, forwards. Halifax—Little, back; Wedgewood, Joe Riley, Wil- liams and Hawden, threequarter backs; Morley and. Gledhill, half hacks; J. Riley, Balmer, Bartle, Swin- bank, Mallinson, Morton, Flockhard, and Langhorn forwards.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. E. Edmondson.'(Bradford), Touch judges Messrs. J. Lea (Leigh) and J. Bennett (Liverpool). !KEIMILEY v WIGAN.</p>
        <p>At Keighley,--1-;Jo-re-i,000 spectators, dull weather. Keighley pressed continuously in the first quarter' and Myers scored brilliantly, Walker converting. Then Wigan's play improved, and soon after the interval they pinned Keighley on the line. Davies got over, and also landed a goal, equalising the scores. The visiting forwards manifested superior poser towards the close, but neither side could score. Result:—Keighley, 1 goal 1 try (5 points); Wigan, 1 goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LEIGH v. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>LEIGH v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>At Leigh,in grand weather, before over 2,000 specta- tors. Leigh had most of the game, and were repeatedly swarming round the Salford line, but lacked scoring power. Salford broke away on a solitary occasion, Lomas intercepting a pass at the centre, and passing after a long run to Heath, who scored, Lomas placing the goal. Leigh experienced cruel luck. Result : Salford, 5 points; Leigh, nil. HULL v, HULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>At Hull, before 9000 spectators. Gordon placed a penalty goal for the Rovers, and this was the only score in a keen game up to half-time. In the second half Hull, who were playing arsuperior game, scored an unconverted try through Parry. Result—Hull, 1 try ; Rovers, 1 penalty goal. SWINTON v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>Played at Swintbn, before 6000 specbltors. Oldham had much the best of matters in the first half, but</p>
        <p>Spot tiswosode failed to take two splendid chances. Sam Lees, however, kicked a goal. for the visitors. After much pressure on the Oldham line, Wallwork scored a try for the" Lions," but Preston failed to land the goal. Result—Swinton, 1 try; Oldham, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>LEEDS V. BATLEY.</head>
        <p>LEEDS V. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>At Batley, before 5,000 spectators. In the first half Littlewood (Leeds) scored from a penalty kick, and Wattle Davies scored a clever try for Batley, but failed at goal. On resuming, Batley played up well, and Will Davies crossed his opponents' line. Wattle Davies also scored a try. Result—Batley, 3 tries (9 points); Leeds, 1 goal (2. points). .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>BROUGHT&amp; RANGERS v. BRADFORD.</head>
        <p>BROUGHT&amp; RANGERS v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>Played- at Broughton, in fine weather, before 6,000 people. Bradford pressed vigorously, and scored close to the interval through Dunbavin, the goal kick failing. In the second half Broughton Rangers showed improvement, but met with a strong defence. Wilson scored for the home side, the place kick failing. Even (play followed: Result :—Broughton Rangers, 1 try (3 points) ; Bradford, 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>HUNSLET v. HUDDERSFIELD</head>
        <p>HUNSLET v. HUDDERSFIELD</p>
        <p>Played at Hunslet, before 5,000 spectators. In the first half Everson played a-great game for Hunslet, and enabled Binks and W. Goldthorpe to score tries, Gold- thorpe improving. In the second half Hunslet held the upper hand, their forwards playing finely, and the half hacks easily beating the Huddersfield pair, but there was no more scoring. Result :—Hunslet, 1. goal 1 try (8 points) ; Huddersfield, nil.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner and Cheshire Free Church Councils.</p>
        <p>At Northwich on Monday night Sir John Brunner, M.P., met delegates from the Runcorn, Winsford, Middlewich, and Northwich Free Church Councils with reference to . the Education Act. Sir John de- clared himself to be in absolute accord with the councils. The delegates represented strongly that the Education Act must be the principal question at the next election. They passed a vote of confidence in Sir John Brunner.</p>
