      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Reiiiaey keen Wesleyan Church, ii Sneday afternoon tho open quarterly meeting in ethinebtiOn with the Men's Bible Class Of the above thniCh was held. There witB a li&amp;y guod uttelidance, presided over _bY Mr, George WeaVer. The vocalisl was Mrs. NV. T. Williams, who Bitng The Better Land" and " Tears "in a very creditable wanner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Baptist Church, Go!borne-street.</head>
        <p>Baptist Church, Go!borne-street.</p>
        <p>The subject of the address on Sunday evening was "Who is this Jesus " ? and the Pastor addressed his remarks particularly to those who believed in Christ as the highest and noblest example the world has everseen but who could not accept this divinity. The reason for this was that men had wrong idea as to what human- ity and 4ivinity really were. Humanity at its highest was divine, when a man was • most human he was then most divine, and God had revealed himself to men through the human yet divine Jesus. "In every man the Christ lies sleeping," tne preacher stated, and closed with an earnest appeal that men would let Christ awake and rule their hearts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Christmas Choral Service at Wycliffe Church.</head>
        <p>Christmas Choral Service at Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>A congregation of about one thousand people attended the Christmas choral service held in Wycliffe Congre- gation Church on Sunday evening. Selections from "The Messiah" were given by an augmented choir, and at intervals, the congregation joined in singing Christmas carols. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., con- ducted the singing and Mr. F. Hickman presided at the organ. The musical effect was heightened by the performance of a small but efficient orchestra, composed of the following instrumentalists i—ist violins, Mr. R. 11. Pearson, Mr. N. Brown and Miss Bouchier ; 2nd violins, Mr. A. Knowles and Mr. T. Wallington; viola, Mr. G. Johnson ; 'cello, Mr. H. Bond ; bass, Mr. R. Whitfield. The choir distinguished themselves in the choruses, their well-balanced singinr, being characterised with power and precision. The "Hallelujah" chorus and "-Glory to God" were especi- ally well sung, the singing of the first named being a virtual triumph for the choir. The solo singing did not reach quite so high a standard, although on the whole it was uniformly good. The soloists were Mrs. R. H. Pearson (soprano), Mrs. A. E. Longshaw and Miss A. Travis (contralto), Mr. A. Griffiths (tenor), and Mr. W. H. Wallington (baritone). Mrs. Pearson's sweet and flexible voice was heard to advantage in the recit " There were shepherds" and also in the difficult air" Rejoice greatly.' The ,recit ad air "Behold a virgin "and. "0 Thou that tellest," were sung with effect by Mrs. Longshaw and Miss A. Travis gave a • careful inter- pretation of the air "He was despised." Mr. A. Griffiths again sang with his usual ability the opening recit and air' Comfort ye" and "Every valley." Later in the evening he sang All they that see him." "Thy rebuke" and "But thou did'st not leave." In the last named air Mr. Griffiths was very successful. Mr. W. H. Wallington was in excellent voice and his interpre- tation of "Thus saith the Lord," "The people that walked" and "Why do the nations" was effective and pleasing. The accompaniments were played with taste and delicacy of execution, although at times the organ was a little too pronounced. Mention must also be made of the tuneful overture and the beautiful pastoral symphony, into which the instrumentalists imported a measure of soulfulness.— Tlie devotional part of the_ service was conducted in the unavoidable absence of the pastor, through indisposi- tion, by "Mr: George Wallington.—A collection was taken on behalf of the choir funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</head>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>Never in all the history of Primitive Methodism at Warrington has there been so successful a service as last Sunday's when the choir at Legh-st assisted by one or two friends rendered Handel's" Messiah." The church was entirely filled, there being scarcely room enough for the choir. The following was the programme.—Miss Alice Bridge the leading soprano of the choir rendered "There were shepherds," "How beautiful," "I know that my Redeemer " with great feeling, her splendid soprano voice being entirely suited to these exacting solos. Miss Alice Woodland sweetly rendered" Come unto Him." Mrs. Taylor, who possesses a rich contralto voice admirably rendered 0 Thou that tellese "He shall feed His flock" and "He was despised." Mr. John Corrin sang "Comfort ye," "Every Valley," "He was cut off "and "But thou did'st not leave " with magnificent effect, his pure tenor voice greatly pleasing every one present, and his splendid performanceigreatly.adding to the good reputa- tion he has already attained. Mr. E. H. Pearce, A.L.C.M.,the powerful bass;nobly rendered "Thus saith the Lord," "But who may abide" and " Why do the nations." This last solo was perhaps his best effort, which demands all the power and execution it is possible to give, and Mr. Pearce -without doubt did justice to this most difficult " item. The choir, under the painstaking, and capable conductor, Mr. Albert Spruce, rendered the choruses "And the glory" "0 thou that tallest" "For unto us " "Glory to God" "Behold the Lamb. " "All we like sheep" "Worthy is the Lamb' and " Hallelujah " with fine attack and precision, keeping a steady tempo through- out, and excelling any previous performance. Mr. Fred Podmore 'presided at the organ and accompanied all the solos and .choruses, besides playing the "Introduction" and "Pastoral Symphony," without the aid of an orchestra. , The Rev. J. Gardhara Reed presided and proposed a vote of thanks to the conductor, organist, choir and friends who had so kindly helped to make the service so successful. The collections were far in advance of last year's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>TERRIBLE CHESHIRE TRA.GEDY</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE CHESHIRE TRA.GEDY</p>
        <p>A tragic affair occurred at Neston, near Birkenhead, on Tuesday, two deaths ensuing. It seems that a fort- night ago two young men, Henry Pritchard, joiner, and Ernest Grundy, a bricklayer, both members of a local political club, had a quarrel which ended in a fight, Pritchard being rather seriously injured. Htkhad to take to bed, mid on Tuesday he suddenly got worse and died early in the evening. Upon the news of his death reaching Grundy. he became terribly alarmed, and art hour later cut his throat in a horrible manner with a razor, the head being almost severed from the body.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>808 PHARAZIWS :MADNESS</head>
        <p>808 PHARAZIWS :MADNESS</p>
        <p>13y r.ssraid Biss, Author of " The Imposter, etc.</p>
        <p>it was a few days ba4ore lass Christmas and I had been to the theatre. The piece was a thrilling melo- drama on orthodox lines, with a lovely and virtuous heroine and an unspeakable and unreal villain. The hero, fair-haired and booted in patent leather, was a perfect prodigy but a parlous prig, and it had rather amused than interested me. It was not a play that I should have chosen from preference, but when you go to dine with people you cannot be arbiter of where they will take you afterwards. However, I had dined well and was in a good temper. The false ring of the pathos, the energy of the actors, and their histronic conception of love- making had all amused me, and I had watched the realistic struggle between hero and villain, the fight to death for the. sake of the heroine, which was drawing all the towu, with a certain brutal interest. One could not help feeling how unreal it was; but it was a survival of that human force that turned the thumbs of the Roman ladies out- wards in the Colosseum, a victory of primitive instinct over the proprieties of civilisation. It was unquestionably very well done, and one could not restrain a feeling of triumph, as the hero success- fully laid out his vile opponent amidst the plaudits of the "gods." Then they both came before the curtain, a little dishevelled, but obviously Veased with themselves, and amicably inclined towards each other as they bowed their acknowledgments, and it spoilt it all. But a philosopher must not carp at the vagaries of melodrama. Outside the theatre it was a glorious frosty night. I said good-bye to my friends at the entrance and left them homeward bound. Personally I never feel inclined to go straight off to bed after the theatre, and had decided in my own mind to stroll round to the club for an hour for a chat Or a game at bridge. I made my way through the jostling crowd down the Strand in the direction of Piccadilly, glad to stretch my legs after three cramped hours in a narrow stall. . The air was sharp but pleasant, and as I puffed at my cigar I remember I recapitulated the incidents of the preposterous play, laughing over them to myself. I realised, however, in exten• nation, that in real life it is most difficult not to dc things melodramatically, but I asked myself seriously whether it was necessary to make love ith such an impassioned thrill, or fight a man with such supreme fury. I hadn't had as much experience then as I have now—but that is looking ahead.</p>
        <p>I found two or three of my intimates in the smoking room of the club. "Hallo, here's Reggie Colgate," called out one, Tony Braithwaite by name, breaking off in the middle of a sentence. He was standing in his favourite attitude on the hearth rug, back to the fire. " Whera have you come from?" "The theatre," I answered laconically.</p>
        <p>"Which one?" he asked, pursuing the question, "Blake and I have just come from the Frivolity— ripping show—plenty of music and fun, and such girls t" "Oh, I've been to the Adelphic," I admitted a little shamefacedly.</p>
        <p>A roar of laughter went:up from the others. " What ? To see A Fight to Death, the most realistic melodrama of modern times ' ! Why, Reggie, we didn't think that was much in youl line."</p>
        <p>"Here's Billy Finnigan," exclaimed Blake. "Hallo, Billy ! " Finnigan nodded. "Hallo, how are you all? You look pretty jolly." "And you look pretty glthn, Billy, old chap. Your face is as long as a parson's in Lent." "So yours would be," answered the new arrival shortly, "if you had just heard what I have." "What's that ? " we asked all together, a little anxiously. "Only that poor old Bob Pharazin ha A gone ofl his head, and they're thinking of p 44-ki,ng him under restraint."</p>
        <p>"Great Scott," ejaculated Tony, solemn for once</p>
        <p>Personally I felt stunned ai the suddenness of the announcement. Bob Pharazin was my greatest friend, and we had been pals over since our Eton days. As to Maimie, Bob's sister, well, I can't express myself adequately, but you will understand before the end of the story.</p>
        <p>"When did you hear the news ? " I asked in a dull monotone.</p>
        <p>"An hour ago, at supper," answered Finnigan, "that's all. I heard it from a chap who had been staying down in his neighbourhood; the doctor there told him. He has been very strange-lately, and they are afraid he may turn dangerous from one or two of the symptoms. So if he gets any worse they fear they'll have to certify him and shut him up. It seems to have come on quite sud- denly, but his father .or his uncle went that way some years ago." "His uncle," I put in. "But I can't believe it of Bob."</p>
        <p>"I wish to goodness it weren't true, I can assure you," rejoined Finnigan, but Bolitho was very positive:' "I am hooked to stay with him for Christmas," I continued, a trifle inconsequently. "Poor dear old Bob, it can't be true of him of all people! you chaps, if you don't mind I'll clear off. Finni- gan's news has been bit of a knock-out for me." And I wont off without another word, nodding curtly to the others.</p>
        <p>As'l drove home I felt as though a great blow between the eyes had stunned all my faculties, and I could hardly think.</p>
        <p>I was due Co go down to Bob's place the next day so as to be a day ahead of the other Christmas guests, and I wns puzzled as to my course of action. " Poor dear old Bob," I murmured miserably to myself: " Poor dear little Maimie, what an awful shook it will be to her! It will blight her whole life, and perhaps break her heart." I knew how she adored Bob, and secretly I had always been a-little jealous of .it, looking upon it as a misdirected affection.</p>
        <p>What ought I to do under the circumstances, I asked myself. If I did not go down from misguided' feelings of good taste, I might be leaving Maimie in the lurch when she needed the help of a real friend and someone to look after her.</p>
        <p>Bob and Maimie, I must explain, were orphans and as he was a bachelor, she kept house for him. So, unless I had a letter from him in the morn- ing, I decided that it was my duty, as the greatest friend of both of them, to go down as though I had heard' nothing, and see if I conld be of any help. At any rate I could watch him closely, and make up my mind as to his sanity. As I had arranged to catch a morning train to be down in time for dinner dinner—the Pharazins lived in a corner of Devonshire—on reaching my chambers I went straight off to bed; but, full of anxiety, I tossed about restlessly for some time before I fell asleep, thinking of Bob and Maimie. Bob himself met me at the station with his favourite fast trotter and high dog-cart. "Hallo," he cried shortly, as I emerged from the station. _</p>
        <p>"Hallo Bob, old man," I replied cheerfully, though a little taken aback at his abruptness. "How are you, and how is Mamie ? " "Hop up," was his only answer, and I climbed into the high cart. Hardly was I seated before he gave the horse a sharp cut with the.whip across its quarters. It started off with a bound like an arrow out of a bow, and nearly unshipped me by the u.nexpectednesa of the movement.</p>
        <p>"Whoa. a moment, old chap,),' I exclaimed, when I had got over the shock, "my luggage isn't on board."</p>
        <p>"That doesn't matter," he answered, "it will do later."</p>
        <p>"But I shah want it to dress for dinner," I objected, "and it *ill mean sending ten miles specially for it."</p>
        <p>" I piefer to make my own arrangements," he replied curtly, giving the horse another unnecess- ary cut with the whip. fob always kept good cattle and drove fast, but never furiously and recklessly, and I began to realise that there certainly was something strange about him. His abrupt, almost discourteous manner was and instead of his</p>
        <p>usmai now or conversabw., turnity was very marked. However, I decided tNaold tight, trust in Provi- dence, and take no notice of it.</p>
        <p>" Who else are coming down, Bob ? " I asked, determinedly making conversation. "No cne,;' he said tersely.</p>
        <p>"Not a very big Christnias party," I remarked with a light laUgh, which was decidedly forced, I must admit. ,</p>
        <p>"Well, if ydti are not satisfied," he replied rather hastily, "you can go off again." I began, to feel that something was seriously wrong. "Don't be absurd) my dear chap," I said, making an effort to show no surprise and chagrin at hig demeanour. "I feel highly honoured and delighted to have Maimie and you all to myself." "Humph," was all he vouchsafea, and conver- sation again flagged. We were thundering down a very steep hill at a most unsafe pace,and suddenly the horse stumbled slightly on a small stone in the road. "Hold up, curse you," roared Bob, lashing it furiously across the bead with his whip, and on we went, fastar than ever. How we got round the corner at the bottom, I never knew. We were certainly only on one wheel, and only on a part 9f that. The swerve nearly swung me out rthe trap. "Steady a bit, old chap," I remonstrated in a soothing voice, now thoroughly awake to the danger of the situation. "You nearly had me out then." Bob shrugged his shoulders contemptuously. "If you've lost your nerve, you'd better get out and walk," was all his reply, followed by another almighty cut with the whip. After this, conversation ceased finally, and I held on more tightly than ever, my heart in my mouth. However, we reached the house safely, and Bob drew his steaming horse to a standstill with a sud- den jerk that nearly brought it down. "The brute is losing its pace," he remarked, as he jumped out of the trap and entered the hall, regardless of me. Maiteie, dear little Maimie (but she won't pass the proofs if I say any more, as she hates senti- ment—at least in public), met us in the hall. "That idiot Reggie's in a fluster," rapped out Bob in a rough voice, "because he won't be able to deck himself out for dinner, but I told him I wouldn't have the trap turned into a luggage cart." Maimie looked at him, obviously anxious, and then at me.</p>
        <p>"I know you will excuse me," I said with over-as- sumed geniality, taking her hand and holding it a second longer than etiquette demanded, "but, of course, Bob was quite right. It would have been absurd to have lumbered the dog cart up with boxes and bags, and knocked half the paint off. Maimie smiled gratefully as she extricated her hand gently but firmly. . "No, it wasn't, it was idiotic;" broke in Bob. "If you had had any sense, you would have made a point of it, and, as it is, you'll have to wait till to-morrow for your clothes, as I'm hanged if I'm going to send one of my horses ten miles on a win- ter's night to fetch a bag. Dinner was a strange meal, and I found it very difficult to keep my end up. I felt like breaking out into a wild, hysterical fit of laughter at Bob's extraordinary behaviour, and then I felt inclined to cry over the tragedy before my eyes. Altogether I felt frightfully overstrung. I noticed Maimie (my eyes would wander in her direction) watching Bob curiously, and from time to time a look of deep sorrow and anxiety passed over her sweet face.</p>
        <p>Several times 800 passed sarcastic comments Oi my dress, and he would insist upon constantly changing his place, and.made us do likewise. We humoured him, and I tried to pass the situation off by a laughing reference to the tea party in "Alice in Wonderland," but the remark seemed to annoy Bob considerable.</p>
        <p>"The Mad Hatter and the March Hare were there,' he said, hitting the table with his fist and making the glasses jump. "No one is mad here, unless you are t "</p>
        <p>Awl he looked round 'pugnaciously for someone to challenge the assertion. Then he put a large spoonful of salt into his claret and drank it off with evident relish. Somehow I did not feel hungry, and food seemed distasteful to me.</p>
        <p>After dinner he insisted upon leaving Maimie alone over her wine, while we adjourned to the drawing-room. - - - - -</p>
        <p>She 'joined us as soon as she dared, and a few minutes later Bob rushed off to play himself a match at billiards. _</p>
        <p>- "I play 'spot' against 'plain' for a fiver," he told us solemnly, and anybody else in the room disturbs me.' - .</p>
        <p>---- --- For a moment we sat silent, neither liking to begin. Then I spoke. " Maimie, what's wrong?" " N-n-nothing," she answered with a break in her voice, turning her head away. "Nothing, Maimie?" I said gently. "Do you think I'm blind ? "</p>
        <p>"It's n-n-nothing, only fun," she replied, and then she began to cry. It was too much for me. In a minute I was kneeling by her Side, holding-her hand. "Oh, I know, Mahnie, Mamie dearest," I said in a hoarse voice, "I heard it in town: Poor oldßob I 0, Maimie, I love you, I worship you. Won't you give M 3 the right to protect you, to help you ? " Even at that critical moment I was painfully aware that I was plagiarising the hero of the Adelphic piece, word for word, but it did not seem melodramatic then. I suppose it's because life is so fearfully melodramatic and crude. I don't remember exactly what she said. It was very confused, but very pleasant, and I almost for- got Bob and the urgency of the situation, as I took her in my arms, and kissed her again and again, just like the Adelphic paragon. Anyhow the next half hour seemed a very sweet one, as she sobbed out her sorrow in my arms, and I did my hest to comfort her, both by word and deed.</p>
        <p>Suddenly we heard a footfall, and separated by instinct.</p>
        <p>"Don't—don't say a word to Bob to-night," murmured Alaimie, as the door opened, " he— ho— oh, he seems so queer, he might resent it." Bob, however, seemed to be in the best of tempers.</p>
        <p>" 7 Spot' won by seven," he exclaimed radliantly. "It was a good game, but I had backed 'spot' all through." And for the rest of the evening he was in very good spirits, till Maimie went off to bed; but he talked very inconsequently. When Maimie said good-night, he dragged me off to his sanctum.</p>
        <p>He gave me a cigar and began to pour out drinks. "1 drink whisky and brandy mixed now," he volunteered, 'taking the •stoppers out of both decanters, "I find it, more soothing and most •palatable, won't you try it ? " t" I think I'll have whisky alone, old chap, hanks, " I answered, laughing. "You'll have what you are given," be rejoined, suddenly turning nasty. In an instant all his geniality seemed to have evaporated. took his unpleasant mixture to humour him, and then began : "Bob, old man, I want to speak to you seriously a moment." He smiled sardonically.</p>
        <p>"It's—well, it's about Main:tie," I went on, naturally awkward.</p>
        <p>His eyebrows met in a frown. "0 yes," he said slowly in a voice that rasped my nerves, "1 know what yOU are up to, you two, but you won't get the bett,q- of me. You want to deprive me of llairaie—you want to leave me in the lurch."</p>
        <p>"My dear Bob," I protested feebly. He ttn.nel on me, blaztug up— _</p>
        <p>"Who the devil are yo'U, iir, that yoz . should steal my sister's heart? I did not as you down for that purpose; you have abused my hospitality most disgro.eeflifly." "But," I began . . .</p>
        <p>"Shut up," roared Bob at me furiously, bitting the table violently with his clenched fist. Then suddenly his passion seemed to pass: be spoke slowly and deliberately in a cold, measured voice:</p>
        <p>, " Do—you—know—why—l—did—ask —you— down ---here,—Reginald7-Colgate ? " he asked,' pausing impressively between each word. I kept silence, fearing to infuriate him. "To—kill—you," he went on coolly, that's why, I saw what-was growing up between Maimie and you, and it seemed to me the best way to stop it— forever," he concluded, eyeing me-calmly.</p>
        <p>, For the first time I realised that I was alone with a dangerous and determined homicidal lunatic, my wits pitted against his. So I decided to temporise. _</p>
        <p>naughed—l must admit it was an effort—and he laughed too. "It's a funny end to our old friendship, isn't it, Bob?" I said, as I turned the</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Thursday December 24, 1903.</p>
        <p>" vour Li Ut, Illy &lt;i on: N-eggle, lie</p>
        <p>in a most rnuter-oi-fact v.)ice</p>
        <p>- • "Can't you g:ve me a few days' grace to put my affairs in order?" I hamirded. " I can't ;trust you to come back again," he said. "I could have done S o once," he added regretfully; "besides, I've got to die to-night; I'm afraid of Christmas, it gets on my nerves." "How do you propose to dispose of us both?" I asked, rising from my chair so as to ba prepared in case of a sudden assault.</p>
        <p>"You first, and then myself," he answered grimly. He too, had risen, and stepping back- wards, and keeping his face towards me, he locked the door and withdrew the key. I began to feel most uncomfortable; he was so frightfully business-like. Then he went to a cupboard in the corner, opened the door, and took some things that clanked off a shelf. Thep he placed on the table— s revolver, a long knife, and a bottle. It was just like the melodrama at the Adelphic. In every detail Bob had copied the big scene in the play. "Bob" I asked, "when did you see Fight to Death '? " I had no idea that he had been in town since the start of the piece. He took no notice of my question, but continued his preparations. "This," he said, touching the bottle lightly, "contains prussic acid. I give you choice of weapons. Personally I recommend the poison. It is the one lam going to use myself. It is quick, sure, and makes no mess for the se,vants to clear up." It was all so stage-like and seemed perfectly familiar to me. Then an idea struck me. How had the Adelphic hero got out of the tame fix? Suddenly it occurred to me.</p>
        <p>Putting one hand on the edge of the table, 1 vaulted over lightly, sprang at him, and in a moment we were grappling with each Locked in each other's aims, we rolled over on the floor, struggling furiously and ii•ll!ing to death. Little by little our grip on cocii h l.'s throats tightened, and every second seeined a c-ntury. As we fought, thump—r-thump—thn:i..p came at the door, and I heard Maimie's voce crying .</p>
        <p>" Help ! Help! Bob, you shan't hurt Reggie I " There was a choking sensation i a my throat, and a black mist seemed to rise before my eyes. . .</p>
        <p>Thump—thump— thump again, and a last desperate eflort—f4 Maimie's sake.</p>
        <p>„ "Seven o'clock sir,” said my man in his well. regulated voice. " Your tea and letters. I could' nt make you hear though I knocked very loud, sir; so I came in, as I knew you wanted to catch a train. As I entered the door, sir, you yelled help! help! and something else I did'nt catch, and nearly fell out of bed."</p>
        <p>Gradually I came to a realisation of myself and my surroundings. My bed was soaked with sweat, and I was shaking all over. On top of my letters was one in Bob Pharazin's well-known hand. I tore it open. "Dear old man," it ran, "I will meet you as arranged, at 6.10; We shall be having a very quiet Xmas, but hope you won't mind. My poor cousin, Bob Pharazin—(same name, familiar enough)— has just gone off his head, and was sent to an asylum yesterday. • His father, my uncle, you will remember, was subject to similar fits. Maimie is looking forward to your visit. Yours, Bob." "It must have been that Welsh rarebit;" I muttered, asthe world came into focus; but I shall never forget the unpleasant impression of that night.</p>
