      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ALLEGED ELECTION</head>
        <p>ALLEGED ELECTION</p>
        <p>BRIBERY,</p>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A BEWSEY WARD</p>
        <p>CONTEST.</p>
        <p>Councillor S. Gough Committed to the</p>
        <p>Assizes.</p>
        <p>HEARiNG YESTERDAY-REIVIARKII3LE</p>
        <p>EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Samuel Gough appeared before the magistrates at the Warrington Police Court yesterday to answer to four charges of corrupt practices at the recent by-election in Bewsey Ward. The case had aroused.a great deal of interest throughout the town and there was a large attendance of the public. The magistrates on the bench at the outset were :—Mr.F. Monks (presiding), Aldermen Fairclough, Smethurst, and Hutchinson, Councillor Thornton, and Mr. Birchall ; but only Mr. Monks and Mr. Birch- all adjudicated. The hearing commenced about 10.15 and continued uninterruptedly until 1.50. There was then an hour's adjourment for lunch and after- wards the case was resumed. It was nearly five o'clock when the magistrates had heard all the evidence and decided that, Councillor Gough must go for trial at Liverpool Assizes. Some remarkable evidence was submitted but at times there were very humorous Pa • ssages which convulsed the Court. The prosecutor was Mr. Ernest A. Sanders, 12, Arthur-street, and he was represented by Mr. Rigby Swift, barrister (in- structed by 3.ilessrs. Arthur Browne and Crompton). Mr. F. E. Smith, barrister (instructed by Messrs. ;Spencer Wallhead and Son) defended. COUNSEL'S OPENING STATEMENT. :Mr. Swift, in opening the case, said he appeared in .support of the information which charged Samuel Gough with corrupt practices at a municipal election in Warrington. He proposed to lay before the court evidence of four specific instances of bribery and if a prima facie case was, in the view of the Bench, made out, he should ask them to commit the defendant for trial to the next LiverpoOl Assizes to be tried on indictment for the offence. There were in relation to Parliamentary and municipal elections three classes of offences which might be committed by candidatas, their agents, or persons who were interested in the election. These three classes were called corrupt practices,illegal practices, or illegal payments, and in the present case the Bench would have to direct their attention merely to the first class—corrupt practices. It was not,at that stage, alleged against the defendant that he had been guilty either of illegal practices or illegal 'payments. Mr. Swift proceeded to explain the law on the 'subject and first directed attention to the Cor- Tupt PracticeslPrevention Act of 1854 under section two of which it was alleged, defendant was liable. By -section three of the Corrupt and Illegal Practices Act of 1883 it was further provided that a person who com- mitted any corrupt practice other than personation, or aiding and abetting 6:c., should be guilty of a mis- demeanour and on conviction should be liable to be im- -prisoned, with or without hard labour, for a term not exceeding one year or to be fined in a ,sum not exceeding £2OO. Under that section, if a prima facie case were made out, defendant must be oommitted for trial on indictment because the only method of dealing with offences of bribery was on indictment. The acts he had quoted from applied only to Parliamentary elections bat by section two of the Municipal Elections Act of 1884 it was provided that a person who committed any corrupt practice in reference to a municipal election should be guilty of a like offence and should, On conviction, be liable to a like punishment as if the corrupt practice .kad been committed with reference to a Parliamentary election. Alluding to the allegations against M. -Gough, counsel wird that on November 30th a by-election was being held in Bewsey Ward for the purpose of electing a representative in the Town Council. It was alleged against the defendant that in four instances he was guilty of the offence of bribery within the meaning! ol the word as defined by the Act of 1854, the specific ! charges being that to one person he offered the sum of 1 sixpence. and to two other persons he gave sixpence before they had voted, and to a fourth person he gave sixpence after she had voted. On November 28th defendant, who was a candidate, -vent to the house of a man named William Rogers. Some conversation took place and Mr. Gough invited Rogers to give him his vote. When the time. came for parting, defendant I shook hands with Rogers and left in his right hand the - sum of sixpence. On the same day defendant called upon a woman named Mary Ward but she declinedto vote for him, statine' that she was going to vote for the other 'candidate. Defendant offered her sixpence, but she ' threw the coin on the table saying that she was not to s3s bought, and she would have nothing to do with it. Whether a person received or accepted the bribes Made -no difference whatever in law. The *offence was offer- ing the money. On the morning of the election a man named I Foxall went to Mr. Gough's committee room. Defendant shook hands with him andafterwards Foxall found sixpence in the palm of the hand which had been shaken. In regard to the charge of giving money to a person who had voted, it was alleged that near the polling booth defendant saw three women together, and one of them, named Jane Kirkham, was an illiterate. While Mr. Gough was in the polling booth Kirkham voted as an illiterate. When she came outside she and her friends had got a little way from the booth when defendant called them back again. He put his hand under his coat as if he was getting something from his pocket, and gave Kirkham two threepenny pieces, telling her to go and get a drink. These were the whole of the circumstances he proposed to lay before the Court. If the witnesses proved the facts he ventured to submit that there was an unanswerable case for inquiry into before a jury. Of course, if his friend Mr. Smith was able to so shake the witnesses as to convince the Bench that no jury eould place any reliance on their testimony, and failed to make out a prima facie case the charge would be dis- missed. If, on the other hand, the witnesses so told their stories as to carrx to the minds of the Bench the idea not necessarily that the defendant was guilty, but that there was a charge to be inquired into, he asked them to send the case for trial. Before the evidence was heard, the Chairman remarked that the system of writing down the evidence in longhand was as antiquated as the law. He won- dered that the barristers did not get it altered. Mr. Swift: Your worships have the remedy in your hands. If you complained something might be done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>THE EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stanley Wilson, deputy Town Clerk, produced three nomination papers lodged at the Town Hall on behalf of Samuel Gough on November 23rd, to fill a casual vacancy in Bewsey Ward; also the burgess roll for the ward, together with a certificate of the result of the poll. The burgess roll showed that William Rogers, 8, Refuge-square, Henry Foxall. 6, Refuge-square,Mary Ward, 3, Refuge-square, and Jane Kirkham, 92, Catherine- street, were all voters in the Ward. The certificate showed that Samuel Gough was the elected candidate at the election. ALL ABOUT SIXPENCE. _ _ -</p>
        <p>William Rogers, iron worker, 8, Refuge-square, said he was a voter in Bewsey Ward. On Saturday night, November 28th, Mr. Gough called at his house about G. 30. He was accompanied by William Pearce and another gentleman. Witness answered the door and Mr. Gough said "Rogers, I have come to solicit your vote." Witness replied "You will not get it sir.' Gough said "Why not?" and witness answeted "I have promised my vote faithfully to Mr. Owen- Toole." Mr. Gough said "Then we shall be none th,. worse friends for that." He then said "I will wish you good night" and placed sixpence in witness's hand as they shook hands. By Mr. Smith: The conversation took place at the door in the presence of the other two gentlemen. Witness told defendant straight that he was not going to vote for him. He told him more than once that he had promised Mr. Toole. Mr. Gough must have had the sixpence in his hand, but the first timeowitness saw it was after they had shaken hands. He was not ex.. pecting sixpence but he did not let the c 3in dropout of his hands. Mr. Smith: Your hand must have been open. Witness: I shut my hand as soon as I felt the six- pence. (Laughter.) Mr. Swift: Did you know you were doing a wrong thing in taking sixpence? Witness: I didn't know anything about it. Mr. Smith : Did you think it was a wrong thing to take it? Witness: No. I did not. Further questioned, witness said he did not thank Gough at the time for the sixpence. ' Mr. Smith : Why? Witness: "I was lost of manners seemingly." Mr. Smith : suggest that the reason why you did not thank him was that you did not get sixpence at all and that this is a false story. Witness : Itold a man in Lilford-street about it and "he put it all about therplace." Mr. Smith: When were you first asked about this question of any money on the Liberal side? Witness: I was summoned to appear last weeks; I was never asked about it until I got the summons. Mr. Smith: But did not somebody from the solici- tors see you about it ? Witness: I suppose he must have done. Mr. Smith: When was that? Witness : I think it was a week last Tuesday. Mr. Smith : Do you know Mr. Walsh?</p>
        <p>Witness : Yes. Mr. Smith : Have you had any free drink there lately? Witness : I have never had a free drink there. I have to pay for what I have. Mr. Smith: Have you been there lately? Witness : Yes.l had a glass of beer there yesterday.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith: Were you given a drink there Mt nothing if yot! would come and speak about this six. pence? Witness : No, sir. Mr. Smith: Will you swear? Witness : Yes, sir. • A WITNESS'S FRANK CONFESSIONS, _</p>
        <p>Henry Foxall, ironworker, residing at 6, Refuge- square, said he was a voter in Bewsey Ward. On the morning of the 30th November he went to Mr. Gough's committee room about 8 o'clock. It was the first time.he had voted, and he threw his card on the table. He had one hand on the table and the other in his pocket. Mr. Gough put his hand in his pocket and shoved" a sixpence in witness's hand. He then took him to the polling booth and said "Don't forget who you vote for." Witness said "All right. I will make it right." Cross-examined: Up to the time he went to the polling booth he had no intention of voting for Mr. Gough. He told his wife he was going to vote for Mr. Toole. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith: Why- did you go into Mr. Gough's com- mittee room at all? _</p>
        <p>_ Witness: Because they kept on "humbugging" me. Mr. Smith: I put it to you that it is false say Mr. Gough was in tir committee room at the time? Witness: That is untrue.</p>
        <p>Further cross-examined, witness said he saw Mr. Gough in the first room before he went to the chapel where he had to put his cross. Mr. Goughaccompauied him. • . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith: How did he give you this sixpence? Witness: I "seed" him heave his jacket up and put his hand in his pocket. He then "shoved" his hand against mine with sixpence in it. Mr. Smith: If anybody had been looking at him they could see what was taking place? Witness: When they turn their heads aside they cannot see. (Laughter). Further cross-examined, witness said he did not know what Mr. Gough was putting his hands in his pockets for. One clerk went to look at a paper on the wall and the other looked down at the books. Witness denied that a man nanied Baker pointed out Mr. Gough to him at the polling station when he went to vote. Questioned as to when he first spoke about receiving sixpence, witness said the men at the works got talking about it. They asked him" who he had voted for." He replied: "I voted for Mr. Gough." They then said "I. reckon you had a tanner." Witness then said: " Yes."</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Smith : Whom were you speaking to? Witness: The bar dragger for one. Mr. Smith: Where did you make a statement that was taken down in writing? Witness: In the Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith closely questioned witness as to who took him there. At first he stated that he was having a drink there, when two gentlemen joined him, but after- wards stated that he first met the gentlemen in the Im- perial Hotel and afterwards proceeded with thein to the Liberal Club. Walsh paid for drinks. One of the' gentlemen was Mr. Owen Toole. He did not know him at the time. He was not a member of the Liberal Club. Hehad had drinks paid for by Walsh before, but he did not see him a week before the 'election. He had only had two drinks with Mr. Walsh since the election. He had seen Mr. Toole three times since the election. Mr: Smith: Did you go to Cairo-street last Satur- day night? Witness: Yes, they thought they had got me on the hop. They thought of getting me into a "nice state." Mr. Smith: Did you go sober? Witness : No. Mr. Smith: Are you generally drunk on Saturday night? NVitness : If I wasn't drunk I had had enough any- way but it was at somebody else's expense. I reckon it was at Mr. Gougtt's expense. Mr. Smith: Who paid for the drinks? Witness: Mr. Baker. I was promised a good feed. but I didn't get it. (Laughter.) Mr. Smith; Who promised you that? Witness : Mr. Baker. Mr. Smith: Did you say in the presence of several people that this story of the sixpence was a lie ? Witness: Yes .1 did say so. I told them I did'nt have a sixpence and that I did not know Mr. Gough. Mr. Smith: Do you go about talking these lies? Witness: They thought they had got me on.the hop do you see. __ .</p>
        <p>Smith: Have you any explanation as to why you lie? Witness' enterei into a long statement in which he stated that Mr. Baker was at his house three times last week. On Saturday, at noon he had an idea that Mr. Baker was waiting at Bewsey bridge. .Mr. Baker after- wards came down to the house and as witness was going out Baker asked him if he was going to have"lush."They visited several public houses, including the" Ram's Nut', as they called it. They always went into these public houses by the front door but left by the back. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith contended that this ;vas not an explan- ation. If the witness knew what he was doing and saving he submitted it was no explanation. -Mr. Swift urged that the witness was entitled to say why he "lied" to Baker. His explanation was that Baker had taken him round to public-houses until he was intoxicatedand at last he told Baker a lie. The Chairman said the explanation witness was mak- ing was to the effect that Baker gave him "lush" with the view of getting him to sign some paper. Mr. Smith: How many pnblic-houses did you go to? Witness: It is hard to tell. I don't know all of them. I don't take notice of them all.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith: Then you said it wasn't true in order to get a little more drink. Witness: Yes. It was atker who put me up to it. . . .</p>
        <p>Re-examined by Mr. Swift, witneasaid when he was asked to signs. paper he told them he would chuck the sponge up. This was outside a lawyer's office. A WIDOW'S TETE A TETE.</p>
        <p>Jane Kirkham, widow, 92 Catherine-street, a voter in Bewsey Ward said she went to the polling booth on November 30th and gave her vote. Afterwards he received two three-penny pieces from Mr. Gough. He put them in her hand when she was near the Imperial Hotel. She had no money with her when she left home. Witness created considerable ammement by her vigor- ous retorts to Mr. Smith's cross-examination. She repeatedly declared that she spent the money, and indignantly told her questioner that she would "never come to the court again." Asked if she told P.C. Hayes that defendant had not given her any money, witness said that if she did she must have been drunk or frightened. Later in the day she was taken by a police sergeant to Mr. Reardon's house. Mr. Smith: There was only the smgaint and your- self there? Witness: Yes, Mr. Smith: It was quite a tete-a-t6te. Witness: Yes. That was all I had. (Loud laughter.) . . _ .</p>
        <p>Witness denied that she or the sargearit had anithini to drink at Reardon's. She had some refreshment there. Whilst in the box she repeatedly sighed and asked counsel "How long will you be before you have done with me, and let Me leave this box." Further questioned she said the sergeant afterwards took her to Mr. Smet- hurst and she definitely said she had received the money from the defendant. When Mr. Swift rose to re-examine the witness she exclaimed indignantly "Now, what have you got to say ?" She admitted signing a paper in the presence of Mr. Smethurst, declaring that she had received money from the defendant in consideration of having regis- tered her vote for him. WOULD NOT SELL HER VOTE.</p>
        <p>Mary Ward, widow, Refuge-square, and a voter in Ilswsey Ward, stated that on Saturday, November "28th, Mr. Gough and Mr.. Pmrs came to her house. Defend- ant asked her for her vote and she said "No. I'll vote for Mr. Toole." Defendant then put sixpence in her hand. Witness told him t,o "clear off " ; she wns net going to sell her vote for sixpence. Silo returnA the coin to him.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Monoghan said be served the summons on the defendant, who made no reply. Mr. Swift intimated that he would call other wit- nesses in regard to the alleged bribery, but he submit- ted that, subject to his friend being able to displace the evidence, he had made a prima fade case. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Sniitit said the witness Kirkham stated that a sergeant accompanied her to Mr. Reardon's and Mr. Smethurst's. He was told that was not so, and he should like to have further evidence on tho point. Mr. Swift laid it was not a police officer who accom- panied the woman. Alderman Smethurst pointed out that the paper was not signed at his house. Kirkham called several times while he was out and .he witnessed the signing of the paper at another house. . This closed the case for the prosecution. MR. SMITH'S CRITICISNI OF THE EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith said he would confidently submit that there was no prima facie case to answer, and there was certainly no case which the Bench would be justified, on such trumpery matters and obviously false evidence. in sending to the Assizes. It was not often that zeal for party in municipal elections took the form of bribery and he thought they would all be agreed that if there had been any bribery, corrupt 'practices, or treat- ing in this election, it was extremely undesirable and ought to be put down. He agreed that the smallness of the amount involved did not make any difference. He asked the magistrates to draw the inference that when a party which had been defeated in an election—as, he understood, the Liberal party was in this case—had the option of adopting the ordinary course and bringing in an election petition, and instead of doing that chose to , comeltolthe police court and bring three or four charges buttressed by the evidence of witnesses of the class brought forward that day, it bore an unfortunate resem- blance to the ebullition at least, of political malice and spite of the lowest form. He thought the Bench would listen with the greatest possible respect to the evidence of Mr. Gough, who, fortunately, on almost every:occa- sion had corroboration. The witness Rogers had sworn that he at once told Gough that he was going to vote for his, opponent. It was improbable that a:rnan would be so foolish as to fling sixpences about in a case like that. There was no plausibility in the story and he submitted that it was a deliberate fabrication. He thought he was not putting it too high when he said that the inherent probabilities of the situation were all against the story of Rogers which required the most careful scrutiny at thehands of the Bench. Rogers had stated that he was not expecting to receive sixpence and he (Mr. Smith) contended that in that case the coin Iwould have fallen to the gnarled. Defendant would state that he never put a copper in the man's hand, or even</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Examiner, Thursday December 24, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Thursday December 24, 1903.</p>
        <p>tried to do so. Then he (Mr. Smith) was inclined to think that if the man had received the money unex- pectedly he would have thanked Mr. Gough for it. The witnesses who accompanied Mr. Gough were positive that the defendant never gave him a farthing and that the man never said "Thank you." In regard to Foxall's evid- ence, he asked the Bench to say that not a single word be had saidlwas deserving of the slightest credenceEvi- deuce would be called to show that when Foxall was taken to the commitee room Mr. Gough was not there and Mr. Baker never gave the man a farthing. Out- side the committee room Baker pointed out Gough to Foxall and said "There's our man. That's Gough, you are going to vote for." There was the strongest reason for supposing that Foxall had been made the tool of men who were bringing the proceedings from political malice because they were beaten at the polls, ; He would prove that the defeated candidate himself had been to see the defendant more than once since the election and had tried to induce him to resign his seat in order to avoid proceedings. When he found he could not persuade Mr. Gough ta do so because he had done nothing wrong, he resorted to the unworthy machinery of the threat of police court pro- ceedings to try and make him resign. It was suggested that Foxall was practically made drunk at Baker's 'expense. Baker would say that they had two—perhays three—drinks together, and Foxall paid for one and he paid for the other. Foxall was absolutely sober, and he stated that he had never received sixpence from the defendant, and had never seen him in the town. He (Mr. Smith) wanted to iiixpress upon the bench the view that the sworn statements of a. man who, on his own showing, lied becausehe thought he might get a drink, were not the evidence upon which they were going to allow the defendant to go forth with the stigma even of a prism facie case against him. Mr. Smith proceeded to criticise the evidence of Mrs. Kirk- ham which, he said,was given with very little considera- tion, and in fact she did not at times understand what she was saying,He thought he would be able to satisfy the Bench that defendant never went near enough to Kirkham to give her a farthing. Kirkham afterwards said in the presence of a policeman andithe two candidates that she had never received a farthing from Mr. Gough. Then the witness stated that she was accompanied to Mr. Reardon's and Mr. Smethurst's by a police officer, but he was now informed that it was a Mr. Dalaney. It was, however, evident that the woman was under the impression he was a police officer, and he did not think the magistrates could have got a much stronger case of the exercise of pressure for political purposes 'by people representing police authority which they did not possess. Thecase of Mrs. Ward was, he contended, the feeblest and worst of the lot. It would be stated that the woman was not sober and that she did not refuse sixpence because no money was offered to her, and he submitted that her evidence was a wicked piece of perjury. In concluding his speech Mr. Smith said he would ask the Welch to remember that the duty of appraising the value of the case was a very responsible one and not merely mechanical. They must think what it 'would mean if the case went to the Assizes. He *could not conceive that such a case would not be absolutely laughed out of Court, but they must think of the expense, anxiety, and discredit of an Assize trial. Unless the magistrates were satisfied that the prosecution had Made out a prima facie case— having regard to the • character and credibility of the witnesses on both sides—he submitted it would be a wrong thing to convulse the town with all the excite- ment of political agitation and subject a man like Mr. Gough to the unnecessary stigma of standing in the dock to answer to a criminal charge at the Assizes.</p>
        <p>as I thought it was not consistent with t 4 course the Non-Traders were taking. At this point the Court adjourned for luncheon.</p>
        <p>AFTER THE ADJOURNMENT.</p>
        <p>The first witness called after the adjournment was, William Woodcock, letter-cutter, of 33 Price-street. He went round with Mr. Gough and Mr. Peers. he said, to Rogers's house. Rogers was in the doorway and they stood outside. They had a conversation together. No money would have passed between them without witness seeing it. The last thing Rogers said was You wont get my vote Mr. Gough," Referring to the inci- dent in Cairo-street, witness said Foxall was far from being drunk. He might have had a glass or two but he could understand what he was doing. Foxall said he hadn't received anything from Mr. Gough. Witness then referred to the conversation with Mrs, Ward. He did not see any money pass between Mr. Gough and Mrs. Ward and he must have seen it if any did pass Neither did Mrs.-Ward say she would not sell her vote. Cross-examined: They went together to see these two doubtful cases in Refuge Square—Rogers and Ward. Those were the only two houses visited on this Saturday night. 'He was out canvassing with Mr. Gough on the same afternoon. He did not see Mr. Gough shake hands with nay of the electors. He .had not seen Foxall before last Saturday night. He went to Mr. Wallhead's office to give some evidence. AN IRONWORKERS' EVIDENCE. '•</p>
        <p>William Peers, Ironworkers, 54, Pierpoint street, also gave evidence bearing out the last witness's statement Cross-examined, witnessadmitted that he was a little deaf but he thought he could hear all that took place. Witness shook hands with Rogers who held out the left hand to him, saying "That is good enough for you." A WITNESS APPEALS TO THE BENCH.</p>
        <p>William Baker, a packer for Messrs. Richmond and Co., Limited, was then called, and said he had asked Foxall for his vote .And agreed with Foxall's statement that he went round for him to take him to the poll. Witness took him to Mr. Gough's committee room. Mr. Gough was not there. Witness remained with Foxall until he got his card, and witness then took him to the polling station. Mr. Gough was standing about five yards from the polling booth and witness pointed out Mr. Gough to him. Witness saw Foxall after he had voted and he did not say anything about having received sixpence. Mr. Gough asked witness onSittur- day to find out which Foxall it was. He had no con- versation about the sixper..&amp;3. They spent the Saturday afternoon together. They had three or four drinks together. They had been together on previous occasions. It was an untruth to say that witness endeavoured to make him drunk. Foxall was not drunk, neither was he. It Was quite an accidental meeting together with Mr. Gough so far as he and voxall ware concerned. . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift cross-examined the witness as to whether he was remunerated by Mr .Gough for the work he did on the polling day. Witness replied that he was not paid. He did it for purity. Mr. Swift : How long have you been one of the purity party. Witness: I have been with them but 1 haven't worked for thom actively. Mr. Swift : It must be since Dace labor 1953 since you became a puritan. The Chairman: Not a Puritan.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: I beg pardon, one of the purity party. (To witness) in December 1898 did you get seven days for stealing an overcoat ? Witness: I object. lam here on this. That has nothing to do with you and I shall not answer it. Mr. Swift repeated the question. Witness: I object again. It has nothing to do with this case. I object to say. It is a thing of the past and lam on the future now. The past is dead and done with. Turning to the Bench,witnesssaid, "Gentle- men I object to this."</p>
        <p>The Chit:lra-Lan : You are bound to answer the ques- tion. (To Mr. Swift) : Do you press it. Mr. Swift: No, lam content if it is stated in the depositions that he refuses to answer. (Turning to witness). In January, 1901, did you get one month hard labour for stealing railway chairs? Witness • I suppose I did, but I didn't do it. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: DO.you mean that you didn't do the mouth? '(Letughter.) Were you convicted and got a month's hard labour?</p>
        <p>The Clerk (to witness) : Answer the question please. Mr. Swift repeated the question. Witness : I was, but it is a thing of the past. Mr. Swift : Is is true you went up to Mr. Gough outside the polling station and said " This Mlll is going to vote for us."</p>
        <p>Witness. I slily pointed out Mr. Gough to Foxall. Mr. Swift: When you were with Fox 11l on Saturday afternoon what were you talking about? Did you men- tion that sixpence? Witness: I askedihira if he received anything. Mr. Swift: About what time? Witness : On Saturday afternoon shortly after we had met, __ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: Had you asked him that previously. Witness : On the previous Thursday I asked him if he had received anything. Mr. Swift : You have told my friend that it was at dinner time on Saturday that Ar. Gough asked you to find out what Foxall it was. You knew all the time which Foxall it was. Witness: No. I did not know. Mr. Swift : Mr. Gough knew on Thursday that it was not the father of FOxall.</p>
        <p>Witness: I cannot say. Mr. Swift: What were you doing all Saturday after- noon; visiting public-houses? 'Witness: We hadn't money enough. I don't think. the pair of us were worth more than two shillings. Mr. Swift : Did yin promise Foxall a " good feed?" Witness : Oh no. • Mr. Swift : Why shouldn't you. Re was a friend of yours ? Witness: What could I stand?</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift; lie says you did make that promise. Witness ; I didn't say so. Mr. Swift : Did you ever have a drink with Foiall before? Witness: No.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : Then it is a mistake to si tint you were friends. Youware not "pals "—nothing more than the merest acquaintances?</p>
        <p>- - Witness : No. Mr. Swift : !Wore the ele.-:tion came or you didn't know him?</p>
        <p>Witnest : No. t know him by sight. Mr. Swift : That is rtther a different tale. We un- derstood he was a pleasant companion with whom you had been spending a social afternoon? Witness : He enjoyed it as much as I did. Mr. Swift : I expect he enjoyed it more because you were providing the" lush "were you not? (Laugh- ter). Witness : No.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : Did you tell Foxall to put his hat on straight or people would think he wai drunk? (Laugh- ter,)</p>
        <p>Witness : It was falling off at tha time, (1-4-tugh- ter.) • Re-exatnined by Mr. Smith: Foxall knew what he was saying and doing. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>ErneSt Allen, booranl shoe maker, also gave evidenca in support of Mr. GongllS case. He saw Foxall go into the conituittee room. Mr. Gough was not there at the time.</p>
        <p>Cross-exa.mined : lie had a slight relationship with Mr. Gough and lived in the same house. A "Cf3A.NCE'' FOR COUNSEL.</p>
        <p>• Mrs. Kenwright. 41, Catherine-street gave evidence as to voting on the date named. She was in company with Mrs. Kirkham and never saw Mr. Gough give that person any money. Witness had coins into a "little bit of death money" and had money of her own on the day of the election. She offered to pay f.r drinks but Mrs. Kirkham said she had the price and showed her two threepenny bits. Witness did not ask her where she got them from. Mr. Swift : If Mrs. Kirkham had had the two threepenny pieces before recording her vote, she would have invited you to a drink on the way down wouldn't she?</p>
        <p>--Witness: I don't know. This is the second vote I have given in my life aul I hops it will ba the last. (Laughter). Mr. Swift: You don't want to vote any more? Witness : No • Thope I shall hlve a husband to vote next time. '(Loud laughter.) Mr. Smith : That is a chance for my learned friend. (Laughter.) Mr. Swift : No, Tar friend Mr. Smith is a more eligible party. (Loud laughter.)</p>
        <p>. , In further cross•examination witness said she did not bear Mrs. Freer ssay, "Now don't tell lies; you know you got two threepenny pieces of Mr. Gough." She was in the hotel at the time. She did not know that some one offered to buy the threepenny bits. She was looking for her share of the money. Mr. Swift: What right had you to any share of the three-penny pieces. Were they for both of youl? Witness: - Do you think if I had sixpence and I had a friend I would not "wack" it. (Laughter.) Re-examined by Mr. Smith: If you meet my friend Mr. Swift and :toil have sixpence, you mean he is welcome to the half of it. (Laughter.) • Witness: Certainly. A CONSTABLE'S TESTIMONY.</p>
        <p>P.C. flayes said he remembered having some co nversa- tion outside the Imperial Hotel with a woman named Kirkham in the presence of several others, on the day of the election. HI-Isked her if she had received any money from Mr. Cough and she said "No." Cross-examined : Mrs. Freer who was present after- wards said "Now don't tell lies as we got two three- penny pieces of him." When she said this she nodded to Mr. Gough. Mr. Councillor Pemberton said to Mrs Kirkham: "Von had better be careful as to what you say or you are liable to imprisonment," The woman replied that she had not received anything and that if there was going to be any bother over it,they must not come to her anymore. Mrs. Kenwright (renllei) said she was with Mrs. Frear, and that woman told her to give her vote for Mr. Toole. COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON IN THE BOX.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor John Pemberton. builder, 7, Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, said he heard the conversation outside tUe Imperial Hotel. The woman seemed very indig- nant when asked if she had received any money. Cross-examined by Mr. Swift, witnesssaid he thought it his duty to tell the police officer to remind the woman that any evidence she gave would be used against her.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: Why are you called upon to tell the officer to do his duty? Witness: I thought it was necessary to do so. Mr. Swift: Do you go about Warrington instruct- ing police officers to do their duty? Witness: No. I was in the committee room when I heard Of the complaint made by Mr. Toole. I followed Mr. Gough down the street to see what was taking place, That is how I came to be there. Mr. Swift : You came up when it was nearly over? Witness: No, somebody had gone into the Imperial Hotel to bring out Mrs. Kirkham in order that she might be questioned. Mr. Swift Did'you say to her, "you are liable to imprisonment if you have taken money ?" Witness : . No. Mr. Swift: At any rate what you said had the effect of shutting her mouth. Witness: No, she most emphatically denied receiving any money. This was all the evidence. THE DECISION.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. F. Monks) said : Our decision is that the defendant be committed tort the uiverpool Ass i zes. Mr. Smith : What bail do you ask for? Mr. Swift: It must be substantial. It was decided to allow bail, and Mr. Gough was bound over to appear at the next Liverpool Assizes which commence in February, himself in a surety of S:200 andanother surety of £lOO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>QUESTION OF PROCEDURE.</head>
        <p>A QUESTION OF PROCEDURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift s Lid the question now was whether the prosecution had made out a prima facie case, and that must be decided before the defence was gone into. The magistrates were not there to try the case. They had simply to say after hearing the evidence for the prose- cution and defendant's answer to the charge, whether there was a case to commit upon. The case could not be dealt with there. That was a misfortune of the law. The Bench must commit or refuse to commit.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith submitted that the magistrates were entitled to hear the defendant's witnesses and decide on the balance as to whether there was a prima facie case. _</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that up to that point they were satisfied that a prima facie case had been made out. If however, Mr. Smith brought witnesses to rebut the evidence already given and the mnistrates were not satisfied the case would drop. Mr. Swift contended that if, after hearing the evidence for the prosecution, the Bench considered a prima facie case had been made out it ought to go for trial.</p>
        <p>It was, however, dnided to hear the evidence for the defence.</p>
        <p>THE DEFENDANT IN THE BOX.</p>
        <p>Councillor Samuel Gough said he was the -Non-Trad- ing candidate at a recent electionand was succeslful. It was untrue that he gave any money to the witnesses who had been called or to any. other.voter. Mr. Smith was proceeding to examine the witness with reference to a visit paid by Mr. Toole whoa Mr.' Swift objected as it was not evidence. The Clerk ruled that it was not evidence. Witness, continuing his evidence, said he remembered going round to see Mr. Rogers and he was accompanied by Mr. Peers and Mr. Woodcock. Mr. Rogers told them four or five times that he was going to vote for Mr. Toole and that he ( Ay. Gough) would not get his vote. They were at Mr. Rogers s house about ten minutes. Witness and his two frieuds were on the doorstep. There was no truth in the statement that he shook hands with Rogers or that he gave him sixpence or and coin whatever. With regard to Foxall's evidence, witness had had some conversation with Baker about two days before the poll with reference to this man. He did not see Foxall at all in the committee room. The first time he saw him that day was when Baker was accompanyinn. Foxall to the polling booth. Witness I was then standing near the polling station door. When ' Baker brought Foxall to witness, Mr. Toole,and the police- officer were there. That was the only t .me he saw Fexall on the polling day. There was no foundation whatever for the statement that he gave Foxall sixpence or anything else. He could not do so when the police were there if he had wanted. Witness was present in Cairo-street on Satur- day night when Foxall, Mr. Baker, Mr. George Henry Howe,and Mrs. Henry Howe were there.Foxall was sober and remembered what had taken place. No pressure was brought to beat on Foxall to make a statement. Witness asked Foxall where he gave him sixpence and he replied that he had not received anything. He told them further that he went to the Liberal Club and meant to make a statement there that he had no evidence, but no one was present. He also stated that he was not going to come to the Court to-day. It was untrue' to say that Foxall was asked to sign a paper or that any influence was used to make him give evidence. Pro- ceeding to the Kirkham case, witness said he had no conversation with the woman on the day in question. He saw her in the polling booth insthe presence of Mr. Toole and the officials. He never gave her any money on that date. Later he saw her in his committee room in company with Mrs. Kenwright. She went there to get her name struck off the list. Witness went out of the room and left them Thereto lookafteranothervoter. Witness afterwards spoke to Mrs. Kenwright outside for the purpose of ascertaining her name in order to strike it off the. list. Witness went back to the committee room to strike the name out and Mr. Toole came and accused him of givino, Mrs. Kenwright some- thing. He had not had a wOrcof conversation with Mrs. Kirkham. When Mr. Toole charged him, witness Mr. Toole, Constable Haves, Mr. Hare and Mr. Pember- ton all went up to the Imperial Hotel. Witness did not go inside the hotel,but someone went in and fetched Mrs. Kirkham and Mrs. Kenwright out of the hotel. The police officer asked Mrs. Kirkham Whether she had received anything from Mr. Gough. She indignantly denied having received anything from him. Turning to the Ward case, witness said he remembered visiting Mrs. ‘Vard in company with Mr. Woodcock and Mr. Peers. They went inside the house and witness asked Mrs. Ward if she would vote for him. She said "No ; I won't vote for you. I have promised it to Mr. Toole. I am going to vote the same way as my son. He is voting for Mr. Toole." It was untrue that he put six- pence into her hand, or that she threw the sixpence on the table. No money passed or was offered between himself and Mrs. Ward.. It would have been impossible for anything of that kind to take place without the other witnesses seeing it. Mr. Swift: Did you get a packet of threepenny bits on the Saturday before the election?</p>
        <p>- Witness : No sir. Mr. Swift : I suggest you received, a£3 packet of threepenny bits or sixpenny pieces? Witness: I emphatically deny it. Mr. Swift: There were a lot of these pieces about? Witness : There w3re a lot on the other side. Mr. Swift : The other side were bribing but you were not ?</p>
        <p>Witness: Certainly. The other side told me that they paid Et.,O to one public-house. Mr. Swift : Can you suggest why Rogers said you gave him sixpence? Witness: He may have been coached to do so. Mr. Swift : He must be lying thou ? ' Witness : There is no question. Mr. Swift : NVhy did you want to see Foxall on Saturday night? Witness: In order to get rebutting evidence In answer to further questions,witness said he did not know that Foxall had had a subpoena to give evidence. There were two Foxalls and they had to identify them. He was aware that a letter had been sent by Messrs. Browne and 'Crompton complaining that wit- nesses were being annoyed and badgered. He did not remember anyone saying, when Mrs. Kirkham denied having received any money, "Now don't deny it. We have got two threepenny bits." He denied that he spoke to Mrs. Kirkham in the polling booth. Asked to say what influence had been used by the other side, witness said he had heard that she hal been to tell the priest. Mr. Swift : She wauld be told to tell the truth. I suggest she is telling the truth and that you are telling a lie ? Witness : I say she is telling a lie and lam telling the truth. Mr. Swift: You are yoursella Liberal? Witness : Yes, I have been for many years. Mr. Swift: Are you a member of the Liberal Club- ? Witness: No. I left it when I became a Non-Trader</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>CHRISTMASTIDE</head>
        <p>CHRISTMASTIDE</p>
        <p>HOW TO-MORROW WILL BE SPENT IN WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>A Merry Christmas ! Once more we hear the familiar greeting, for the cycle of titne.has brought us to the eve of the season of rejoicing and good-fellow- ship. For the nonce, hard, grinding toil is laid aside, and in its place we welcome feasting .and merry- making. The days of seasonable Christmases appear to have gone beyond recall. It is, indeed, hard to realize that to-morrow dawns the festal day which all Christendom will unite in celebrating. The icy grip of 'King Frost is no longer felt. There is an almost spring-like mildness in the atmosphere,and altogether the weather is entirely out of harmony with tradition Perhaps, however, it is well that it should be so. To many people slackness of trade has meant straitened resources, and keen frost and snow would, in their case, spell suffering rather than enjoyment. But is there not much to show that Christmas is at hand? A. walk along the main streets is surely con- vine ng enough. The decorator has done his work •well, and some of the shop windows are ablaze with light and colour. Christmas presents, Christmas fare—a hundred and one :varieties of goods are so al- luringly displayed that the. temptation to buy is often. irresistible. Then We have had the waits—not, how- ever, of the good, old-fashioned type. The abuse of the -carolling:custom shows little sign of diminution as years go on: As the writer pens these lines the. sound of shrill young voices monotonously drawlin`g hymn tunes is heard in. the streets. There are, of course, a few exceptions to What has now become the rule and occasionally one does hear a genuine carol. Neither are the bands one whit behind in -filling the night air with music of a varied order. Warrington will keep the feast in much the usual way. In most of the churches and chapels services of an appropriately joyous nature will be held. In the day's rejoicings the deserving poor will not be forgotten. The spirit of charity and good-fellowship, such as was manifested by Scrooge after his trans- formation from a hardened miser t) a generous open- -hearted man, will find its outlet in various channels. At the Drill Hall there will be the usual much-appre- ciated distribution of hot-pot and mince-pies, and the dwellers in public institutions will not go unprovided for. • Even in the Workhouse there will be jollifica- tion,- and the inmates are eagerly looking forward to the red-letter day. Below we publish details of all the local arrangements :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>AT THE INI4IRAIARY.</head>
        <p>AT THE INI4IRAIARY.</p>
        <p>Every effort is being pat forth to .make Christmas Day as eajoyable as possible for the patients at this in- stitution. The warls will be tastefully decorated and those patients who are able to partake of it will be re- galed with Christmas fare. During the day carols will be snag and there will be games and other forms of amusement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>AT PADGATE SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>AT PADGATE SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The festivities at Padgate inclustrial Schools will be on much the usual lines. In the morning the children will attend service at their respective places of worship, and at noon an excAlent dinner of roast beef, veget- ables and plum pudding will be served in the school- room which has been beautifully decorated. Oranges, apples, and sweets will afterwards be distributed, and the afternoon will be spent in recreation in the play- grounds. At five o' clock tea will be provided and the remainder of the evening will be devoted to a concert by the scholars and friends.</p>
        <p>AT THE BLUE COAT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Prollably by no on is Christmas Day looked forward to with greater.pleisure than by the children at this old institution. To-day they will be busily employed in decorating the rooms and the task is one in which they take a pride and delight. Only one thing will be lack- ing on the festal day to fill their cup of happiness to the brim. Unfortunately there is so much sickness in .the neighbourhooi that it has been con3ldered advisable not to allow prents and friends to visit the schoOli The children, hcrvever, can be relied upon to enjoy themselves thoroughly. In the morning they will, aa usual tittend service at Holy Trinity Church. On re- turning th 9 Christuris dinner consisting of roast pork, plum pudding and other seasonable fare will be served and afterwards oranges and apples will be distributed. During the afternoon. and evening games and various amusements will be indulged in, and the youngsters will partake of a "special" tea. • HOT POT DISTRIBUTION. Between eleven and twelve o'clock on Christmas Day there will be a busy scene at the Drill Hall where the usual distribution of hot pot and mince pies provided for the deservingpoor at the expense of the Mayor will take place. Two hundred and forty tickets have been issued and it is anticipated that there will be about 1,200 recipients of the wholesome fare prepared by Mr. Powys. A number of ladies have been invited to assist in the distribution,and doubtless there will be a willing response. _ _</p>
        <p>AT ORFORD BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>Tommy Atkins always enters into the Christmas celsbrations with great zest, and festivity will reign sapreme at Orford Barracks to-morrow. The men of the South Lancashire and King's Liverpool Regiment have decorated their quarters lavishly and artistioally, and very bright and inviting the rooms appear. A sumptuous Christians dinner will, as usual, be served, and the celebrations will conclude with a dance in the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>AT THE POST OFFICE.</head>
        <p>AT THE POST OFFICE.</p>
        <p>Christmas is always a busy season ,for the postman and to-morrow morning we shall see him staggering ,uuderhis burden of seasonable missives and presents. At the Post]Office the traffic this week has been very heavy and to-night it will reach its height. The new early-posting arrangements have been put into operation for the first time this year. The new departure has not, however, been taken advantage of tp the extent that was anticipated. As the accornodation at the head °lee is inefficient to cope with the traffic, the authorities have rented the gymnasium, and here the work of sorting the parcels will be carried on. Supernumeraries for both inside and outside duties have been employed, and the author- ities have also hired extra vehicles for the delivery of parcels in the outlying districts. The letter box at the head office, which is usually open until 10.30, will be closed to-morrow night at 10. 15. The arrangements in regard to the late postage of letters at extra fees will be suspended.</p>
        <p>AT THE WORKHOUSE. PREPARING THE CHRISTMAS FARE.</p>
        <p>The joys of Christmastide will be participated in to the full by those whose HI-fortune it is to be domiciled in that great hall of poverty which we know by the name of the Workhouse. A reporter of the "Examiner" on visiting this palatial establishment found the offi- cials and some of the inmates busily engaged in making preparations for the celebration of the festive season. fhis is the first Christmas celebration for which Mr. and Mrs. Awbery, the popular Master and Matron, have been responsible, and judging by the advanced state of the preparations which the reporter found on Tuesday, there is no likelihood of the inmates being left unpro- vided for this Christmastide. Since entering upon their duties six months ago, Mr. and Mrs. Awbery have had a busv time. Extensive alterations to the Workhouse premises are still in progress, and these together with a full house and changes in the staff have kept them fully employed. Accompanied by the Master, the reporter made a round of inspection. The dininghAll was still in the hands of the decorators, but one was able to form a good idea of what the general effect would be, when the decorations were oomplete. The bareness of the walls was hidden by crimson material bearing seasonable mottoes and there was a profusion of holly, and evergreens. The window case- ments had also been treated with evergreens, relieved with red paper flowers. There has been an entire absence of snow this Christmastide but in order to give a seasonable aspect to the surroundings a snow-fall was represented by long lines of cotton-wool suspending from the ceiling.Pestoons of holly and coloured lanterns lent further enchantment to the scene. The hospital wards were being similarly treated. Some of the rooms with their van-coloured lights and festoons of evergreens pre- sented a scene of fitiryland, and the happy faces of the patients beamed with satisfaction and .delight at what was taking place. Proceeding to the kitchens, Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>11110111101."'"'- _</head>
        <p>11110111101."'"'- _</p>
        <p>mas preparations of an entirely different kind, but even more important, were in progress. Peering into the large brick oven, the reporter saw the currant bread in process of baking, and on accompanying the baker to the larder, one found a slab containing the Christmas puddings wrapped securely in cloths, ready for the pans. To give the public an idea of the quantity of stuff required to feed and entertain the 530 guests of the Poor Law Guardians on Christmas day the following particulars were obtained from the Master: roast beef, 470. lbs.; suet, 70 lbs.; currents, 130 lbs.: raisins, 120 lbs.: Valencias, 112 lbs.; candied peel 112 lbs ; beer. 54 gallons; ginger bear, 16 dozen bottles ; oranges, 3 cases :apples, 4 cases; ounces oftobacco, 330; half-ozs. of snuff, 100.</p>
        <p>The quantities are larger than usual owing to the greater number of inmates in. the House. This year there are 120 more in the House than last Christmas. Arising on Christmas morning the 50 or 60 children in the House will find that Santa Claus has been busy during the night in filling their stockings with toys and delicacies: There will be carols singing in the Wards and Christmas cards will be distributed. .Breakfast will consist of bread and butter and tea, after which, divine service will be conducted in the dining room by the Rev. W. Bra. cecamp (chaplain). The dinner will con- sist of theold English fare of roast beef and plum pudding. Beer will be supplied to those who desire it and mineral waters to those who prefer them. There will afterwards be a distribution of tobacco and snuff, oranges and apples. In the afternoon, the inmates will be at liberty to smoke the pipe of contentment until tea-time, when the menu will consist of tea bread and butter and cake. The evening will be devoted to social entertainments.</p>
        <p>Gifts of evergreens were made by Miss Broadbent: Mr. Lyon, Appleton Hall • Mr. Monks, Walton Old Hall; Mr. Parr, Grappenha:ll Heyes ; and Mr. Mather, Feamhead. Scrap books and toys were sent by Mrs. M. A. J. Worsley. of Winwick, and Mr. Jackson Lee. The decorations were under the direction of Mr. Turpin (clerk), Mr. Constable (porter), Mr. Fellows (labour master), Mr. Berkett (painter), and the nursing staff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Dark December.</head>
        <p>Dark December.</p>
        <p>Now that we have arrived at the period of the shortest day, there is some degree of consolation in the thought that the days will soon be lengthening, and we shall before long be experiencing lighter if not brighter weather. Of all months December is the most gloomy, and the accumulated results collected for past years show this month has the least amount of bright sunshine, the average duration being only about three-quarters of an hour a day, while there are more days absolutely without sunshine than in any other period of the year. For some time past the days have been dull and gloomy, and there is also a decided tend- ency to fog. In London on Monday fog was prevalent for a time,and in parts or the suburbs artificial light had to be used for some hours. There is no tendency at present for cold weather, and, although in some recent nights frost has occurred in the far North, yet the days have been fairly mild over the whole country. •</p>
        <p>The match gave Warrington a . cotnmanding posi- tion in the South West Lanca.shire Competition, and with anything like decent luck they should annex the trophy once more. The table up-to-date is as follows: . Pts.</p>
        <p>r, W. L. D. for agst Pts Warrington 4 ... 3 ... 0 ... 1 37-14 ... 7 *St. Helens 1 ... 1 ... 0 ... 0 5 2 ... 4 Runcorn Wigan Widnes Leigh 4 ... 1 ... 3 ... 0 22-10 ... 2 *The St. Helens matches, not being home and away engagements, each count 4 points.</p>
        <p>There was anther shuffling of positions in the Northern League table on Saturday, three of the lead- ing clubs, Hull Kingston lovers, oidham, and Leeds all: going down before Hull, Swinton, and Batl4- Salford are again at- the top by virtue of a win over Leigh at Leigh. The latter dub seems to be qualify- ing rapidly for the second division.</p>
        <p>The 14•? ague table up to date is as follows : For. Aga in P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pt- Salford 14...10... 3.. 1. 26 31-145... 5 11-43...21 Oldham 1G...10... 5... 1-.16 28-116... 5 10-40...21 Bradford Hull K't'n it 15...10... 4... 1...13 19— 83... 5 12-16...21 Leeds 15...10... 4.. 1. 8 22 82... S 15-61...21 Br'ghton R 15... 9... 4... 2...1031-116...15 14-72...20 Hunslet 15... 9... 5:.. 1...18 13-105...12 17-75...19 Warrington 15... 8... 5... 2...15 19— 87...10 16-68...18L Halifax 14... 6... 5... 3... 8 9 43... 9 9-45...15 Runcorn Hull - 81...21 20-108.11 Batley Widnes 16... 4... 9... 3...10 13— 59...19 34-140..11 Wigan Huddersfild 16... 5.-11._ 0... 9 24 90...17 31-127-10 Swinton Leigh Keighley -</p>
        <p>There is no first team fixture on the card for to-- morrow, Christmas Day, but the " A." team will entertain a team picked from the Works League, and the match should provide an inter-? sting strug,gle, especially seein(4. the fine form of the "A " team recently. On Saturday they won at Runcorn by 15 points to nil, Kenyon scoring two of the three tries. The match to-morrow will commence, at 10-45 in the. morning.</p>
        <p>On Saturday Salford will be visited by the War— rington men, and bad as their record is against the. Reds, I think they will give their hosts a tough struggle. The" A team match with Salford "A " has been re-arranged for Saturday morning in order to enable the men to be at liberty in the afternoon.</p>
        <p>Weaver Navigation.—Further Borrowing: Powers. The trustees of the Weaver Navigation hare 'de- posited a bill for introduction into Parliament next- session, under which power is sought to raise the sum of i:30,000 on the security of the Weaver tolls. The• moneyiraised under the power of this bill is required. according to the preamble, "for the maintenance and. improvement of the navigation and for the require- ments of the trade thereon." Power is also proposed to be given to the Cheshire County Council to lend, - the whole or any portion of this sum " on such terms and conditions as they think fit," or in lieu of lend-- ing, to guarantee the principal of, and the interest on. any moneys borrowed by the trustees under the - powers of this bill. In the event of the County Council deciding to lend the sum required, provision' s made in the bill to enable the Council to. plAge - he county fund. Buckley-street Band of Hope. An entertainment in connection with the al •v' ‘va: held on Monday evening when a first-class programme . was provided' by the members of the 1.0.G.T. Lodge, Ebenezer Chapel. Solos were well rendered by Messrs. A. P. Walm ley, C. J. Sheath, Albert Egerton- ' and Miss Ada Littlewood. Mr. F. Merlin give a short and instructive address, evidently making a good im- pression. Messrs. C. J. Sheath, A. P. Wahnsley and 'l'. Hughes sang in a trio entitled "A Social Glass." Miss -Lucy Sheath recited " The Drunkard's Death,' and Mr. Maidment, of Orford Barracks,highly amused:, everybody present with several ventriloouial sketches and was loudly encored as also were -2.fiss, A. Littlewood and Mr. Albert Egerton. Miss P. Plinston, A.L.C.M. and Mr. A. E. Trow were the accompanists. A. dialogue, entitled " The Outcast" in which Messrs. F. Merlin, T. Hughes, P. Bate and ; Miss E. Littlewood. .splendidly took Dart, brought a • most</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>NOTES ON CAIItIES AND ATHLETICS,</head>
        <p>NOTES ON CAIItIES AND ATHLETICS,</p>
        <p>Rugby Football. By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>The Warrington and Rancor!' engagement pro- duced a surprise on Saturday—everal surprises in fact—and: eventually the Linnets retired suffering from the severest beating they ever received at the hands of Warrington. Eighteen points to five is pretty conclusive evidence of superiority, but the marvel is that the margin . was not much greater. And yet at one time Runcorn certainly looked like sharing the spoil if not winning outright. At half- time even, they were on level terms, but then came the deluge. .</p>
        <p>There— was no fog about on Saturday, and this ittelf must have been a treat to Warrington after rambling round in the mist for 160 minutes during their two previous eng,agemeuts. The weather was distinctly favourable, and the crowd rolled up. solidly till there must have been some 7,000 present, includ- inc, a fair number from the Cheshire port. Owing to the rain the ground was on the soft side, but was still in good order, and a fast game resulted.</p>
        <p>The Linnets opened in a nnst chirpy fashion, and Warrington found themselves busily engaged straight away inrepalling the clever invasions of Butterworth, Price and Co. An advantage in the scrum for War- rington was at Once apparent, but the Cheshire for- wards made up for their want of avoirdupois by nippiness and quickly supplemented the tactical moves of their backs. A cmsiderable time passed without much accruing, though Runcorn had been the most dangerous team. Warrington seemed disjointed,and the backs careless, with the result that the inevitable lesson followed. Dickeuso4 attempted to intercept a pass from Butterworth to Price instead of going for his man, with the result that flockenhall was left with three men to deal with and after a fine rut; -Moran. scored at the corner. Farmer placed a goal. It was a smart effort on the part of the Runcorn backs, who obtained po§session in their own half, but they would never have got clear away had Dickenson played the game instead of indulging in a risky experiment.</p>
        <p>Matters now began to look serious for the home side, and in response to the cheers of their supporters they swept the opposition away with a . dash which carried play to:the line. Not more that four minutes had elapsed when Bate robbed Jolley and scored :tear the posts, Fish equalising amid frantic expressions of delight front the crowd. Play ruled even to the inter- val witla 614 side w ts in pissession of five points.</p>
        <p>During breathing time the Warrington men were lectured severely, and the result was apparent on re- sumption. For a while the Linnets, by the aid of tacticaltouch work, kept Warrington at bay, but the home forwards made their weight felt in such fashion that their opponents were driven to their own quarter. A particularly loose pass resulted in Dickenson field- ing the ball from the ground, and racing through the Runcorn backs in grand style he scored behind the posts, Fish again adding a goal. This practically put the issue beyond doubt for the Linnets had hardly a chirp left. Warrington beat thern at all points and Bate very soon after dribbled past the Runcorn custodian, for Isherwood to score, Fish kicking his third goat. Visions of a r.eoril score began to float through the disordered minds of the excited home partizans, wit ) screamed joyfully when the forwards carried the ball right over the line a moment later. The try was not allowed, however and then Hocken- hall ran cleverly to the goal-month, failing to score by inches only. Runcorn had barely cleared before Isherwood came up at top speed and profiting by Moran's mull he dashed over with the fourth try. Fish just failed to make the t )tal into a round twenty points and the game ended.</p>
        <p>'Referee Priestley distinguished himself on Saturday by very officiously interfering with the half backs, and by addressing the Warrington forwards in the following remarkable terms when they rushed the _l/uricorn defence late on in the game: "You've got a good lead. Don't rub it in ! " Good for a referee, isn't it, especially as he disallowed what seemed a fair try. •</p>
        <p>Runcorn are very rarely so thoroughly beaten as they were on Saturday, and it is indeed rather sur- prising that they should so completely go to pieces after making such a bold show in the first half. In the last 30 minutes they were totally demoralised, the forwards simply being swept off their feet at times. The score does not at all over-represent Warrington's superiority on the day's play. It is a marvel 'titat it was not much larger. The combination of the home backs was really -below par, or the wing men Would have had a hand in the try-getting. It was dash alone which got the points. In fact I consider the Runcorn centres play d a.much better game than Isherwood and Dickenson though the Warrington pair were responsible for three tries. Butterworth and Price never failed tp field well, pass cleverly, and display resource at critical moments. They could not be blamad for the ovcrwheltninz of their Sicde in the least.</p>
        <p>The Warringtznt forwards had a. big say in the victory; and Sc, had R. Bate. He prayed a game worthy of "Smack" at his best and was easily the best half on thelield. I must compliment bun on the head work he displayed in putting Warrington on the attack by means of touch play. He saved both backs and forwards a big lot of work by thus taking a leaf out of Runcorn's book. The tactics are worthy. 9f repetition—though not too inuch—and might perhaps be employed with, advaroage against Salford on Saturday. An advantage :in position thus easily gained gives the men a chance of throwing all their energies into an attack from short range.</p>
        <p>Hallam was much the better of the two full backl. Hough did by no means badly but he is not a Hough- ton. In addition to the men I have already mentioned Jolley deserves praise br a sound display under adverse circumstances. 11ichardson was also good, but like Davies, be wa.; nit prominent. Walker 'once more proved his worth as a scrummager and smart man in the loose. Oir he whole I was a bit sorry for the Linnets, thoughlWarrington owe them a good drubbing.. That 18-0 at Irwell-lane last season re- quired wiping out.</p>
