        <p>POUNTY BoROUG II OF WAR EINGTON.— N._.) THE EMPLOYMENT OF CHILDREN ACT, 1903. NOTICE IS HIPIEBX GIVEN, that on the 15t day of JANUARY, 1901, the following general Restrictions on Employment of Children will come in foree, t•--- SvcTion 3.-0.1 A child (i.e., a person under the age of 141 shall not be employed between the hours of Nine in the evening and Six in the morning: Provided that any local authority mar, by byelaw, vary these hours either generally or for any specified ocCupatilD, (2.1 A child under the age Of ELEVEN years shall not be employed in STREET TR ONG, (3.) No cLild who is employed half-time under the Fa,tory and Workshop Act, 1901, shall be employed hi any otner "ccupotion. (4.) A child shall not be employed to lift, carrvor move anything so heavy as to be likely to cause injury to the (5.1 A child shall not be employed in any oceupation likely to be injurious to his life, limb, health at- educa- tion, regard being hall to his physical condition. Until the Town Council as the Local Authority make bye laws varying the hours of employment under section 3 (1) the above general restriction will he in force. No Boy or Girl under the age of 14 years may be employed oontrary to such restriction, for instance in selling or delivering milk or newspapers or other asticles, carrying paroeia, trading in the streets, or be employed in any shop or other `premises or in iny industrial work at home The pe'naltv for the first offence is a fine not exceeding 40s. and in the case of a second or subseqnent offence not exceeding 25. The person who commits the offence whether he be the employer, agent or workman or the parent or guardian will be lialle to the penalty. Dated this 11th day of Decemher; 1913. J. LYON WHITTLE. Town Clerk, Town Hall, Warrington.</p>
