        <p>Again I pointed out that the plot had been given to my namesake ; and that, for convenience, it would be very easy for me to suppose it was meant for me. Still she was doubtful if I would escape scot free of the consequences. She had a full measure of the timidity , of her sex; bat, come what might, I was determined to disclose the facts to the editor, and have the pleasure of witnessing his confusion. At eleven o'clock next morning I was climbing the stairs which led to his office. As I went up, I wondered what would happen before I came down. I imagined all sorts of things, chief among them being the sight of ' an infuriated editor. I was shown into his office. There he sat writing away as if all the world were awaiting his thoughts and sentiments. When he had flaishsci the sheet he 'wheeled his chair round and looked across at me. I gave him my name again,-at which he started as if he had been struck. He looked intently at me,and then, as if an inspiration had seized him, he rang the bell which was near. A junior clerk answered the summons, to whom he gave some whispered instructions. I smiled, and it annoyed him. You have not the slightest need to take any pre- cautions against my escape," I said. "If you will listen to what I have to say I think you will see that the mystery attached to the publication of "The Triumph of Patience and Virtue' is about to be cleared up to your advantage." "It cannot be. Already my paper has suffered tre- mendously. Its circulation is rapidly falling,—but what is it you have to tell me ?" I unfolded to him ' all that had happened up to the publishing of the story; how, having received the letter, commencing, My dear Stanhope,' I had sup- posed it was meant for *me ; although, I added, :‘‘ I can see now that it was meant for the gifted writer of that name." Further, how, having signed;niy name to it correctly, it was no infringement on the name of the author of "Mysterious Moonlight." He was dumbfounded. I could see he was chagrined because of the simple mistake as he supposed. He investigated on the spot, and found that having left the letter to be addressed and posted, with only the instruction"for Paul Sta,nhope,Esq., 'ithad been given to 9, clerk unfamiliar with the work. Not knowing the address of the writer he had turned up the directory and taken the first Paul Stanhope he had come across: Thus the mistake happened. He sat with his elbows firmly planted on the arms of his chair, and his eyes fixed on the floor. Occasionally he muttered something to himself, as if he had forgotten that I was present. Suddenly he turned and looked straight in my eyes. "How strange I did not notice any difference in the style. Have you ever written anything else ? " This was my chance, and I determined to utilize it. " Yes ; I have written other stories which are up to the present unpublished." I did not tell him that every one of them had been , sent to his paper, and returned to me. "Send them on to me and I will consider them." I could hardly conceal my joy. This affair was developing beautifully. He was actually going to con- sider stories which he had refused. At this moment a thought struck him and he began to talk again. "I have'just conceived the 'idea," he said, "of turning this unfortunate affair into a huge advertisement for my paper. To-morrow, if you have no objection, I will publish in all the papers an account of the dis- covery of the author of 'The Triumph of Patience and Virtue.' T will also publicly apologise for the mistake in supposing it was written by the well-known author of the same name. I will also state that several more stories will follow, of an equally fascinating nature, by the newly-discovered writer. If I mistake not, this will cause a big rush for the paper by people anxious to read more of your stories ' • for, after all, the story was a good one or I should never have passed it, and I feel safe in offering to take all you have." He did it, and his conjecture was correct. The public went crazy over the affair, and when my next story appeared, entitled "What's bred in the Eyelashes," I had suddenly risen from an obscure position to that of a well-known writer. The "Penman's Friend" re- covered its lost ground and added to it. * * * * But the great man had scarcely uttered the last word when through the half-opened door of the refreshment room, he saw the guard of his train giving the signal to start, and without anadieu or evena parting glance he was off,and Twas left alone to think over the tale he had just narrated to me. I would have liked to ask him some auestions,but, of course, that was out of the reckoning when I saw hovo- he was leaving me. But what did anything matter? I had got a choice tit-bit and,inturn,l hand it on to you my readers, that you may know the true story of the sudden rise to fame of Paul Stanhope, the author of "The Triumph of Patience and Virtue.</p>
